      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>GENERAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>GENERAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Whine riding a bicycle at Cheltenham ore Saturday a boy named Causier °Minted with a carriage and sustained fatal Injuries. John Evan, a labourer, aged thirty, was executed at New- gate on Tuesday morning for the murder of IVice-constable Baldwin at Hoxton. The Gland Trunk express from Montreal for Toronto ran ' into a goods train near Murray Hill early on Tuesday. Eight passengers were killed and many injured. A Royal Artillery pensioner named Towner. reaiding at the village of liorey, Jersey. murdered his wife and youngest child on Tuesday, and afterwards committed suicide On Monday bight a eeriness fire occurred in London,causing damage to the extent of £20.003 to the premises of Messrs. Wofeker, Diorite and Co.. 8. Bridewell-place, City. At Croydon en Saturday. a boy of twelve named John Gregory was orderee ta receive ten strokes with a birch for placing two iron chairs on the railway at Clipstead. A sample case containing jewels of the value of £3,000 has been stolen from a railway carriage while in transit between Leeds and Bra Iford. A reward of £2OO has been offered. young wiernan named Elizabeth Lawrence war shot dead on Saturday at Riverhead, near Sevenoides, by George Henry Beakes, a local jeweller, who afterwsrds attempted to com- mit suicide. William Morgan was charged at Merthyr, on remand on Monday, with the wilful murder of I zabeth ancoek. and remanded for another week, at the r :quest ot a solicitor °presenting the Home Offiee. Tice fishing village of Dalatel in Spain has been visited by a destructive cyclone. Fifteen persons were injured, and serious damage was done to houses and shipping. Two Sailere injured ace not, expected to recover. The Balton magistrates on Saturday committed to the Liverpool Assizes two Manchester girls wimed Elizabeth Tunstall and Edna Taylor. on charges of obtaining £3 by means e,f forgery and stealing a postal order for El. During a d-nsa tog on Monday evening a pair horse van fell into the Surrey Commercial Dack. London. the driver area both horses being drowned. The driver leaves a widow and six chi`dren. It is reported that a single-horse van and carman are a' so missing. Able seamen Arthur Beale and James Kelly, of destroyer Salmon, were found guilty by a court-martial. at Sheerness on Monday, of striking a Sect class patty officer and a stoker. Beale was sentenced to twelve months and Kelly to nine months' imprisonment. ,very serious subsidence occurred at Northwich on Tues- day afternoon, London-road—ene of the Cheshire County Council main roads—bsing rendered impassable for a distance of forty yards. Traffic was brought to a standstill for some time, but before nightfall a new temporary road had been opened. Joseph Henry Maddock', aged thirty-three, was remanded on bail at the Liverpool Pollee Court on Saturday. Charged with the manslaughter of Robert James Boeffienin Its-thboate- road, under circemetanoes already reported. In the course of the day the inquest on the body of Bouffier Was opened and adjonrned. A distressing accident is reported from Ingleton, the victim being Police Superintendent Blanchard, of Settle, who while out shooting with Police-Superintendent Haynes, of Inglet on. was fatally shot by the latter. An inquest was held on Saturday. when a verdict of "Accidental death was returned. A London coroner's jury who on Monday Investigated the death of Conrad Berndht la a bakehouse returned a verdict of wilful murder against Joh: no Schneider. A medical witness said Berndht was provably alive when Schneider threw him into the oven, but the cause of death was a blow with a hatchet. iCapt tin Count Stonseeg Wernigercnie, who during the re- cent manoeuvres wounded a sergeant with a blow from his sabre. from the effects of which the sergeant died, has been dismissed from the array and ordered to undergo three and four months' imprisonment. The German Emperor has con- firmed the sentence of [the court-martial. .The LUCalliS left the Landing-stage for New York on Satur- dry, having on iboard Dr. Nancy Guildford, who has been ' extradited on charges of a capital character. Tod Sloan, the famous Amertran jockey, was also on board. An American passenger who had been in Faris made a somewhat extraor- dinary statement with regard to the Dreyfus affair. 1 On Tuesday morning, the body of a young woman, *bole twenty-five years of age, was found on the Wirral Railway between New Brighton and Wallasey Stations. The body, which was almost decapitated. was subsequently identified as that of Miss Frances Sophia Dougherty, who had resided in Lombard-street, West Derby-road, Liverpool. A singular ice accident occurred at Veliki listing. in the government of Vologda. Russia. on Saturday. While a number of vehicles were being driven along the fr ,zen sur- face of the Suctions river the water suddenly began to rise. The ice commenced to drift, and several carriages were pre- cipitated into the river. Twenty lives are said to have been lost. At an Association football match at D vrsbary, on Saturday between Dewsbury and Sheffield, a man named Ellam died from injuries received. Whale he was following up the ball one of the Dewsbury men kicked it. The first player was struck In the stemach, and fell. The usual remedies were applied, but he died in about five minutes. Play was at once stopped, A shocking case of snicide was discovered late on Saturday evening in the vicinity of Laxey. Isle of Man. Some men were wandering on the lofty cape which overlooks the har- bour, and in a sheltered spot they found the body of a young fisherman. named Thomas Quills. An examination revealed that his throat was terribly Cut, and that it was clearly a Bury.esday evening in the The building case of suicide. Fire broke out about eix o'clock onillTu top storey of the Soho Spinning Mill. was five floors high, seventeen windows long, and contained 35,000 spindles. The fire was difficult to get at for some time. It gradually spread till the roof, little by little, fell in. Then the second storey caught. Several, thousand pounds dam- age was done. The steamer Voltaic arriven in the Mersey on Saturday mornbsg. having on board the crew of the steamer Cognac, which she had sunk between Holyhead and Skerries on the previous day. On Saturday morning the three-masted schooner Edith Crossfield was in collision Off Crosby with the Beaver liner Tangariro. The schooner was seabsequently towed into the Canning Do-k. At the Manchester Assizes on Tuesday, before Mr. Justice Phillimore. William Taylor an ex-ba:liff, aged 56 years, was</p>
        <p>nrlicted for the attempted murder of his Honour Judge Parry at the Manchester County Court on July '46 last. The jury found the prisoner guilty, and his Lordship, characterising the crime as terrible and outrageous, sen- tonced him to tweaty years' penal servitude. A shocking fatality happened on the Blackpool and Fleet- wood tramroad late on Saturday, near Bispham, about three miles from Blackpool. The driver of a well-laden car saw a dark object ors the lines and slowed up, but net before the car I jumped over something. The car was stopped, and examine- Hon (made showed that James Lingard. aged sixty-live. , labourer, of Bispham. was nearly decapitated. M. Bourey, a Paris examining magistrate, when near the entrance to the Palate de Justice on Saturday, was attacked , by a well dressed irady named Hinque, who fired two revolver pilots at him One bullet pierced the magistrate's left lowerjawbone, but the injury inflicted is not so serious as to endanger life. The lady, who was arrested, said she wanted to take revenge on the magistrate for having, as she asserted, refused her father justice. The Rev. James Davidson, vicar of Nafferton. Bast YoYk- s'llre, who, at the age of eighty-two. was recently married to Nfrs Douglas Denton. a clergyman's widow, aged n..arly o'ghty. on returning from the honeymoon was on Friday presented with an illuminated address, a lamp, a GlaAstone bag, and a carriage rug. Ms. Denton received a gold shawl pin. The village was ex fete, and the aged couple entertained 5)0 villagers at tea. The Herta coroner has been advised of the death of Mary Datt,n, a New Barnet fort, who a few days ago masquer- n led as Winter in a local cycling carnival. Miss Battson was dressed In cotton wool, and in brushing from her sister's dress a spark scattered by a torch her dress ignited, and in a moment she was ablaze. The crowd shrank back horrified, and before aSt John's Ambulance man could aid her she was terribly burnt, and died after lingering several days. A great fire occurred at Dawson City on the 14 ult. Four business blocks were destroyed, comprising forty buildings, and including the Post Office. The loss of property Is esti- mated at 500,000d015., and none of It is covered by insurance. The!fire began through a woman throwing a lighted lamp at another woman with whom she had been quarre ling. There is no lumber or glass available to rebuild houses destroyed and the sufferers for the present will have to live In tents. The death of Thomas Walter Turner. after a glove fight at he National Sporting Club, London, was the subject of an inquest en Monday. The house surgeon at Charing Cross Hospital gave its. hie opinion that the man was not fit in a physical sense to train and take part in a boxing contest. The j cry found a verdict of accidental death, and added rider to the erfeet that men should only be allowed to take I part in glove fights when they had been medically certified to be in a fit condition. At the Leeds City Police Court on Monday a women named Sarah Ann Craven was committed for trial at the Leeds Assizes charged with having broken into an office and stolen two cheques, and also with having forged a cheque for £5,000 and uttered it. The woman, whose c.reer latterly ap- pears to have begm rather remarkable, presented a cheque for £5.000 at a Leeds bank, but disappeared while the clerk was making inquiries. When arrssted she was found to have a leaded revolver in her possession. An exciting scene was witnessed on Sunday afternoon, on the Albert Embankment, London. While several children were playing on the steps that lead down to the river, one of their number, named Frederick Whittarn, aged seven years, and living in Stangste-street. Lambeth, suddenly sit raped and fell in. One of his mates, named Montague Crisp, a lad also of seven years. seeing his danger, without a moment's hesitation plunged into the river and swam to his companion's rescue, succeeding after a severe struggle In bringing him safely to the shore amid the cheers of the crowd who had gathered round.</p>
        <p>The Royal Laundresses ay Critcthey "STARCH GLOSS Is nnequalled for Imparting a beautiful finish to starched linen. &amp;c., either cold or boiling water starch. Mt.—. Corn- wa • s aye:—My wile will not finish our linen without your "Starch Gloss.'" From Mrs.—, Bradford :—Dear sir,— I am, pleased to Inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch, Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be In.ed in every house. Sold at Id., 3d., and 6d., by most starch sellers. if not in stock will procure it. T CRITCHLZY Blackburn. Proprietor.--"ldet.</p>
        <p>DRATJGUTS.</p>
        <p>el EXAISLINERI NOVEMBER 19th, 1898).</p>
