      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>CHAPTER XVII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XVII.</p>
        <p>A PLAIN TALK WITII LUCIA.</p>
        <p>After conferring with Kana, Mark approached Lucia. "The Queen has commanded me to join the Royal party at supper. I hesitated, thinking her hospitality would be extended to you; indeed, I hinted as much, but in vain. Sandy, will you oblige me by seeing the Senorita home?" I bowed, but glancing at Lucia I saw a flush of mingled anger and disappointment. However, it was only momentary. Taking my arm, she tripped along quite gaily, nodding to the many salutations on our way. Presently, on reaching a quieter part of the city, Lucia observed, half talking to herself : "Ah me! Mark goes to seek pears on the elm." I reiterated her words in an interrogative tone. She nodded significantly, but my guess ma to her meaning was left in the lurch. "Where are Jip and Pecos ? " "Gone with the Royal party, Senorita." "Tell me," said she, haughtily, "has Kane, the Royal schemer, asked whether Mark and I are married?" I shrugged my shoulders and shook my head. "I conclude from my polite banish- ment from the Royal party that the Queen simply considers me the minstrel of our troop, and hence the design of this match-making dame to dangle Olive's charms before Mark. But all is not gold that glitters." - "</p>
        <p>The curl of her lip and bitterness of her voice chilled my blood. I felt my position keenly, for a man who betrays the confidence of his master deserves two dozen with the rope's end. Evading her question, I asked: "Who taught Kana her smattering of Spanish ? " Lucia threw back her pretty head and laughed derisively. Then she suddenly stopped, and with dogged earnestness said: "Who fouled her nest? " I shrugged my shoulders and shook my head. Lucia was gay one moment and tragic the next, and before I could solve this riddle she continued : "Concubinage, even among the Royal courtiers, is unknown in Golden Vale. What a virtuous race! When a cuckoo takes possession of a nest you may expect the rightful owners to clear out. Time will reveal much that must astonish you. If Olive takes his fancy, Mark Pepys will rue the day he set foot here!"</p>
        <p>An awkward pause ensued, and I bit my finger- nails in moody contemplation. "Senorita," I ventured, still in Spanish, "your words are evil." _</p>
        <p>She shook her bead, and, stopping short, tapped her closed fan upon my breast. "No, no, Senor Dees, my words are rather of warning, for Mark has more to fear from Indian intrigue than from Lucia Diaz." I felt that a crisis was at hand, so, mustering all my persuasive powers, I begged of her to make me a closer confidant. Her significant answer sur- prised me. "Did you see the languishing looks of Olive when the victor received his prize? Can you not divine the object of Kana in excluding me from the gathering of dusky lords and ladies round the palace festive board? She would ignore me, and have Mark wed the half-caste. Faugh Can a lion mate with the wolverine? "_. .</p>
        <p>"Seiu;ita," I replied, with all the solemnity at my command, "you are weaving the cords which will bind you to misery. A jealous woman is always restless. You will surely burn yourself out in two years; your beauty will fade, your disposi- tion grow crabbed, and your pitiable position become the scoff of the countryside." "A doleful picture," she retorted, "but I shall never live to grow old, for I would rather crawl into some cavern and die—not a lingering death, though, for I have learnt from Pecos the secret of making the milk of herbs." She laughed, but there was a metallic ring about it; and yet again a wilder laugh escaped her pretty lips, which made me shudder—it was maniacal to my prejudiced ears. Women were ever an enigma to me, their strange fancies and humours, their inconstancy—save in the case of a mother's love, or a good wife's devotion—their caprice and jealousies ; but Lucia's idiosyncrasies utterly bewildered me. Nevertheless, it was ___ _ _</p>
        <p>ly duty o defend and protect Mark, so I observed, very kindly: "Senorita, you need have no fear that Olive will usurp Mark's affection, for no one but an ardent lover would have consented to you accompanying the expedition from Yerba Buena." "Ah " she replied, smartly drawing herself up at full length, "but I forced his hand." I drew a long face, and she tittered, saying lightly: "Come, come, you too have noticed the rapt attention which Mark pays to Olive." I looked angry and shook my head. "No? Then you are a tinkering fool! Ha, ha, ha!" "A jealous woman always fancies a gooseberry- bush is a forest of pines." She turned upon me quickly and exclaimed: "I have eyes( " Indeed, just then they were very much in evidence.</p>
        <p>"Yes, lyi.irt;;;;;;ee with a prejudiced sight. Many a good ship ha been lost on the rocks because the pilot took green for red. You will run on the quicksands. if your jealousy makes you colour- blind."</p>
        <p>"Jealousy is like the rattlesnake," she retorted; "it is fascinated by love's music, but if you prod the reptile it will sting." I placed my hand soothingly upon hers, and observed: "After jealousy comes hate." She instantly threw my hand away, interposing bitterly: "The wasp alights gently enough; it's what it leaves behind. Your words cut my heart!" "This mood means sorrow."</p>
        <p>lig reproving tone of voice and manner touched her, for she exclaimed, wringing her hands: "I was a fool to speak, and yet my heart was bursting! When he rejects me you will despise me. All who envy success jeer at the least failure. One has to act the hypocrite to win sympathy. The beggar gets alms before the respectable poor. Selfishness usually debases confidence. You know my fears, and I trust to your honour as a gentleman not to betray me." Tears suffused her beautiful eyes' and there was an indefinable something about her disposition which made me pity her. "There is an old saying, Lucia. common enough among your people, that adversity makes the human race wise." „</p>
        <p>"Ah," she sobbed, "Sufre por saber." "Yes, and your wisdom must be born of suffering. It is so ordained that the mind of man shall be purified by man's own bitter experience. Why—is a mystery, like many problems of life. Some member of every family has a cross to bear the brave carry it with cheerful resignation." We had reached our abode, but as Lucia seemed profoundly miserable and loth to go indoors, we left the city and strolled on along the hilly path, until we reached the mighty cascade where that sprightly bird of freedom, the ouzel, was skimming the dashing, gurgling rush of waters, diving into the spray, and emerging again to enjoy the purity and elegance of its home. Yonder, down the valley, was the mightiest fall in Golden Vale, called, on account of the beauty of its colours, the Spraybow Cascade. In the rainy season the noise of its was was like the roar of thunder, but as Lucia and I listened it was soft and subdued. The gentle winds through the firs resembled the music of many harps—so unlike the ominous whistle of the storm-fiends, when the loosened rocks would bound down the precipitous cliffs and shatter at the base with a noise like the rattle of musketry. "This," observed Lucia, after some commonplace talk, "is my ideal of Arcadian bliss. Here Mark and I have spent some happy hours—hours which instinct tells me will never, never again fall to my lot."</p>
        <p>"Nonsense, Senorita," said I, consolingly. She sighed. "Content makes the beauties of nature perfect." I replied: "If I am very hungry, a bit of bread and cheese and a glass of ale go down better than a banquet. And my enjoyment is lasting, for I feel more comfortable than an Emperor after his f easting. " "Ah, I have the choicest that money can buy, even to a surfeit of good things." "But when another seems to grasp at them you snarl like a dog over a coveted bit of meat." This was plain speaking with a vengeance, but for Mark's peace and her reproof it was necessary. In creation endless variety abounds, and no two minds are similar. To some persons a gentle wish is an order, while others require a command. When a woman like Lucia was vain enough to expect men ef my temperament to bow down and worship her</p>
        <p>!harms, then woe betide chivalrous lovers of Mark's mould. By flattering the Senorita you could draw forth the sweetest disposition, but she fenced reason as an antagonist with a rapier, skilfully piercing her opponent at his weakest spot. Strangely enough, Lucia vouchsafed no reply to my observations, but with her eyes towards the ground strolled along in moody silence. Presently she tossed her head and stood before me like a tigress suddenly and unexpectedly brought to bay. "Then you have been the evil genius dogging my every step!" she scornfully cried. "You took a dislike to me at Yerba Buena; you placed all sorts of obstacles in the way of me joining the party for San Leno ; you objected to me coming herel Why ? " "You are frank, Senorita," I firmly replied, staring her full in the face, "and I will be candid with you. At the old home I was suspicious of your faithfulness to my employer, because I knew of your friendly relations with Muro, whose reputation was none of the best. Again, I was not sure of the part you played in the strange affair at Puke's Delight, when that Indian labourer was murdered as he defended Pecos against his villainous captors. Furthermore, your recognition of the hostile leaders at San Leno warned me of treachery, but your subsequent bravery wiped away all doubt. If I protested against you joining us in this expedition,l did so on the ground of its dangerous nature. I am a matter-of-fact man, and if you can give a satisfactory account of the spell which the name of Muro seems to have over you, then alone shall I be convinced that you gave no sign ot betrayal when we lay in ambush on the morning that Golden Vale was invaded I" The Senorita's face blanched; she trembled from head to foot; a clammy perspiration was upon her brow; and I had just time to catch her as she swooned. My words had cut her to the quick, and I was heartily ashamed of my brutality. Lucia's fainting fit placed me in an awkward predicament; and for certain reasons, which will be obvious as we proceed, I regretted taking the initiative in speaking my mind so plainly. This swooning was neither a subterfuge nor the result of hysteria. "My head seems numbed," were her first audible words. "I feel as if I had passed through a long illness. Let us go home." I excused myself as best a conscience-stricken man could; but her manner was subdued, and not vindictive. "Let us say no 'more about my past," she observed sorrowfully, "or the motives which prompted you to suspect me. Suffice it that no hair of Mark's head shall be injured for my sake. All is fair in love and war ; and if Olive secures the affection that was once mine, there may be little cause for complaint--but I refuse to share it with her. Only the selfish object to make some sacrifice. Mark thinks Olive such an innocent girl —but there, I would rather have to do with a rogue than a fool, though, like all half-castes, she is more cunning than roguish. That you should ada to the anguish of my soul by doubting my fidelity to Mark -Pepys is a wound that needs a lot of li ding." "Your pardon, Seficrita." "It is freely given, as in the goodness of the Holy Virgin's grace I ask forgiveness of all my sins. This has been a very perplexing day, and I am weary, as though a yoke were upon my heart. At some time or other we are all creatures of circumstance. It is at such a time as this that one feels the want of a good mother. I would give the world to lay my aching head upon my mother's breast, and cry myself to sleep. Tears are a relief, but now I cannot weep; I have not even the con- solation of a sister's sympathy, nor the strong arm of a father to protect me. All the physical comforts and enjoyments of life are lost when the mind is uneasy. In the palace yonder Mark is happy, while lam miserable. Olive has a good home, great expectations of wealth and position, and blood relations around her; while I am but a minstrel, cast upon the wayside, forsaken and alone. Until now I could never realise the feel- ings of the five virgins who, not having their lamps trimmed, were left out in the cold. But there," she added, a little more cheerfully, "regrets will not change bad weather. I shall go to bed early. If I can but sleep I may feel refreshed. Good-bye." To speak the honest truth, a sense of great opprem- sion and a lump in my throat robbed me of words. The pathetic manner of Lucia Diaz, her singularly subdued voice, and the whole circumstances sur- rounding this scene would tend to give the most callous a great heart-ache. I was painfully affected; and retiring to my room, I found solace in a pipe and a good long think. Bogeys disappear with the sunrise, and next morning would possibly reveal a more cheerful picture. As night approached and Mark had not returned, I went to bed; but, as is usually the case after unpleasantness, my rest was disturbed by nasty dreams. Like books with an unwholesome tone, these nightmares depressed rather than refreshed my mind; and so, when Mark returned about mid- night and had occasion to need my advice, he found no little difficulty in waking me. I turned over and rubbed my eyes. "Sandy, rouse up, old chap! I never heard a fellow snore so in all my life! Listen! While I was at the palace an angry crowd of citizens came demanding an audience of the Queen. They bore a wounded Indian—a guard of the royal house— and Goya led the mob. On %aim agreeing to see them, they were taken into the audience chamber, and there Goya pitched a strange yarn about you and me being caught in the act of breaking into the treasure-housa but an hour ago! The burglars got off, but a big fellow wounded the guard rather dangerously, and the comical part was thal he swore I was his assailant! I believe there ill aipme truth in the burglary, but, as I was luckily dining with Kam at the time, the Queen naturally scouted the idea of our complicity." "It was the wark o' that de'il Muro ! " "One of the serving-women tells me that fully two hours ago Lucia went out, ostensibly for a breath of fresh air. She hasn't returned." _ -</p>
        <p>My face blanched; I clutched the bed-clothes convulsively; and then Mark and the things in the room seemed to swim round me.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>CHAPTER XVIIL OUR QUEST. •</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XVIIL OUR QUEST. •</p>
        <p>I staggered on jumping out of bed; for if my words had driven her to commit a rash act my predicament was awkward. I seemed chilled to the marrow, and a cold sweat was upon me. I dressed quickly, Mark questioning me the while. "Was she upset because I went to the palace ? " "Somewhat," I stammered; "an,' to be honest wi' ye, Mark, I was a bit cross wi' her fer being so jealous o' Olive." "If she has committed suicide," he cried in distress, "I shall never hold up my head again!" A wintry smile passed across my face, for I knew how time makes us forget a most poignant sorrow. Also, the rich, like Mark Pepys, can have change of scene and company, while the poor have always the associations of the event about them.</p>
        <p>After the first flush of excitement the natural calmness of the courageous patrician asserted itselt. "I hate a fuss, Sandy; let us quietly rouse thR lads. I have quieted the women's agitation. They think Lucia guilty of craven treachery, but I spurn the idea. It will be a pretty pickle if anything tragic has happened." "How's the nicht " I asked, fumbling:with my braces.</p>
        <p>"Very clear." And he drew his band across his forehead. "Perhaps it was not generous of me to accept the Queen's invitation without Lucia. The qualms of conscience took away my appetite. I sat an hbnoured guest of the woman Iliad come to kidnap!" _</p>
        <p>- "A man canna always be tied to a wuman's apron - strings," I replied somewhat testily. "Perhaps Lucia's engaged i' some intrigue." "No recriminations, Sandy," he rebuked, plain- tively adding as he paced to and fro: "Whatever has happened is entirely my fault. Look alive, do You want a blessed valet! I could dress in half the time!"</p>
        <p>"Perhaps the Sefiorita has a touch o' neuralgia, an' is passively nursing it i' the moonlight—wi' a sympathiser saying nasty things aboot Olive an' ye! Wumen don't usually destroy theirselfs until despair has driven 'em to temporary insanity." "There is one consolation," he rejoined, brighten- ing up a bit, "Lucia is not quite of that turn of mind. We must light all available lanterns." "An' go weel armed, to guard against the sur- prise o' her harmless flirtation! She wouldna wi' ane wing, nor spoil her pretty face in a mu'/ pond." "You make too light of it, old fellow, and don't like it. There is a serious side to this episode, which you can't fathom, nor I explain. I shall be heartily glad to be rid of the suspense. She has not gone provided with money or jewels. If in a sudden frenzy she has committed suicide--" "Bosh! " I replied somewhat grimly, though with inward misgiving. "Perhaps the jealous puss went to the palace to see if Olive walked part o' the way haine vvi' you. The Sehorita will turn up in a few minutes, smiling at putting us in a stew.' . "Not she." firmly averred my companion. little ,</p>
        <p>knowing that every regret of ms was a stab in my guilty heart, and that her last conversation with me was etill ominously ringing in my ears. "I have an instinctive dread of the search. The serving-women tell me she has taken nothing with her but what she stands upright in. Had sue run away she would have taken her music and guitar. You may depend upon it we shall have to drag the river before her body is found." I sighed in response, and with heavy hearts we went forth to pursue our doleful search. Try as we might, it was impossible to keep the maids from chattering, and when the news of Lucia's mysterious disappearance reached the palace Kane was much concerned, and ordered every available means for discovering her whereabouts. Volunteer searchers multiplied until all Golden Vale seemed astir. By nine o'clock in the morning every square yard of ground within the palace and public gardens was examined, every gully, nook, dead wall, out-house, kitchen-garden, and dwelling was either examined or thrown open for inspection. The gates were locked and barred as usual, so it was impossible pass the guards, who were, indeed, extreme!: vigilant since the invasion by Muro. Every bastil n and turret door was securely fastened inside. No mountain guide was missing; nor, indeed, had a soul seen the passing of Lucia. The ground about the walls, both without and within, was examined for foot-prints. Practical anglers skimmed the placid river in light canoes five or six abreast, all but the paddlers peering through the crystal stream for a glimpse of the body. Every hollow tree, cistern, bath, and bed of rushes had eager attention, and to encourage the searchers Mark offered a handsome reward for any trace or clue. But not a soul came forward with a scintilla of information to solve this mystery, not even after days of search along, and even without, the valley. All sorts of yarns prevailed, silly and ridiculous, for during an excitement people exaggerate wofully. The Senorita had, indeed, gone as if spirited away; but Mark had too much of the gentleman left in him to suspect Kana of treachery, and Olive was sorely distressed ; in fact, of the women, she was the most assiduous in the search. After the wonderful hypnotic power of Kana's soothsayers, the predictions of her astrologers, the perfection of her scientific experiments, and the subtle use of the forces in nature such as to-day are being developed, I was foolish enough to con- jure up the story of the Witch of Endor. You see, the whole tribe was so hemmed in by. inaccessible cliffs, the few dangerous trails were so guarded, and the miniature canons rendered impregnable by torrent and glacier, that an escape was miraculous. Of course, bribery might effect much, but the punishments for betrayal or treachery were so severe, and the military training for generations so patriotic, that the Senorita 's disappearance was incomprehensible. Mark laboured incessantly, and the general excitement was intense. The slippery sides of the bluffs, the grisly points of the inner range, every crag accessible to deer and mountain-sheep, even the domes covered with juniper and dwarf pine, the lakes, muddy ppols, bear dens, eagle eyries, crevasses, and gullies formed by granite needles, were searched in vain. As the days sped and not a spark of intelli- gence came to us, Mark's face began to show signs of silent grief and unspeakable anxiety. After the eighth day strict watch was kept on the waters of the lake, and for miles down the river beyond the entrance to the westward; but bewilderment seized us when not the shadow of a clue rewarded our hard work and anxiety. Into our search alive continued to throw herself with zest, and to facilitate matters a conference of the civil and military rulers was held. Among other suggestions was one for the sending out of well-armed search parties in all directions and for many miles. "Could the Spanish lady climb the smokin; mountain and throw herself into the cone? asked a young brave. "No living soul within our borders," replied Kane, "has been able to go where the bears fail." As the Sefiorita was not credited with super- natural powers, Jip and other keen-scented dogs were enlisted in this new phase of searching; but, after many days of wearied hunting, party after party returned disheartened. Our united efforts were fruitless, and so, after a while, the excite- ment simmered down. But Mark's happiness was seared, and my conscience lashed with many stripes. A plague upon the women!</p>
        <p>Now it came to pass that, as Olive was custodian of her ancestral dead, the time arrived for her customary visit to the hermetically-sealed sepulchre where the bodies rested. Not that the Princess would enter the caverns, but simply examine the adobe paste at either entrance to report its security to her mother and the priests. It was after the fulfilment of this mission that the girl came running towards our house with anxious face and tearful eyes. We went to the front door and met her.</p>
        <p>"What ails thee, Olive? " asked my colleague. "Naught ails me, save such fright as makes my hair stand on end and congeals my blood! " "Tell me thy trouble," said Mark, gently placing his hand upon her shoulder. "The eagles which swooped down upon our fold to seize our lambs were beaten off' but have returned to plunder and destroy. They have entered the caves of my ancestors by the secret opening that gave us access to the Golden Vale, and stolen the four Royal bodies. Alas! the punish- ment falls upon me, for while I slept the enemy entered in and desecrated the dead. Woe - falleth upon the watcher, oven on me. My mother's anger will be more than I can bear! I was saved from the tomahawk and the fire, only to linger in the Queen's everlasting displeasure! It is a living death!" _ _ _</p>
        <p>"This is unlucky," observed Mark. "Cheer up, Olive; I will plead for thy forgiveness." "Mark," said I, "ye dinna comprehend. Na ordinary vigilance could avert this. We're a' betrayed." "Sandy!" exclaimed my leader, seizing me by both shoulders, while the girl was crying bitterly, "what do you insinuate ? " "Why," I whispered, drawing him aside, "Pecos hasna stirred frae some o' us. Ye yerser would be the most interested in the shrivelled corpses, especially it there was a likelihood o' treasure-trove." Mark opened his eyes wide with astonishment. "None of our party would have a motive in stealing such a ghastly quartet." "An' I wouldna gie a bawbee fer the lot. E'en the Indians ken desiccated flesh an' muscle frae dried bison. Some antiquary has stolen 'em." And I nodded my head meaningly, to gin emphasis to my idea. "Then disabuse your mind on that score," angrily replied my friend, his voice laden with disdain. 'lf I were in the mind to seize anything or anybody here it would be my stolen treasure or the Queen. The theft of Kana's ancestors has been planned and carried out with considerable skill, or we should have heard something about it before." "Na doot a wuman is at the bottom o' it," said I with a smirk.</p>
        <p>"I am sick of hearing you preach about woman's deceit. In your eyes Olive is designing and liana crafty. One would imagine you were the son of a she-dragon!" I was peppery in a moment, for my mother was a good and patient Christian, and I told Mark so, fair and square. "Spell ho I " he cried, lifting his eyebrows and hands in a deprecating sort of way. 'Your sus- leioneThave dawned on me. I was blind, and a fool I" "Folly is a leech ; it reduces the fever, but sucks the life. Lucia knew the -secret entrance."</p>
        <p>"By Jove, yes," he answered, staggering so that I gripped his hand to steady him. Pecos, who was comforting Olive, stared with astonishment. "An' what is mair, Mark, she saw the blame would fall on Olive if the bodies were stolen. A jealous wuman has had her revenge." "Oh, nonsense, Sandy; you are always harping on one string. If cabinet ministers bungle you say it is petticoat government; if a lord pa, bankrupt you attribute it to the influence - actresses or music-hall singers ; if domest), relations are strained you blame the nagging wife. With you men are demi-gods, and women inferior mortals."</p>
        <p>"It is comfortable to pass through life wi'oot helping to correct abuses, but there must be men to clear the course afore ye can enjoy the race. I put it to ye as a tentative proposition only, that Lucia was in league wi' ootsiders—Muro to wit." fl "No, Sandy, I was not simpleton enough to love the girl who would play such a vile game. I feel intensely disgusted with myself for letting her come to the Golden Vale, but I'm not the least dubious of her sincerity. Lucia may have been upset, but she was not disloyal." "Weel, she has a noble champion. "Thanks for the sarcasm—don'tlapologise, old chap, but tell me, what prompted her to escape? " , A deesposition quick to tak' offence, an'--" "Bosh!" he 'interrupted ; "your liver is out of order. The bodies were of no earthly use to her." II "Assuming, fer sake o' argument, that she took</p>
        <p>'ern, her motive was revenge. licittier ;blood-rela tionship nor wealth can save Olive. History pros the bloodthrstiness o' the Indain nature, wbiel underlies this apparent civilisation. A negli prince, educated at Oxford, has been known return to the barbaric state in his am n counial Ye may hae seen an instance ? ' Mark wt silent; Oxford was forbidden ground for discussiot "John Bull is hard t' move," I continued, "b 1 he can mak' a mighty stir if goaded to 't; 60 Nl.' liana's people. We are saddled wi' a grill, responseebility if those sacred bodies are thieved. Well, if all you say be true, I shall be in another addition to the list of fools wise after tl event. Yet, from certain misgivings I have, I fel the worst. If Olive is placed in danger by folly of mine I shall do my best to save her." "That's manly," said I, pointing to the prostrel girl, who, in her distress, was nursing her achill forehead on her hands, "an' I'll help ye wi my might an' main. Let's see to 't at aince." At that instant Mark stepped forward, and pieke up a cross bid behind the glass on the mantel shelf. He examined it aside, and when he turtle it over the expression of his countenance ws significant. His lips formed words, and I coul see by the reflection of his face in the glass till this discovery angered him. "What hae ye there, Mark?" "A piece of Noah's Ark, worm-eaten and rusty, he answered, tossing his head as my words rouse him from a grievous reverie. "But wi' a message scratched on the back; 3 canna deny that!" "Nature made a mistake, Sandy. You shoal have been born a Scotch female, for you take ti prize for curiosity." And as I screwed up my lii and whistled he tapped his pocket and added, wit chilling dignity: "Drop the pump-handle; this i my secret." The style and nature of the writing, the 'writsl the purport of the message, whether the cross el placed there by design, whether certain marks o it were made by the thief or an accomplice, Lucia was mentioned, or Olive—none of thei things should I know yet. We moved towards the couch where the Prince' lay, and on hearing us approach she started u and threw herself at Mark's feet in an implorin attitude.</p>
        <p>"Save me " she cried, her dark eyes an troubled face the picture of misery, "save me fro] the wrath of my people! If liana frowns on for my lack of vigilance, the priests will whip 1:11 in the market-place! " "When mob-law rules," I observed, "reason I smothered."</p>
        <p>"Perhaps the Queen herself has ordered a removal of the bodies into some deeper recta; c the Royal caverns," said Mark, raising her. nee search further, and not despair." Then, smoothing her glossy 'black hair, this inn of valour nobly vowed to shield her from danger. "Remember the traditions o' tribe,' said to him as we made preparations for assapesd examination of the sepulchre. "If the de hi disappeared, their spirits vanish, ne'er t' return thus a great calamity threatens Rana, to aro which somebody will be sacrificed." "The Great Spirit will mock me," cried Olive "when I cry for mercy!" "Her protection will require both diplomacy a scrurage," said I. "Come, then," he answered, "let us cautioned search the caverns."</p>
        <p>"Terra weal," said I. "We've a dangerous toe afore us. I'm game to face it like a Briton." "A zealous man never thinks of failure," 1J responded. "My hope needs a lot of killing." I had seen no reason yet to suspect Kane c duplicity in the robbery of Mark's treasures, s? I was fully convinced that any attempt to play tv fool with the Queen or her officials would land ti in serious difficulties. If we got out of the preset dilemma we might think ourselves lucky, for a picion even that we had meddled with the WO relics of the past would rouse the worst part of ti Indian nature.</p>
        <p>(To be ootstiqued. )</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WINTER CALF-REA-RING : DESIRABL AND REMUNERATIVE.</head>
        <p>WINTER CALF-REA-RING : DESIRABL AND REMUNERATIVE.</p>
        <p>Its oldee times there was little winter cilf-rearine, I the simple reason that winter dairying was not Mu practised. But now that the direct supply ef milk tewns has becqme such an impsrtant branch of daircit large numbers of cows ar, required to calve in auto( and in early winter, that the supply from firmere merchants in towns may be regular and continuous. fact, most farmers connected with the trid are un4 written contracts bound to supply stated quantitiee winter as well as in summer, and this renders it t indispensibly necessary that there eh uli be frit calvers to take the place of the cows, which came lo profit in spring, as they get dry. In co, s this, many farmers find it necessary to calve dosell, mans cows in autumn as in spring. There is also 01 more butter-making in winter than there used tf,„ Consumers at the present day insist on having le; butter all the year round,and foreigners wi I suppil breakfast-table adequately with it if British fennel,' net. This, too,leads to the old system of causiug all cows to calve in spring to be depart,cl from. 'f'7, have changed, and brought in their train the requ'' merit of a great deal of winter calf-rearing Many farmers are under the fakes impression t1:1 winter calf-rearing is a matter of difficulty, yield, little, or no profit, whereas it is quite the reverse. 94, ducted on proper principles, it is not only one or; greatest necessities of our modern agricalture,but aso lead to highly rernuverative returns. Of coulee t maternal nourishment must be done without. Milltt winter is too valuable for calves to subsist on, set, contrasts before named having to be fulfilled, esul cannot be allowed to partake of it after they are tw° three days old. Nor, indeed, is there the !lightest Oe that they shoula.The necessities of the case have brow Into existence a new food called Lactifer, which, Detl a thoroughly cooked meal,is specially adapted to suit, delicate stomachs of young calves, and vssessen elements Of milk,se proved by scientific analysis. Beo, which there is abundant testimony i•if prac'ical fare' that calves do quite as well on Thornley's Lactiter aq:‘, the milk of their dams. The writer of this article " nearly a hundred calves at a large Milk Supply Fan' few weeks since not one of which had received 3 of milk after being three days oil, and a more well-doing lot never met his eyes before. The young; calves take readily to Lactifer, and are very fond 0 and until they can take a lit is solid food, the soup 33! 'from it must be their only nourishment. AfterWe' toot pulp, intermixed with pollard, bran and bean 031 seasoned with Thorley's Food, should be provided them, together with a whisp of the swertest and pi hay for them to nezzle-to and pick from. The degfeet richness in the solid food must depend on whether , calves are being reared for early maturit beef-1115t?l or merely as stores. In tee former case they might "°' • large proportion of maize meal or barter meal iest,e of bran. Oatmeal is a very stimulating auxiliary for all kinds of young growing animals, and Thin Cake, broken into small bits and laid in their treng will make them lay on flesh rapidly. Current prices for beef have fallen exceedingly !owl that graziers find it impossible any longer to melte se, factery profits by conducting their business on the lines. The cause is very patent. The beef produci enters into cempetition with American, and consoill require a different article altogether. Butchers tl!' long since told the grazing community that their turners demand small joints, not made too fat, SO readily give from 1d to 2d per pound more for!, than the beef commonly produced. Farmers afb°;/;44 to make their cows calve in autumn or early crl"„ might find it easy and natural to rear their calves 01 to meet this demand. To make their calves best ade,P4 to lay on flesh rapidly while in growth, they shentat sired by pure-bred bulls. Being reared by Lactifso er , would not miss the milk of their dams, and could be made to partake of solid auxiliary food, , Thorley's Cake, pollard, and maize meal, so would fatten readily while bring reared. Fe lei these substances,with root pulp, seasoned with Thnfiti, Food, and a little hay, they would be in prime een'srs after May Day to make the best possible return grass feeding. Henceforth the s'udyr of their °Wilt would be to make them fatten as rapidly as Pro while in a state of growth, and they would be in ?ow condition to supply just the kind ot care oses and J, betehers and the consuming public are prepared the highest price for when at f-rim 18 to 24 montbil e fre The redemption of the British grazing intereet"...pc utter ruin is therefore bound up with this highly ll"so tint matter. Calves, instead of being wanrool9,,, • feed at a tender age, as they have been so generallb;e decidedly wanted for rParage purposes, if dairY can only be induced to keep pedigree bulls. T's ve winter rearage of calves by Lactifer would prove,os, necessary link in the chain of a modern system 1"`co tl be even more advantageous to the grazing tea" dairy section of the agricultural community. in a Plsr; gr CLARKE'S WORLD-FAMED BLOOD MIXTURE teed cure for all Blood and Skin Diseases. It iarireet searching blood cleanser ever discovered, and it wi" system from all impurities from whatever Oath 50, For Scrofula, Scurvy, Eczema, Bad Legs. Pimple' of Teo of all kinds, its effects are marvellous. Thousands ere monials. Sold everywhere, at 2s. 91. per bottle. Bew (0 worthless imitations and substitutes.-4 drf• CLARKE'S B 41 PILLs are wa rranted COte irecia sex, all acquired or constitutional Discharr Free .1:1 Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the bi" 2 boxes Mercury. Establishe upwards of 3) years. " ‘r I lora thli each, ot all Chemists ind-Patent Medicine • en`mekersi but the world, or s: it for sixty stamps by thl roll* Lincoln and Midland , 'curdles Druz CompsilYi ydvt</p>
