      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>During her stay on Deeside, the Queen received an almost constant stream of guests, and before Her Majesty had been many hours at Windsor, she welcomed her granddaughter, the Grand Duchess Serge of Russia, who with the Grand Duke, arrived from Paris. They were accompanied from Darmstadt to Paris by the Grand Duke and Duchess of Hesse, who travelled incognito as Count and Countess Starkenberg. The Queen when travelling incognito styles herself " Couatess of Balmoral," the Prince of Wales "Earl of Chester." The Empress Frederick ot Germany fre- quently travels as the " Countess of Lingers," while the ex-Empress Eugenie invariably assumes the title of " Comtesse de Pierrefcnds." The name has very happy memories for her. The magnificent Château de Pierre. fonds was a favourite halting place in the course of her excursions in the forest of Compidgn, and the restoration of it, entrusted to the famouc architect Viotlet le-Duc, was one of the artistic events of the Second Empire. The Duke and Duchess of Fife's long autumn visit to Mar Lodge has at last come to an end, much to the latter's regret, as Her Royal Highness dearly loves her Scotch home. The happy home parties to which only her immediate relatives, and a few intimate friends are bidden; are a great delight to the Duchess, who to this day, retains much of her old dislike of "strangers." The Duke is by profession a banker, being the principal partner in Scott's Bank. The original Duffs were Irish peers, but the Duke of Fife regards himself as a High- land chieftain, and very often appears in a kilt. Probably no one more sincerely welcomed each of the lchildren of the Duke and Duchess of York, than Louise, Duchess of Fife. She looked with absolute horror ors the possibility of the Crown of England falling to her, and for some time the crown hung within appreciable distance above her head. Had the Duke of York, then Prince George of Wales, who was in an extremely deli- cate state of health died without children, we must , have looked on Princess Louise as our future Sovereign. The Duchess inherits from her mother, the most perfect I taste, and knows in a moment how to arrange things to Ithe best advantage. Her two little daughters are very sweet looking children, and just at an attractive age. Princess Louise, Marchioness of Lorne, and the Mar- quis, are at present the guests of the Duke and Duchess of Argyll at Inveraray Castle. They will shortly pm- ceed to Windsor Castle on a visit t the Queen, and afterwards to Sandringham. The Marquis of Lorne, as most people are aware, is the Duke of Argyll's eldest son, but he, being without children,the title will pass to Lord Archibald Campbell and his son. Lord Archibald throneh his mother will also become heir to the Ardkinglass estates. The Marquis or Lorne has made excursions in. to the realms of decorative art, as well as those of litera- ture and music. He has executed a number of designs for wall-papers, and friezes, several of which were pur- chased by a well-known London firm, by whom they have been printed and placed upon the market. The Ladies Primrose, stand high in favour with the Queen, and Prince and Princess of Wales, and the Empress Frederick seems to have been likewise favour- ably impressed with her young hostesses, during her re- cent visit to Dalmeny. Both have the cagy grace and charming manner of their father, and are in great re- quest at country house pastes. They are always beauti- fully, but at the earn° time very simply dressed, and wear comparatively little jewellery. Lord Roceberry is naturally very proud of his daughters and takes them about himself as much as his many public engagements will allow. Madame Adelina Patti's concert at the Albert Hall (London) was, as is usual, a huge success. The vast building was packed from floor to ceiling, and not only the prima denna,but nearly &amp;lithe other performers were called upon to double the contributions for which they , had been announsed. Gowned in pure white, and wear- ing magnificent diamonds, Madame Patti looked more youthful than ever, and, need it be said—sang divinely. Madame Patti is considered to be one of the best women billiard players we have. The billiard room at Craig-y-Nos is exquisitely fitted up, and is the favourite rendezvous. Here all guests collect at certain times of the day. The diva has a most valuable collection of cues, many of them the gift of Royalties. One of the most cherished of her possessions however, is an auto- graph album, magnificently bound, and secured with a secret lock. In this may be found the writing of all the leading musicians of the last half century. Ten thou Sand dollars has just been offered by an American collector of autographs, for a genuine Shakes- peare specimen, but as there are only seven known to be in existence, he is not Irk ly to succeed in securing the treasure. The British Museum is credited with having paid over £3OOO for its Shakespeare autograph. The Queen is said to possess the most valuable collection of</p>
      </div>
