      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>One inan's Cure.</head>
        <p>One inan's Cure.</p>
        <p>A CORRESPONDENT of the "Wilts County Mirror," hardly believed, when he saw Mr. John Lampard, Barford St. Martin, near Salisbury, whose illness had been described to him, that this was indeed the man who for ten years had been the victim of spinal disease of the worst kind. "It is ten years ago that I recklessly lay dow in an open field when in a perspiration," he confessed. "I caught rheumatics, and my spine also became seriously diseased ; in fact, I seemed to be completely breaking up I had to give up work. This was ten years ago. I failed to get any better, until early in the present year I read of the wonderful cures that had been effected by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and decided to try a box. I did not seem to feel any benefit for a day or so, but I went on with the pills, and to my great surprise, befoie I had finished the first box I certainly felt much better. I bought a second box, but actually I did not require them all ! I completely recovered my strength, and was never better in my life than at this time"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Husband and Wife.</head>
        <p>Husband and Wife.</p>
        <p>MRS. WAREHAM, a Hampshire lady interviewed by the "Portsmouth Times," stated :—"About three months after my baby was born I was seized with dreadful pains all down the back, and began to lose a quantity of blood. As I had previously undergone two operations for this, I naturally became much alarmed. I had suffered in this manner for seven years on and off, but after my child came I was so ill that I was almost out of my mind. Asked what she next did, Mrs. Wareham said her husband had read in the papers about Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and advised her to get a box and see if they would do her any good. "So," proceeded Mrs. Wareham, "I sent for a box of the pills before I had taken half its contents my trouble stopped. I took two boxes and ever since I have been quite myself again. Dr, Williams' Pink Pills have entirely cured me." Mrs. Wareham added that the pills had no ill effects on her babe at her breast. She also mentioned that her husband had suffered dreadfully from rheumatism. Using Dr. Wil- liams' Pills, he found relief after taking the first dose. Mr. Wareham fully bore out his wife's statement as to the beneficial effects accomplished by taking Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday last week the Warrington Musical Society opened its season with a concert in the Parr Ball which will be remembered as one of the most successful of the Society's efforts in this direction. 1 The audience, in point of numbers, left little to be desired, and the building being well tilled in almost every part, the whole interior presented a very animated appearance. "The Creation" formed the chief item in the programme, and being curtailed of part 3 relief was thus given (to that not incon- siderable section of the musical public who do not care for an evening devoted entirelyinvoEtevdangeleri teorr)raet:,iiereic:)Mbry. some miscellaneous items forming a second part of programme. m and Mr. Andrew B.ack had been announced nlolivanmced en as principal vocalists, but the tenor again failed to make his debut in our midst, and as this is, we believe, the third occasion on which he has disappointed Warringtonians it will not be surprising theretora if attempts to secure Mr. Green's services are once ada%r tahlel gievleevnenutph. in Mr, Edward Bh hour,roswa es cv oeamr b most td s aoalbclilewet ye snit found,3wtietlult. We have no hesi- tation in saying that the music of such portion of Haydn's masterpiece as was undertaken never re- ceived a finer and more adequate interpretation at the hands of the Musical Society than on Wednes- day. The singing of the principal vocalists one and all was nighty satisfactory, and the repetition of several (Dios and concerted numbers demanded by an enthusiastic audience might have been gran- ted had time permitted. And more effective choral singing than was here shown at the very beginning of the society's working session has rarely been heard. The earnestness with which the various sections of the voices coped with the difficulties of the more massive choruses was alike refreshing and augurous of future successes. Nor were there many shortcomings in realising the sub- tler nuances of expression at suitable opportunities as notably in the first subdued entry culuminating in the grand burst of choral harmony at the words " And there was light " as also subsequently at the point" A new created world springs up.' And while speaking of the work of the chorus, unqual.fied admiration must be further ex- pressed of the neatness and unity which was noticeable in the part-song " Who is Sylvia ? " (E. German). This was a really fine piece 0 F choral singing anycl,thcennsdkuileftuolr,wcanyatirnolwlehdicahndalrp.iCiortoesisltehye, tmhaessSeodcvieotices was not less worthy of high praise. An animated performance of the chorus" Liberty," a highly dramatic little work by Eaton Fatting, completes the enumeration of the choral items in the prJgramme, and it is due to say that the sing- ing of the soprano solo in the last-mentioned work by Mrs. E.Atkin (one of the Society'e members) was both artistic and effective, and contributed largely to its sacces4ul performance. The Society's orchestra (considerably augmented, chiefly in the brass instruments) hardly reached its accustomed standard in dealing with the instrumentation of "The Creation." Amongst the strings more especi- ally there was at times slack of purity of tone and , a tendency to overwhelm the solo voices which it I would be well to carefully guard against. Nor was • the execution in this and other sections at all times free from a certain roughness and vagueness which, though it may be largely exceptional, is not the less to be regretted. The introductory re- presentation of chaos in " The Creation " and Schu- bert's overture" In the Italian style " were not- withstanding these strictures in many respects well played, the last mentioned especially presenting in its tunefulness and variety many Commendable points. In the second part ot the programme Miss Evangeline Florence was most Ast h t h rapturously ap- plaudedertrounseleys ( Venmn) whicheinth w e we rsaultnzd-seontgand was re-introduced at this concert by request, and she acknowledged the resulting encore with a cbarraing and piquant rendering of the little song "You and I.' Mr. Andrew Black was similarly highly successful in pleasing the audience in "The young Royalist" (Stephen Adams)--a success due rather to Mr. Black's ability alum to any merit in the song itself. Complying to demands for further favours Mr. Black essayed the quaintlf humor - song from "The Sorcerer " "Time was when Love and I were well acquainted," in inimitable style and re -iced vociferous applaaTunhsdee.smaMiulorsri,cei Grave,".Eadnwlyarad (dB ran rube's vtbtio° the haml iiosnas intelligent oit geoeqfnui vocal signs of the audience's llollv ) rn af p wasmph r - ciation. It is to be hoped that having go auspic- iously opened its concert season the Musical Se,.ipty will be stimulated to attain further succes,es in "The Messiah" and "St. Paul," announced for , Christmas and the New Year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>/tOTHER,</head>
        <p>/tOTHER,</p>
        <p>anxious for the healthy development of their growing girls, will find confidential information in the Directions for Use, and especially on pages 8 and II of a useful little book (sent anywhere free of charge by the Dr. Williams' Medicine Company) called</p>
        <p>“THE WAY TO Be Nileug.”</p>
        <p>autographs in the world. Besides the names of nearly every crowned head in the world with his or her por- trait, distinguished artists, musicians, and litterateurs abound. In hats, those of the tricorn persuasion have taken a considerable hold of us,and the effect is often—too hard. Folks seem; to find it so difficult to realise that what is one woman's meat is ancther's poison. A very pre- minent forehead and chin can bear that the brim should be simple and rather high. Another face, with smaller irregular features will require a bunch of black tulle above the forehead and wings or plumes that droop downward either side of the front almost hiding the up- turned brim. And with this earticular style of head- gear, the hair must be dressed low. At hour or se spent occasionally in deliberate "trying on," experimenting as to the effect of one thing with another, is always a good investment of time, even for a busy woman. An hour in front of the glass, in a spirit of genuine self-criticism tends to the right distri- bution of one's wealth, whetlpr limited or otherwise. The woman given to carefully thinking out what she needs, and what suits her is often able to sally forth well-turned out upon half or a quarter the sum spent annually by the reckless individual, who moreover seldom appears fit to be seen. "True lovers" knots in lace, jet, or silken passemen- terie are well to the fore in all the best shops, for appliqueing on evening skirts, and the front of the bodices. " Knots "of this description, made of velvet wound upon wire, to wear in the hair, are shawls with I this .form of trimming. A ILttle imitation osprey, two or I three tiny plumes, or an aigrette of small flowers, usually accompanies each " knot," and the effect is usually most becoming. Once more an attempt has been made to bring in the single eye-glass, and a few women who would rather be ridiculous than not be talked of at all have promptly patronised the idea.Of all the inventions of the optician the single eyeglass is probably the most injurious to the ' sight, and further the handsomest woman looks detest- able with one screwed in her eye. It will not take them long to find all this out, and the mode is certain to pass soon into the limbs of thing one" does not do." The latest discovery is that a thick, yellow fog is by no means altogether banef lin its effects. It c Attains innumerable sulphurous and carbonaceous particles and these tiny atomies act as disinfectants and deodorisers the particles of carbon being particularly active. Only a confirtned optimist could however, gather comfort from these scientific suggestions. and to the majority of us, these atmospheric antiseptics will always be worse than the disease. According to the Director of the Illinois State Weather Bureau, there is a decided connection between the weather and crime. After considering the police records of various American cities for six years, he has coma to the conclusion that the total number of arrests shows a marked increase in crime, with the increase of tempera- ture. The increase of crime is very marked when there tis a deficiency of rainfall, and still more marked where temperature conditions are aggravating causes. There is a decrease in crime during the winter months, and when there is an excess of rainfall in summer. Only a few years back it was a common thing at a country house for thelangaid town bred man to complain bitterly about the "everlasting tramps" which the girls insisted upon him undertaking. Timings have changed nowadays. We cheerfully play cricket, golf, hockey, and ride a bicycle till we are almost ready to drop, but we don't walk, which is a pity, for of all exercises per- haps walking is the most healthful and complete in inself. It is indeed an exercise which ought to be freely indulged in by every woman who sets any value upon a clear complexion, a good figure, and a free and graceful bearing. STEWED NORMANDY PIPPINS.—,WeII wash one pound of pippins, stand in one quart of water all night. Nest day turn into a pan, add half a pound of castor sugar. and one lemon cut into slices. Let all boil slowly for 30 minutes, then add a further half pound of sugar and simmer gently till the apples are done. Add a little cochineal, arrange the apples in a glass dish, and strain the syrup over the fruit. " MADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>PHENOMENAL TONG JUMPING. A. ST. HELENS MAN WINS THE WORLD'S CHAMPIONSHIP.</head>
        <p>PHENOMENAL TONG JUMPING. A. ST. HELENS MAN WINS THE WORLD'S CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>On Saturday Joe Darby and T. Colquitt met at Bury. to jump, without weights, ten standing jumps for £5O aside, and practically also for the championship of the world. For many years Darby was simply unapproach- able at jumping, whether backward or forward, high or long springs, and there was hardly a record that did not stand to his credit. But he is getting older, and, as he has fallen off, others have come on. His record for a standing long jump, without weights, is 12ft ltio., and with weights 14ft Sin, which latter he did at the Star Music Hall, Liverpool, in 1890. Without weights he had done 61Ft siln in five jumps, and he has also done 16ft 4in in the last of three springs. In a standing back spring he has covered 12ft llin. while he has cleared 61t siin. in a running and 69t 3in in a standing high jump. Colquitt, who hails from St. Helens.has recently done some big things,but Darby was considered to have the better chance. It turned out that he had not, for, jumping in beautiful style, Colquitt heat Darby's first effort by 10ft, clearing 118 ft 6in, and Darby, after mak- ing another effort, gave up the contest, the distance be- ing beyond him even at Ws best.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</head>
        <p>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>me. J. E. Moore, of the Roy al College of Science, is about to conduct a scientific expedition to Lake Tanganyika, with the object of exploring the geology and life of its basin. He is an old biologi- cal explorer in the same region, having already given ten months' research into its fauna and flora, resulting in some very remarkable discoveries, and some extremely interesting speculations at to the geological history of the lake. The speculations rest, however, on insufficient data to warrant any d, finite conclusion being drawn. The finds con- sisted partly of laying molluscs, whose shields are almost identical in appearance with some very old European geological fossils, and partly in entirely unknown forms of lacustrine life. The new expedi- tion will dredge and drag the lake, and with this more thorough form of exploration some remark- able results are expected. It is estimated to cost , 1£5,000, towards which .£l,OOO has been promised by ' a gentleman connected with Central African trade. Mr. Justice Lawrance's criticisms recently on the frequency with which witnesses are now allowed to write on a slip of paper and hand to the judge the names of persons they desire not to disclose are meeting with hearty approval amongst lawyers. It is admitted that there are cases where this cus- tom is laudable and even necessary, but it often presses very hardly on individuals who have been identified in a general way by the witness and who have no opportunity of denying the imputation, as their names have not been given. The matter could not be dealt with by any rule of court. But there is a strong feeling that the practice ought to be discouraged, especially in inferior courts. It may be true, as is stated, that the members banded together last session under the leadership of Mr. Yerburgh to promote a " forward" policy in China were dubbed The Pig-tail party." But if so the nickname never came into common use. At the outset they were referred to as the " Far East party "or the " Far East Committee," When, how- ever, it leaked out that their usual meeting place was Mr. Yerburgh's house, in Kensington Gore, where discussion was carried on across the dining table, and that the only wines consumed were port and champagne, a member from Lancashire des- cribed them as the "Open Port and Champagne party "—a nickname which stuck to them until the end of the session. Mr. Yerburgh, who can appre- ciate a joke even when the laugh is against him, did not object to the nickname in the least. During the recent operations in the Soudan great interest was taken in the working of the howitzer battery under the command of Major Elmslie. It was the general opinion that the Lyddite shell would be more destructive than any explosive pre- viously used in warfare,and sensational accounts of the shock which it was said to produce— compar- ebbe only to that of earthquake— appeared in the London papers. But now that the detailed accounts of the fighting at Omdurman have been received it is surprising how little is said of the howitzer battery and the effect of its shell upon the forti- fications. As a matter of fact, I learn from a tra,t- worthy source that the Lyddite shell has not borne the test or war as well as was anticipated. The shooting it made was excellent. No fewer than six projectiles hit the Mabdi's Tomb at a range of two thousand yards, while the citadel wall was breached at almost equal range. But as there were at least three ascertained bursts inside the tomb and the edifice was still left standing it is evident that the appalling destructiveness of the shells has be over- rated. The most important test was when Major Elnislie ordered a small mud house standing in the line of sight to be demolished. No fewer than thirty rounds of Lyddite shell were put into this structure without doing it any serious damage. At the same time, tha report on the shell as a life- destroyer are far more " satisfactory," and some terrible sights were visible in the fallen city. It appears that when a shell bursts within an en- closure all living things within are frightfully mangled, even although not actually struck by the flying fragments. When he became bankrupt, Mr. Hooley ceased to be a member of the Carlton Club, and inasmuch as a bankrupt may not enter Parliament it may have been supposed that he also ceased at the same time to be the Conservative candidate for the Ilkeston Division of Derbyshire. Mr. Hooley entertains a different impression. He asserts that his candida- ture for Ilkeston is still in force, and that he in- tends to oust Sir Walter Foster at the next election. Even if Mr. Hooley is in a position to Persist with his candidature he might find some difficulty in fulfilling this amiable intention. Sir Walter Foster is a popular member, and in the height of his op- ponent's prosperity he was able to look forward with equanimity to the prospect of a struggle. His sense of security must have been materially streng- thened by recent events. The Conservatives of Ilkes- ton, of course will please themselves in their choice of a candidate, hut it is not imagined that they will accept Mr. belay's view that nothing has happened to change his position. In any event the candidate's bankruptcy offers a bar to his Par- liamentary ambitions. The disqualification ran only be removed by an order of discharge, which may be held in sum:else for a term of years. It re- mains; to be seen whether Mr. Holley will succeed in extricating himself during the life of the present Parliament.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>FUNERAL OF TIIE- LATE MR. JOHN' BURGESS, J,P.</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF TIIE- LATE MR. JOHN' BURGESS, J,P.</p>
        <p>The moral remains of the late Mr. John Burgess were quietly laid to rest in the churchyard of Overton Church on Friday afternoon, It was but fitting that he should be buried amid the scenes of his early life and in the family grave. Many sym- pathetic friends gathered round the graveside to pay a last tribute of reepect.The grave of red sand- stone was lined with ivy, veronica, and white chrysanthemum.. The Corporation of Warrington and magistrates honoured the . memory of the deceased gentleman by accompaning the cortege to the outskirts of the Borough. At the invitation of the Mayor (Alderman Wright, J.P..)the members of the Council and magistrates assembled at the Town Hall at 10 am, and proceeded to the late residence of the deceased. The conveyances 'were here dis- pensed with and those present walked in procession to the Stag Bridge where the conveyances were in waiting and brought them back again to Warring- ton. The Mayor sent his brolighain on with the funeral party to Frodsharn, and it was occupied be Mr. T. Longdin (borough surveyor). The town and corporation were represented by the Mayor (Aider- man Wright),Alderrnen Fairclough, Harrigon,Sutton Roberts, Blackhurst, and Hutchinson, Councillors Pimiento°. Lee, Bennett, Monks. Shaw Green,Jolley and Pierpoint ; Rev. Canon Willis (rector), Messrs. F. monks, 3.P., C.C., R. Garnett. J.P., T. Birchall j.p.. w. Roger., T. B. Malkin, R. T. Fairclough, W. L twleee, J. C. Thompson, Stanley Wilson, (deputy town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), and " T. Lonedin (borough surveyor). Letters of apology for non-attendance were sent from Mr PetPT Stubs, and Mr Milner. Toe time fixed for the funeral ceremony was 2.30 but it was a quarter of an h our later before the cortege was to be seen wending its way slowly up the steep incline leading to Overton Church,which, as our readers are doubtless aware, is situated near the base of Overton Hills. The Vicar (Rev H B Blogg) was in waiting at the church gates, and im- mediately on arrival the funeral service was pro- ceeded with. The coffin was of polished oak with brass mountings and was covered with numerous wreaths, A shield on the lid bore the following inscription :—" John Burgess, honorary freeman of the Borough of Warringten, born of a simple January, 1819, died 8 November, 1893." The service wag character and was conducted by the Vicar of the Parish, The mourners present were :—Mr William Bur- gess (eon), Mr William Fletcher (son-in-law), me Craig (son-in-law), Mr William Dawburn, Mr John Dawburn, Mr Fred Dawburn and Mr Bubert A Dawburn (grandsons) ; Councillor W J Burgess (Liverpool), Mr Charles Burgess. Mr Samuel Bur- gess, Mr John Burgess, Mr Alfred Burgess and Mr Herbert E Burgess (nephews), Dr Wood and Mr J A. Wood (Manchester). There were also present in the church Dr Gernall (medical officer of health), Mr Norman (inspector of nuisances), Mr William Broomfield (fever hospital), Mr Albert Smith and a few people from Frodshara. Floral tributes were sent by the widow and children, Mrs Kendrick (sister), Mrs George Bur- gess. Mr 11. Smith, Mr and Mrs John Burgess, Alderman and Mrs Bolton, Miss Burgess (Hudders- field), Mr and Mrs T Longdin, Councilor John Cros- field, Mrs Pickmere, Councillor and Mrs W J Bur- gess (Liverpool), Mr and Mrs Charles Burgess, ser- vants from Spring View, Whitley, Medical Super- intendent and Matron of the Fever Hospital, Chief Inspector of Nuisances and other officers of the Health Department, and Dr Gornall. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr T Jackson Lee (late Fletcher and Lee) of Bridge-street. The coffin was supplied by Messrs M Greenhalgh and Co., of Sankey-et., Warrington.</p>
      </div>
