      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WILLIAM rigADiN, Jusi.</head>
        <p>WILLIAM rigADiN, Jusi.</p>
        <p>There is great risk in allowing even an ordinary cold to go unchecked. Consumption claims thou- sands every year simply b-cause the weakened system does not receive the nefdA aid untd it is too late. When a person finds that his or her system does not recover quickly from a ccid it is a sure sign it is weak, and needs budding up. There is no denying the seriousness of taking cold and there is every reason why more than a local specific should be employed to assist in a speedy re- covery. Specifics alone will not overcome the con- ditions which both make the body susceptible to taking cold easily and also aggravates the trouble and encourages al sorts of complications. What is needed is the remedy v hich cured the gentleman whose photograph we hero reproduce together with his letter, which is as follows "35, West-street, Sheffield, July 19, 1898.</p>
        <p>. , " Gentlemen,—ln the early part of this year I caught a very severecold, which left me with a hard dry cough, and, although I tried various remedies, it seemed to get no better. Usually I have found mustard applied outwardly quite sufficient to cure me, but on this occasion it seemed to take no effect, and I was almost afraid of some serious lung com- plaint. However I happened to read one of your advertisements, and was induced to try one of your bottles of Scott's Emulsion, and the effect was marvellous. I felt a benefit from the very first few doses, and by the time I had taken the contents I was quite well again. It is now over four months ago, and I have never had the slightest return of the cough. I fed quite sure that Scott's Emulsion is a wonderful remedy for all lung troubles, and,</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL REFORMED CHURCH.</p>
        <p>if its curative properties were universally known the demand for it would be almost greater than the supply.—Yours truly, (signed) Wm. Aladin, (Jun.)" Mr. Madin's letter gives you the truth in a nut- shell. He had evidently tried various so-called remedies, but his system needed more than a specific, and be found in Scott's Emuleion immediate relief and a permanent cure. Scott's Emulsion is more than a speclfic. It is a well-known and stan- dard remedy for coughs, colds, bronchitis, and all ailments of throat and lung ; and this is by no means all of its merit.</p>
        <p>Scott's Emulsion contains cod-liver oil, hypophos- phites, and glycerine. It presents these remedial agents in a palatable form and one easy for indi- gestion. It is a preparation which has the praise of the medical profession by reason of its superior- ity over all over similar remedies, and finds popular favour on all sides because it is so pleasant to take and so effectual in restoring health and strength. Merely naming the ingredients of Scott's Emulsion is enough to show that, not only will i relieve a cough or cold, but it will further nourish and strengthen the system so that a permanent care can be effected. Now is the time to begin taking Scott's Emulsion, and fortify the entire body against un- favourable influences of the weather.</p>
        <p>You can obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion by sending threepence to cover postage to Scott and Bowne, Limited, Manufacturing Chemists. 95 Great Saffron-hill, Loadon, E.G., and mentioniug this paper.</p>
        <p>Diocesan Conference. If there were mire like him in. the Church of England things would not be as they are at the present time in regard to the ritualistic practiCelil in many churches. The Emmanuel Ref( rmed Church had his sympathy and as far as be could help them he would be pleased to do so. lie hoped their anticipations would he realised, and tat their hearts would be cheered by the success of the efforts thci had put forth. (Applause).</p>
        <p>FRIDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. There was a fair gathering of visitors for ths opening on the third (I ,V.</p>
        <p>_ . The Rev. E. T. REED said the honour had been conferred upon him of opening the sale of work that evening, though not sought for by biinsolf in any way He would much rather have called Upon settle one else, but it was the desire of the congregation. Re wished it was within his power to give a donation of five or even two guineas, blit ministers of tho E2iscopal Church had not very deep pockets, or if tliey thsro was little in them. He had supplied all the help he could,and perhaps that wasjust as necessary towards the success of the sale. The totL receipts for the previous day amounted to £37 15s. 5i., and when that was added to the first day's prenee is (269 us 2), it brought the total to £lO7 es 7d. (Applause.) He considered the result very sat- isfactory. He had received a letter that morning from Mr. G. B. Hunter, of Newcastle-oa-Tyne whom be met whilst in Canada. Re was informed by a friend that Mr. Hunter and his daughter were in Ottawa. Re went round to see him and they could imag Eie how pleased they %ere to se,/ him. ID the letter he received that morning from Mr. Hunter a cheque was en- closed and a bundle of photographs of the Klondyko region. Mr. Reed then extßaßSed his thanks and the thanks of his wife for the kind and hearts, assistance rendered by all. Ho must als3 thank Mr Ntterson who although not of their sect had as on a or.,v OU4 orcssioii given most able asiiyance. It is the intention iif Mr. Reed in December to give a lecture. illustrate 1 by a hundred limelight photographs taken with his camera whilst in Canada.</p>
