      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>SALE OF WORK.</head>
        <p>SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>There was agaln a good attendance on Thursday afternoon when the second day's proceedings were opened by Mr. John Taylor, of Museum-street, Warring- ton. Amongst those also present were the Revs E. T. , Reed, W. H. Towers (Mauchester),and H. G. Roberto (Wesleyan.) In the course of a few intr sductory remarks Mr. Reed said the total result of the first days proceedings was £69 lie 2d. (Applause.) That was a result which was exceedinely satisfactery. Out of that amount the sum of £22 les was received in donations. He was Pleased to see preseet that day the Rev. W. EL Towers, of Man- chester. All these connected with the Emmanuel Church and especially those of the Christian Endeavour Society were aware that Mr. Towers had passed through a seri- ous illness which brought him to the very gate of death. They all rejoiced that he was se far recovered as to able to be present. One hardly expected to receive sutsnriptieris on the second day but his (Mr. Reed's) heart was gladdeu,d to receive a cheque for £2 24 that morning from Councillor J. G. Graves, of Sheffield, who was as,ociated with him when he (Mr. Reed) had the pleasure of holding the position of secretary to the Young Men's Christian Asso- ciation at Sheffield. In calling upon Mr. Taylor to de- clare the Sale of Work open Mr. Reed said he could speak of many acts of kindness reeeived from him since he came to Warrington. In their work they had re- ceived a large amount of sympathy from other congre- gations. It was their desire to help their friends in Christ's cause in other denominations when the oppor- tunity came in their way. Mr. Taylor hai shown his true interest by handing teem a cheque for £5 53. (Ap- plaiire.) Mr. 'Lemon said he felt it no small honour to bo pre- sent among so many of his own personal friend 4. was pleased to have that opprrtunitv of eho wing his sympathy with them in their work. He had been some- what acquainted with the history of the Emmanuel Church from its commencement, aid knew somewhat of the inner working of the church. With regard to Mr. Reed,he thought that with such a man at the head and a great many young people who were able to work and did work, that they would prosper. He knew of no youne people in connce'ion with any chureh in Warrington whoee efforts in this and other directi ens did so much for the Church as the young people in connection with Emmanuel Church. (Hear, hear). He bad more than once held up these young people as models of perfection in church work and he trusted they would continue the work they had so well begun. As a church the Reformed Episcopal Cherch had taken a very bold stand against ritualism. He b lieved ritualism was growing in the church, and more particularly amongst the upper classes. (Hear, hear). He hoped the day was not far distant when the State Church of England should be no longer utilised by any vicar or curate, to tr ach and epread the doctrines of the church of Rome. (Applause). He did not think as far as Wealeyan M:thediem was concerned that the members would tolerate a lot of things that were practised in the various churches throughout the country. Be thought the laymen would pretect them in such a way that any Popish doctrine coull not exit many. devs. (Hear. hear). • There was great scope for reform in the Episcopal I churches of the Church of England, and he was pleased . to see the remarks of the Bishop of Liverpool at the</p>
