      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>NOVEMBER 18th, 1898</p>
        <p>Tun old-age pension problera Old Age Pensions_ Advancing by has been raised, and it has got Nesaqves to be settled. It has now bee* discussed for some years, and very little advance has been made. It looks as if no advance were possible unless we begin by clearing our minds of I cant. If we think that we can devise a achenle ' that will not be costly, and that will reward the virtuous, and leave existing provident organisa- tions in exactly the same position, we are labour-, ing under a delusion. We cannot begin too soon, to ascertain exactly wherelwe are. At present old age is left to the care of the poor law, the. family, or the provident society. The first makes' no distinction as to virtue, and every often it paYe more heed to the clever wastrel than to thr unfortunate and the careful. Let us re-^ member that if we frame an old-age pension scheme for the virtuous we cannot neglect the vicious, under the terms of our poor law, and without the accusation of cruelty.' Thus, whether we give to old age:pensions, or pooi law relief be offered where no pension is allowed, we have to keep old people who cannot work, an/ whose relatives cannot maintain them, out of the] local rates, and who are not otherwise providedl for. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN wants to see a poor lavi distinction made between the good and the bad characters. It is not recognised at present, and it cannot be made without leaving the unworthy poor to perish or live on the border of starvation We do not believe that any Legislature will ven- ture to make it.</p>
        <p>This negative gives us some little push forward. If the bad characters will get subsistence anyhos,, the question is simplified. We may as well have two scales and settle the matter—so much paid in relief to the bad, so much of a National Pension Fund for the good. But, if the bad get 38. 4s. a week and the good only 55., after the lat have contributed, where is the reward of virtue': Again, if the provident societies, as already worked" give old-ate allowances to the thrifty, on able terms, why talk of a national scheme ? The national scheme implies deficiency, failure,' urgency. If we are to have a national scheme all, it must be simple, uniform, easily worked, ope°, to everbody, and supersede the poor law at a cer7l tam n age. Are we prepared to think out a schenir„, on these lines ? Mr. Chamberlain is not frei wants a poor law on discriminatory lines, ' State assistance to provident schemes. This not an old-age pension scheme as ordinarilt understood because it does not provide genera pensions for all. A plan that is partial will never, be popular. A plan that is to be buttressed by discriminating poor law, intentionally severe and' cruel to the vicious and improvident, will not coincide with the generous feelings of the peo at large. If it be contended that the universeti pension must mean the compalsory universe° payment, then we see where we are. We have: still to deal with the defaulters. If it be a volun' tary scheme, there will always be the poor 101 system for the non-payers, with what ought to be: a better system, with a higher pension for thel subscribers to provident societies. Thus we have as yet only reached a series of negatives, and sv&amp; shall, perhaps, have to advance in this fashion oZ not at all.</p>
        <p>THERE is no reason why, eb an Intnresting Experiment." with so much that is interesti abroad and controversial at honi we should not turn our eyes to Ireland with a tam n amount of pleasure and impartiality. Therdi are signs of a better feeling between all classes WO that unhappy island. The cause of unity is a mend on the finances of the Empire. Irishme can always agree when they desire to make a cli into the Imperial Treasury, if the result of t dip be not for the benefit of a single class. TO claim for reduced Imperial taxation is now by landlords, merchants, farmers, labourers, every class in the community. Under Act of Union, Ireland was to pay much; and she is paying more. The policy maintaining the Union invoices fir revision, it is said, or the maintenance of the sent financial relations involves the cessation 01 the Union. The latter is not admitted at L as a point in practical politics. Well, about unity there cannot be a doubt, and what ml us most is whether this unity will be pi. during the new elections which are to come c)t/' shortly under the Irish Local Government Upon the special point we do not think there be any change. We hope that what we shall will be the presence amongst the candidates men of position and wealth, eager to do thew duty and to serve the public in local affairs. ,0 landlord class will make a great mistake if _ holds aloof, and thinks less of local than of penal politics.</p>
        <p>The experiment must be one of great coo.: quence. Some new voters will come to exerels°. the franchise in local affairs for the first tinl°ll3, their lives. They have usually cared less abotl" them than about railing against the distant Casti:, or the still mo,-.3 distant British GovernMel3l How are they going to vote now And what</p>
        <p>be the effect of their voting? There is much U 0 Certainty at present. Divisions of opinion promise to be more numerous than ever. They will be Personal, as we have already suggested, but they will also be industrial. In the large towns the 'Nice of labour is to be heard. Every ward in Dublin will be contested by a Labour candi- date. If the candidates in question decline to mix themselves up with Nationalist politics their eebrt may be of great effect. In allY mai it must be interesting to watch the growth in Ireland of a new party—a party Which need not have a creed on the Imperial ' question, and ought to be quite independent. It iS difficult to believe in the existence of such a Party at present, but it is one of the possibilities of the situation. The towns may very well re- turn some Labour councillors, as the country dis- tricts may return tenant farmers. We shall be • delighted to see both these things occur. It will give us more confidence in the results of local government in Ireland if it should bring in more new types and new classes. The ordinary type of politician in Ireland is unsuited for this new ex- periment. There is real business to be done, and men of common sense are wanted, who will study how to make the best of the new Act. The ex- periment will be watched by the whole world, In short, Irishmen are on their trial as local adminis- trators, and they will be judged according as they succeed or fail.</p>
        <p>THE statistics which have been published in re- gard to rabies encourage the hope that in a short time the order may be revoked in all parts of the Country. There are many people who have so great a horror of hydrophobia that they will hold this step undesirable, and will say the dogs are so accustomed tothe bondage that it is a pity to dis- turb the existing arrangement. But these orders or counties are grossly anomalous, and nothing short of muzzling throughout the island, or free- dom throughout the island,can ever be satisfactory. There must be constant irritation in the villages Which lie on the edges lf counties, and while re- gulations are made for districts there must remain a.„,P3mptation to bring pressure to bear upon the fainister of Agriculture, of whatever party, to re- the rule at the time of a bye-election. During tue forty-five expired weeks of the present year there have been only sixteen cases throughout England, whereas in the same period of the last three years the figui es were 142,411, and 597. In London, where the muzzling is most effectual, there has only been one case since January. ; but that may be partly due to the vigour with which stray dogs are hunted down. The" Critic" says "The Bishop who lately hazarded the statement that chemical workers as a</p>
        <p>class are well provided for 'ought to promptly join the board of the A.B.C. It is a concern in Whose control he would find congenial occupation; Or, if he preferred it, a judicious investment in the sweated slipper trade of Leeds or in East End tailoring might equally serve his purpose. To be Slowly done to death for a salary at which the Meekest of pales-faced curates would revolt, may o9ostitute the episcopal idea of being well pro- vided for.' It is not mine. For myself, I would r,athor be an unduly persecuted Editor than a L'.lllPered chemical worker; and I believe this :IL:4IY Bishop, if he were candid, would freely oowledge that even the Bench has advantages over a Widnes slum or a S Helen's workshop.</p>
        <p>The "Journal des Mats ", couples England 1 and France together in the phrase, "the two great nations of the West," to the complete exclu- sion of all the other western countries. That, , however, does not matter to us, though Germany imight feel inclined to dispute France's premier , claim to the title of one of the two great nations ' of the west. France is yet behind some of her con- temporaries as regards the higher principles of ‘' tivilisation. A relic of barbarity yet existing, and Which we in this country have long since abolished i 3 public execution. Across the channel condemned - criminals are guillotined in the sight ef all and 11141'4,7 • One would have thought that such revoiti yng procedure would have been abolished ears bill for ago, but it is only at this late hour that a Putting an end to these degrading spec- tacles is being ushered through the Chamber. We can well imagine the crowd there would be round the portals of an English prison if a prisoner was to be hanged there publicly. Morbid curiosity is a strong element in the physical nature of a large Proportion of the people,and there can be no doubt that it would lead many to view a spectacle repul- sive to the last degree. There are enough pernici- ous influences in the land, which tend to sap the tnoral strength of the young especially, and we Ought to be heartily thankful that such a thing here as a public execution is buried in the obscu- rities of the past. The French character is so emotional, and easily work fl upon that it will be a good thing for the nation at large when the full -Penalty of the law is inflicted within the four walls Of the gaol, and beyond the ken of the morbidly curious.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of members of the theatrical Profession in the Haymarket Theatre,London,this Week, a sum of £3,000 was contributed towards the Actors' Orphanage Fund in the space of half- an-hour.A. majority of the stars of the stage wa3 present,but we question whether another audience 13f similar dimensions and monetary weight could he found which would respond so generously to- Wards an obj,ct which they knew was naeritorious. The spontaneous generosity of the subscribers at the Haymarket meeting was magnificent and re- freshing. Mr. Cecil Raleigh set the ball rolling With a "sporting offer" of £25 if nine others would contribute a like amount—and the nine were at</p>
        <p>°nee forthcoming. Then two or three theatre Managers promised £lOO, and sums down to on.a.guinea being tendered in the most delightful spirit of emulation the handsome total of £3OOO Was netted in thirty minutes. They deserve help who help themselves, and we hope others will assist the " theatricals" in their honest endeavour to provide for the orphaned children of actors and actresses.</p>
        <p>Alderman John Edward Wright is Warrington's -Mayor for another year. On the proposition of Councillor Smethurst, seconded by Alderman Roberts, he has again been elevated to the highest of municipal honours and has accepted the burden Of onerous duty attached to it. We had had a fleeting thought that perhaps His Worship would decline the honour this year on account of his not 'very robust health, but he has decided to under- take the responsibility once more. It must have needed some courage to do so, and we hope that ' ne will be blessed with all the physical and mental Vigour necessary to make the duties a pleasure. --</p>
        <p>--- The brutal assassin of the Empress of Austria has at last met with his deserts. Lucchini's trial occupied the Swiss tribunal the whole of last Thurs- day, and finally the notorious prisoner was sen- tenced to " reclusion en perpetuite." The meaning of the sentence is perfectly plain. Louis Luccheni is condemned to eternal oblivion, and for the rest of his natural life he must bid adieu to the gay and fascinating world he so horribly ahocked. Judging by his defiant and vain attitude 111 the Court, Lucchint is not as oppressed with t,.110 Solemnity of his doom as we should desire him co be, hut in course of time the monotony of an existence as purposeless as his crime must inflict terrible mental punishment on this ..amurderer of an inoffensive and beloved lady la the first fierce thrill of anger at the assassination of the Empress of Austria 116.71 e were ready to revive the tortures of the ~1!31 dle ages for effective retaliation on Lucchini, a calmer feelin,.,cr has since prevailed. We think Punishment admirably fits the crime. Such a suffian as this warrer against Society would glory '2l the capital sentence—in fact feel it a privilege to. die for his crime—and the effect on accomplices would not be such as we '4 desire. These maniacs would gladly become</p>
