      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>martyrs for the principles of Anarchism if they were ensured a speedy dispatch from the scene of their villany, but they will be very unlikely to view with longing a complete extinction of their individuality coupled with the rigours of per- petual confinement. The murder of Empress Elizabeth had one good result, it con- vinced a number of conspirators of the Luce- hini type, that they are abhorred and detested from the gutter to the crown, while the decision of the Swiss Court, will, we devoutly hope, have a salutary effect on those who still possess a desire to kill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>STRANGE FATALITY AT WIGGPS</head>
        <p>STRANGE FATALITY AT WIGGPS</p>
        <p>WORKS.</p>
        <p>AN UNSUSPECTED DANGER.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Woreley (coroner) held an inquest, at the Runcorn Police Court, on Tuesday morning upon the body of John Lawrenson, aged 42, chemical labourer whd died at his residence, 20, E ;erten-street, on Satur- day evening from the results of injuries sustainej, whilst following his employment at the Wige's Works, Runcorn, on the Wednesday previous. There were present :—Mr F J Norman, J.P. (manager) and Mr Hawkins (Messrs Fawcett and Hawkins. Liverpool), representing the United Alkali Company; Mr F A Lake (Messrs Day and Lake, Runcorn) representing the rela- tives ; and Mr Sidney Eraut of Factories, Liver- pool). Mr Robert Wright, Green way-read, was the fore- man of the jury. Henry Gordon, 19, Elliott-street,Widnee,gave evidence of identification. Deceased was his brother-in-law, a chemical labourer by occupation, and 42 years of age. Mr. William Crowther, 68, Halton-road, Runcorn, stated he was a chemist employed at the United Alkali Works. He was in the works with the de- ceased. and the foreman, Alfred Lyons. It would be about 7 minutes to 2. As they were turning the taps of some pipes used for conveying gases to the condensers, by means of a long key, there was a sudden report and the gas all went out. Witness stood for a few moments with the man Lyons ; bet Lyons called out "Clear out," the steps leading from the room. As sd otthbedh n outecreaasedd by not follow he made id inquiries for him and asked whether he had come down the other way. But he was told that nothing had been seen of him. They went back and found Lawrenson lying on the stage with a piece of plank by his legs. He called another man and together they corned him out. He only said "0, Crowther." Witness helped to carry him to the office and was present when the doctor came. He was also present at the death of deceased which took place on the 12th inst, about 8-30 p.m. Dr. Baschen said .10eves consulting chemist to the United Alkali Company. Ha was stationed at Widnes, and knew the works where the accident happened. processes carried on there, but the There were several particular one concerned in the present case was called the Cyanide process." It was a process for the extrection of gold from ore. It was not extensive. They ascended to a platform by steps which were a con- siderable height from the ground, all railed round. Upon the platform there was a tank called a condenser which was used for the cooling of gases. The tank held water and there were cart tin pipes through which the gas travelled. The gases entered at one Bide of the tank, and left at the other end. The gases travelling through the pipes were a mixture of nitric oxide,hydro- cyanic acid and steam. It was not what was called ex- plosive—that is if a lighted match was put into it, the match would be put out. The gases were conveyed to the tank by two pipes. On tease pipes there were two large taps turned by a key. It was the duty of the deceased to turn one of these keys at certain times to divert the course of the gases. Since that lamentable accident he had investigated the affair. There was a deposit in one of the pipes, and to some extent on the tap. The deroosit was a mixture of carbon and a com- pound caused by the decomposition of the hydrocyanic acid. This in itself was not explosive. When the deceased turned the tap friction was caused with the result that the powder detonated, and the detonation was sufficient to ignite the gases inside the pipe, which ould not ignite by ordinary means. Then followed the explosion of the whole. That kind of plant had been worked in Widnes and Ruacorn for some time. But the particular plant from which the accident resulted had only been worked about three months. He had had a f similar plant under his control at Widnes until that one at Runcora was seered. The one in Widnes had been in existence two ye are. There had never been an acci- dent of that kind at Widaes. There was not within his know:edge, nor had he reed in any book any kind of suggestion that nitric oxide would explode, and this had come as a complete surprise. So far as he knew such a thing was unknown to chemistry. In order to obviate anything of the kind in future, it was intended t ) pre- vent the formation of the power or to make it non- explosive. They would also do away with the iron taps and substitute rubber ones, so that when the taps were worked there would be no friction. They could not pre- vent the powder being deposited in the pipes but they could prevent it being explosive. The danger was alto- gether unsuspected.—By the Coroner: He had been in that profession twelve years. That process was anew one and had net been carried on in the Same way any- where else. The cause of the mishap was the friction set up by moving the tap, generating sufficient heat to detonate the powder and so ignite the gas. Dr. McLennan, Runcorn, said he was called to the deceased on the 9th inst and saw him in the works. Deceased was unconscious then. He was euffering from a wound in the right side of the head. He attended him up to his death which took place on Saturday night. The cause of death was fracture of the skull and lacera- tion of the brain. He had had the assistance of Dr. Mitchell Bankes through the iastigation of the United Alkali Company in attending to the deceased, and the company had done all in their power to assist them, Dr. Mitchell Bankes was one of the most eminent specialists in that part cf the world. Sidney Brant, said he was one of Her Majesty's In- specters of Factories connected with the Liverpool Dis- trict. He had been to the works after the accident and examined what remained of the pipes. The result of his examination led him to coincide with the opinion expressed so clearly by Dr. Beechen. He could state that so far as the Factory Act was concerned that portion of the works where the accident occurred wan entirely satisfactory.—The Coroner : That is what I was de- sirous of ascertaining.—Witness had never heard of any similar aceident,and as far as he knew those gases had always been regarded as safe substances. The Coroner summed up the evidence, which he said clearly pointed to an accident due to unknown and un- suspected causes. There was no blame t 3 attach to the company in any way. Mr. F. A. Lake, on behalf of the deceased's widow said she was perfectly satisfied with the way the com- pany had acted towar is her hesband, and also on her behalf he desired to express thanks is the company for the kindness and consideration shown to her of the pro- mise of work to her sons.</p>
        <p>The Ceroner considered that it was very kind of the company, who ought te be highly commended for their humanity in the case. A verdict of Aclidental death " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <p>THE " LEVIATHAN" PLACARD, Single Sheet 51 t. Sin, by 31t. 9in. THE LARGEST IN THE DISTRICT. A MONSTRE PLACARD may be foamed by joining number of the " LEVILTEtaIi " SHY:ETS. MAMMOTH POSTERS. A SPECIALITY MAMMOTH POSTERS. LARGE TYPE MAMMOTH POSTERS. FOR MAMMOTH POSTERS. THE MOST STRIKING 1 MAEMOTH POSTERS. POSTER I " EXAMINER " OFFICE,</p>
