      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>EXAMINER RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>M. L. W. Jeacock-,w—h-o—re-Cen-ti; left Runcorn after five years' faitaful se:vice as an assistant ITlASter,hl9 re- ceived from the headmaster and assistants a handsome writing desk as a 81113. U. token of their esteem. Mr. Jeaernk has been appointed assistant at the Higher Grade School, Moss Side, Manchester. WHITE GLOVES AT WIDNES POLICE COURT. On Monday morning at Widow Police Jourt, the ,1 Mayor (Alderman J. W. Warein;) was presented with a pair of white gloves by Superintendent Beighley, there being no prisoners for trial, a most unusual occurrence on Menday morning at Widnes. In the course of afe congratulatory remarks, the Mayor, t)gether with Mr. B. Brown, his colleague on the bench, strongly depre- cated the opprobrious reference to the borough at the recent Diocesan Conference. THPI BIRKENHEAD DOCK DISASTER.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday,the remains of the man Thomas Rutter (hand on the Salt Union Flat " Look-out ") who was drowned in the M irpo•.h Dock disaster, at Birkenhead, on Octets !r 17th, were laid Zo rest in the Ellesmere Port Cemetery. It appears that the body was found in an advanced stage of decomposition neat' to Ellesmere Port on Monday last. Toe inquest was ladd the following day, when the neerssary evidence of identifleation was given.—This is ths second body that has been recovered but so far no tidings have arrived of the whereabouts of Arthur Williams, who is still missing. MERSEY MISSI,N TO SEAMEN. On Tuesday night there was a potato pie supper, held at the Mersey Mission, when a fair number of s tilors and their families, who hod been looking forward to the gathering, availed themselves of the opportunity of spending an enjoyable evening. Tables were pres.de over by Mrs. Evans, Mrs. Mock, Mrs. Hughes, and Miss Bowyer. Aft •r• the tables were cleared games and o Thar amusements were indulged in,which contributed m etc- rially to the entert tinment of all. The affair was pro- moted by the mis4ion workers, FREE Luirsteav Commyrriak. On Tuesday night the usual monthly meeting of the Runcorn Free Library Committee was ne'd at the C euti- cil Chamber. There were pres :at M•ssrs D. Bisbrown (chairman) M. L. Davies, T. Riley,and Mr. J. Sweennin. The Librarian (Mr. J. D. Jones) reported that during the month of October the Library was opened 21 days. Volumes were issied os follows :—Theology 26, Philoso phy 5, Science 52, History &amp;. 106, Poetry 11, Fiction 1632, Miscellaneous 172, French Literature 2, Mosm 11, Reference 33, total 2050, being a decrease of 292 in the ending department, and 3, in the reFerence department.</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLLSS. Mr. T. Simiater pre sided over the usual weekly meet- ing of the ab.eve, held at the Brunswick schoolroom. There was a capital attendance. The subjects before the class were two paperettes on " Martin Luther" and " Prejudice." Irde firs", was taken by Mr. R. Cnriitie n and proved quite a masterpiece. Mr. J. Walker dealt with " Pr.ejudice " and was also well received. Amangst those who took part in the debate were :—Messrs, T. Saunders, W. Millinrton, D. Bisbrown, and W. Franci3 Jones. The paper given by Mr. R. Christien excited the highest admiration, and far exceeded any given at the class for some time.</p>
        <p>Tun PICKWICK CLUB. At the uaual weekly meeting of this club there should have been a debate betwesn jr,q membsrs and thsse of the Elleetnera-street Club, on "Vaccination." But ovrlng to the latter class being unable to keep their engage- ment the event had to be indefinitely postponed. The night was occupied by the Pickwickians taking the sub- ject up themselves, and it WAS argued out at tenth. Mr. T. Sauniers led off with a very c-nnprehensive paper. Amongst those who took part were Messrs. W. Millington, G. Haves. R. Banaioa, and J. Booth. The attendance was small. WEsusrese Poi/Eters MISSIONARY SOCIETY. On Sunday and Monday next, the annual sermons and meeting, will bs held, in connection with the Run- corn branch of the above society, at ths St. Paul's Church, High-street. The preacher Major General Campbell late of Her Majesty's forces in India, has been previously before the Runcorn public, some twelve months ago, arid his eloquence on that occasion can be vividly recalled, The Rev. William Burgess of the East Indian Mission Stations) has been out in the mission field of India for 20 years, and his experiences are exceed- ingly well told. The chair will be taken at 7 o'clock by Mr: William Walker, of Bolton. MAYOR'S SUNDAY AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>In accordance wito the annual cut n, the newly. elected M syor, Alderm in Wareing, J.P., attended divine service at St. Paul's Church on Sunday morning. His Worship and the members of the Town Council, School Board, Free Library and Technic sl Instruction Com- mittee' officials, and the Fire Brigade, assembled in tee Town Hall, whence they marched in processional order to the church. They were headed 13; Gossage's Prize Bled. A large number of people witnessed the proceed- idgs, and the sacred edifice was filled. The Mayor read the lessons, and an able sermon was preached by the Vicar, Rev. C. E. Stocks. The offertory, amounting to £lB, was devoted to the Widnes District Nursing Association. At the conclusion of the service the band played the National Anthem and the " Hallelujah Chorus." " ON SHOD AND SEA." .</p>
        <p>This popular cantata, by Sir Arthur Sullivan. will be one of the principal items at the forthcoming concert of the Runcorn Pnilharmonic Secietv, to b 3 given at the Public Hall, Runcorn, on the 23r1 (Wednesday). In addition there will also b 3 rendered the part songs " The Lovely Vale of Streams" and the " Miller's Wooing" not to mention the " Soldiers Chorus" (from Faust). Amongst the artises at.° Miss Alice A. Simons (London), and Mr. Caarles Saunders (of Ashton-under-Lyne), both talented singers. There will be a full orchestra and chorus, which, isnier the manipulation of Dr. C. T. Reynolds (lui. Doc.) and Mr. Maurice Kruger (Ralle's) :who leads the band are certain to reoder a capital ac- count of themselves. For further part mislars see adver- tisement.</p>
        <p>THE RUNCORN AND DISTRICT SHORTHAND WRITERS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>At a general meeting of this Association held at Mr. Riley's Cafe, Bridge-street, on Monday es fling, thi re- port of the Provisional C 'nominee was sunittel. Tne followine; gentlemen signified t lair consent to accept office an 1 their election was unanienously confirmed : President, Mr. S. Taylor (chairman of the Urban District Council), viee.presidents, Messrs. P. Speakman, James Handley, J kat eleadowcroft, John Wilson, F. A. Lake, P. R. Allen, J. E. Meredith, Jos ph Pritchard, and J. F. Moulton. The meeting proceeded to coasider the draft rules s obi-flitted by the Provisional Committee,and these with one or two slight alterations, were adopted. The objects of the society are thus stated in the rul33 "To promote the extended use of the art of phonography by mt,ans of weekly meetings for reporting practice ; to provide for the mutual improvement of the members by lectures, essays, and discussions on shoreha sd and ether topics, and by conductin; an ever-circulator magazine during the vac-it ons ; t maintain a library of b ooks on shorthand,typewriting,comtnercial and general subject; to arrange periodical social gatherings, and to further the isterests of Runcorn shortha od writ irs' gen Tally." The meeting elected the following officers :—Consmittee, Messrs. r. A. Millinger, J. Milline;ton, J. Sirnkin, E. Ward, F. Whalley,l.O. Williams,W. H. Dadley,A. Dodd, J. White,and R. H. Bridges; hon. secretary, C. Tomkins; assist ant secretary, T. Clare ; and hon. treasurer, R. Bennion. A vote of thank; to the Chairman (Mr. J. Millington) brought the meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>The "Pickwickians," it is generally understood, have been successful in their 'Arens, at solving the mysteries of thought reading, which was the object they set out to accomplish last week, and the way they account for it is entirely satisfactory. They assert that the two principle factors are "prepared ground " and " prepared pads." The subject, however,is likely to form the theme for one of their meetings, so that those who desire .to become ac- quainted with the inner workings of the " dodge" would do well to attend the meeting when it is held.</p>
        <p>" Vaccination " seems to be the one subject of comment with the debating element of Runcorn, and on Tuesday there was to have been a set-to bltween the Ellesmere-street Improvement Class and the Pickwich Club. Owing to the loss sustained, however,by the Elle ,mere-street Club in the lament- able death of Mr. Gan dy, it has been indefinitely postponed.</p>
        <p>On Saturday last, at a temperance meeting held at the Congrega•ionil Schoolroom, Sims Cross, Widnes, reference was made to the necessity for Band of Hope work in the town undergoing a con- siderable revival, and several hard things were said about Wi Ines. Yet, notwithstanding all this, it is interesting to note that the Mayor, who presided at the Police Court proceedings on Monday last, was presented with a pair of" white gloves.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Shorthan I Writer's A.,•sociation was successf ally launched upon its career of expected usefulness on Mond. ay evening. As this body is an educational ons it worthy of every support, and from the amount of enirgy displayed by its mem- bers, gives promiss of becoming one of the leading —if not the leadin.; literary society in the town. Its utility cannot be over-rated.</p>
        <p>" Nliss Hazlehurst " has been arrested at Sale, where it appears that she took the inhabitants by storm and distributed orders broadcast. In one instance it is stated that she Was even entertained to dinner y a tradesman who received a £lOO Jrder from her "ladyship.,</p>
        <p>The School Board—What about it? That is the general question. Well, it is no vv offi3ially an- nounced that the election will take place on the 30th of November (if necessary) • that nominations will be received up to 19th ef November, and that the la-t day for withdr.wing will be November 23rd. Both parties—undenotainational and church —are working as if an election was a foregone conclusion, and no compromise seems to be thought of by either party.</p>
        <p>The church section have an organised mode of procedure, and have a recognised committee room, whilst the undenominational section seem to have edged themselves in under a mysterious name—by " ratepayers' association "—but whose policy is not clearly definei. The so met this mark is dropped the batter. Undenotninational supporters waist a clear and above board • definition of p )licy. Bu perhaps the " executive" are only waiting for the 23rd. _ _ _ _ -- _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>VICTIMISING RUNC-JRN TRIDESMEN.</head>
        <p>VICTIMISING RUNC-JRN TRIDESMEN.</p>
        <p>"MISS HAZLEFIURST "_ ARRESTED.</p>
        <p>At the Sate Petty Sessions on DiCa-diTb7to;B. Mesirs. R. H. Joynson (in the chair), E W. Joynson, R. P. Gill, G. Rook°, H. Member, .1. IK )3clall, G. V. li,lvert, &amp;. Lawson, and J. Shaw. A respectable-I-JAM?: young woman named Mary Ed azelhu'st was charged with ob- taining the sum of 14. from Annie'B iresford, of Peel Causeway, by certain false pretences and with intent to defraud.—Prisoner, who was accommodated with a seat fainted during the hearing of th4evidenee.—Annie Beres- ford, single woman, living at Melrose Villa, Peel Cans way, said that last Tnursdey prisouer came to her house. Witness let apirtrnents and pr!soner cam i to see about them. Witness said the rooms were for herself and father. She wanted four i rooms. She offered £2 10s. weekly for the rooms, saying n Warrington she had paid £3. She offered to give a week in advance, and said she had furniture which she would bring up. She was to have the rooms for this week. Before she left shs ilasked witness to let her have a shilling in order that she might go on to Manchester by train and then to War- rington. She represented that she was coming to Peel , causeway again the next day, but witness had not seen priioner since nor received back her shilling which she let prisoner have on the strength of the tale she ',old. Prisoner said her _father was an invalid, and it was on his account they were removing from Warrington.— Superintendent McDonald asked for a remand until Mon- day. de found thi prisoner's father was a labourer re- siding at Dutton, and there were other cases against the young woman.—Prisoaer was remanded until M )(play. when in addition to the above she will be charge I with the offences committed in Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RTJNCOEN PHILHitt NIONIC SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>RTJNCOEN PHILHitt NIONIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>CLOSE OF THE LIAZAAR. On Thursday the chair was taken by Mr. J. R. Frith who briefly introduced Mr. F. J. Norman as a gentleman well-known and respected and greatly in sympathy with the movement. Mr. F. J. Norman said he was pleased to be present to open the bazaar. As far as he could remembsr it was the first bizalr of its kind which had been conducted on undenominatioaal lines. They were all aware of the appreciable results that arise out of bazaars whee there was any religious cause to forward. He hoped that should there ever be a need for fu.ther- ing any Cause of their city fathers, and when it was found that a penny rate and grant from the County Council Was somewhat inadequate, he hoped they would take into consideration the benefit to be derived from a good bazair;supported by the whole of the townspeople. He understood that Mr. Taylor had in his remarks the previous day referred to:the whole history of the Musical Societies of Runcorn. He was glad of that. Personally whilst he thought there was not room for two societies—b ith a tonic and Musical &amp;ciefy—yet he felt there was great need for a Pnilharmon'c Society. (Hear heir). He was pleased to see that the people of Runaora were determined to co Akin° their Musical Society, and not to let it go down. Ho ws.s pleased to be present at the bazaar because it was in supperf of the Philhar- monic Society. (Hear, hear). He had pleasure in declaring it open..</p>
        <p>Mr. Wesley ooke proposed, and Mr. William Booth seconded a hearty vote of thanks te Mr. Norman. The vote was accorded with acclamation, aril Mr. Norman, in responlng, said hs hoped their appreciation of himself would be shown in a practical way, by pur- chasinr, the articles displayed. The sale was then proceeded with. On ne fir3t day the proceeds realised £56, and oa Toqrsday tee total wag raised to a I,ttls over £lOO. Mr. Samuel Taylor and Mr. F. J. Norman each con- tributed £5.</p>
        <p>Thus the venture Iris proved highly successful, aad the debt upon the Society which amounted to a little over £6O will be wiped off.</p>
        <p>RE-OPENING. OF THE Ciricus. The Q leen-street circus will be re-opened for one week only, commencing on Nlonday next, when Messrs. Livermore Br3s: miniature Italian circus will make it first appearance in the town. The circus comes direct from the Zoological Gardens, Vienna, and Messrs. Livermore Bros., with com- mendable enterprise, have engaged Signor Volpe and his splendid traupe at a salary of £250 per week. There will be performances by over one hundred trained animals as bare back riders, clown dogs, clown monkeys, and performing ponies and goats. One special Item on the programme will be ' a military trial by eight monkey judges, in which the prisoner is found guilty and condemned to be shot. An Italian brass band will be in attendance. In addition to the evening performances there will be two additional ones .on Thursday and Saturday afternoons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>ACCIDENT AT EASTHAIN LOCKS.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT EASTHAIN LOCKS.</p>
        <p>About nine o'clock on Monday night as the steamer " Mitigate" of Manchester was entering the Easthana Locks, the mate Fred Wittle, (of Milford Haven) was thrown violently against the wheel and sustained severe injuries. At first as the man was in an unconscious state it was thought tiat he would not survive and in- formation was telephoned to Captain Heaseley (super. intendent of the ship Canal), to have a doctor in attend. ance at the Pier Head. Accordingly C sptain Heaceley accompanied by Dr J B Delaney and Mr Shaw (mission- ary) boarded the vessel and a careful examination was made of the injured man. Then came the difficulty of where to remove the man as the extent of his injuries in the doctor's opinion necessitated his instant removal on land, but as there was no hospital accommodation of any kind Mr. Shaw kindly offered to find &amp;letter for the man at his own home, to where he was removed. This accident again illustrates the necsssity of forwarding the erection of a cottage hospital for Runcorn sad neighbourhood in as expeditious a manner as possible.</p>
        <p>SCALDING FATALITY AT RUNCORN. At the Runcorn Police Court Buildines, on Tuesday morning, Mr. J. E. Woesley (coroner) held an inqueet upon the body of Hilda Whitby. a little child who died on Monday morning *OM the effects of scalds sustained on the previous elturday afternoon.—Mrs. Ellen Whitby, stated that she was the wife of John Whitby, Greek-street, Runcorn, and mother of the &amp;clued, who was 2 years and 3 months old. On Saturday afternoon she had been washing the deceased, and dressed the girl who went into the yard. Witcess then took ROM 9 bail- ing water and poured it into a mug which she left in the yard whilst she went into the back kitchen to re-fill the kettle. Se heard the deceased seream, and on going into the yard she found the child sitting in the mug of hot water. The child had a very good night on Saturday night, and let her father nurse her all the time, 'On the Sanday she took a change and had a fit. She sent for the doctor. There was na one in the ysrd at the ' time except th 3 child.—Dr. Andrew McLennan, of Run- corn, said he was calle iin to see the child on the S in- day. He found her in convulsions more or less all the time up to her deeth on the ,ionday morning. He was of opinion that death was due to convulsions caused by the injuries sustained from the scalds.-21 verdict in accordance with the medical evidence was returned.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION. A meeting of the members of this Association was held in Victoria-road Board School on S iturday evening. Mr. B. Crosby, of Garston, the president, presided over a good attendance. After the ordinary business ,had been transacted, Mr. Albert Croft, of N ittiogham, a member of the Executive Committee of the National Union of Teachers, delivered an ad- dress on the subject of " The next step. After Mr. Croft's address, the company, numbering between 40 and 50, sat down to tea. Toe tables .were arranged with excellent taste, reflecting great credit on the ladies who had undertaken this part of the after- noon's programme. During tea, vot,s of thanks were passed to the Runcorn School Board for their kindness in granting the use of the rooms, and to Miss Kilburn and her ce-workers for their efforts in making such ample provision for the inner man. After 630 an adjournment was made to the large new room, where the time was happily. spent in social diversion, consitt- ing of singing, games, and dancing. The Misses Kul- burn, Stubbs and Watts, and Messrs. W. C. Cooke, B. ICrosby, and J. Brown contributed to the evening's enjoy- ment. The proceedings terminated at 10 30, the com- pany having spent a veryinstructive and enjoyable time.. —9t the afternoon meeting the following recommenda- tions of the committee were unanimously adopted :-1) That the following candidates be nominated for (a) the executive for the North Mid and District, Miss Selvage, Ds, Beach, and Mr. A Croft ; (b) Vice-oresident, Mr. J. C. Ilorobin ; (c) treasurer, Mr. Hamilton. (2) On the proposition of Mr.. T. Phillips, seconded by Mr. J. F. Moulten, it was unanimously decided to invite the Ches- biro District Union to hell its annual public moeting next yeas in Runcorn ; also that the Council of the Dis- trict I.lciioA be asked to send a deputation to address the local association at its nest meeting on District Union matters.</p>
        <p>TOBA.OOONISTS Commencing.—See Illustrated Guide (159 pages) 3d.. Tobacconists Outfitting 00.,186,13u5t0n-road, London. Hairdressers fitted.—Manager, H. MYERS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 18, 1898</head>
        <p>FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 18, 1898</p>
        <p>BA.ND OF HOPE WORKERS' CONFER- ENCE AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ENTHUSIASTIC TEMPERANCE MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening, at the Milton Congregational Chapel, Sims Cross, Widnes, there was an important confer -rice of B ,ne of Hme arid Temperance workers, Sunday school teachers, etc., held under the auspices of the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope and Tem- perance Union. The chair was taken by Mr. Jacob Earnshaw (chairman of the Union), and there were also present :—Revs M H Chapinan,J El Hughes, H W Pates, J Pedlar, J T Ttionaason, J Johnson (Sale),A J Bamford I (Royton) E T Reed (Warrin et ne),J C Thomas (Hovlake), Councillors G Davies, S Owen, E Barton (Heaton , Caapal), Messrs C E Bingham, D Lewis, J Maier, T S Swale, Evan Thomas, T Baldwin (Colne), R Cooper (Southport), G T Higson (Bolton), E Lloyd Jones (War- , ringten), J II Livesey (Liverpool),T E Hallsworth(Maa- chester),J B Jones(Hoylake),Berresford Adams (Chester), , J W Lees (hon secretary Macclesfield Band of Hope), W R Thomas (local convener) and W Chandos Wilson (counties secretary), eta., etc. The proceedings were preceded by an excellent tea to • which all who atsended the meeting nad been generously ; invited by Mr. W. Vickers (of the United Alkali Corn- , pany.) The Chairman (Mr; Jacob Earnshaw) stated that they ' were gathered together that night by the kind invita- tion of ear. W. Vickers for the purpose of meeting the : I Executive Comm,ttee of the Lancashire and Cheshire Counties Band of Hope Temperance Union. He must ; apologise for Mr. Vickers absence,as he had been obliged ,to go out of town, but Mr. Vickers had asked him (on I his behalf) to give them all a hearty welcome. (Hear, , bear). He might say that when Mr. Vickers was asked to be their host he consented without a moment's hesita- tion. (Applause.) The Rev. W. U. Pates proposed a hearty vote of I thanks to Mr. Vickers for so kindly entertaining them. It was very pleasing to see so large an employer of , labour taking an interest in the moral and spiritual well- fare of the town. (Applause.) Rev. H. M. Chapman said it fell to his lot to second the vote, and he did se with very great pleasure. (Applause). Mr. T. Hallesworth Supported, and the vote was , accorded with acclamation. The tables having been cleared and the opening hymn " Soldiers of Christ arise "having been sung The Chairman read letters of apology from :—Rev. Canon Hicks' M.A., (President of the Union) ; Rev. F. J. Thomas, the Mayor of Accrington, Mr. George Lewis (Preston), Councillor To wnend (Middleton) Rev. Le Bert, M.A., Rev. J. Adams M.A.., and many local triends. The Chairman said though he was down loran address I he would not inflict one upon them ef any length as , the hour was getting late. Be could speak of Widnes I authoritatively he had been in and out ot it for a period , of 30 years. Be knew on his own authcirity that diem was no town that needed the temperance cause more : than Widnes. 1:13 had been reading in the Manchester papers a description of Widnes by a clergyman but it : was not his (the speaker's) opinion. (Hear, hear!) Yet in some things the clergyman was right. They were , not what they might be in Widnes. (Hear, hear.) It , was for teat reason and to stimulate the work of tern- ; perance by the formation of a Band of Hope Uaioe in 1 vVidnes—that they were present that night, and he I would point out cave the way Co attain that object was Co train boys and girls to become total abstainers, and as they grew up the whole town would become improved. (Applause.) He had pleasure in calling upon Mr. J. W. Lees (Macclesfield) to read a paper on " United Temeer- ance Work " ore' What fa Band of Hope Uuion can do for a Town," which would be followed by open discus- sion. , Mr. J. W. Lees, in his paper dealt with the beneficial results that were to be obtained from combined action. ,In the first place if anything of the nature ot reeical improvement was to be effected in the driuk.ng habits of the community it could only c,me from coin weed temperante efforts. It must be admitted that naturally a large and organised body could cope with a reform better than units who were disorganised. As badly,- duals, they were small and puny creat,ues, out they became a great power when unted. It would be his (the speaker's) province to state what might be done for a town through the aid and instrumentality of a Band of Hope Union. They should use the whole of the talents that God had given mein, and associate themselves with others so as to still further extend their influence and power for good. Had they ever censedered tee fact, and if they had did not they righteously revolt lat the thought, that over £150,000,000 was annually spent in drink, and in temporal rine moral depravation consequent thereon. One hun died and twenty thousand subjects diced prematurely from the result Of the drink traffic either directly or indirectly. The workhouses, asylums and jails of the country were largely tided with the victims et the drinking class, not to speak of the lives of toe thousands ot boys and girls met were cacti- flood through the drunken habits ot parents. As Caris- tiaa men and women th y could not fall to be filled with the determination that suca a curse should b C banish ei from their midst. (Hear, hear.) It was by combination —that was the means by which they were to fight against that work. ic 'was necessary, there- fore, that there should be a centre of sesiemeon Hope warkers. They hid largely co deal fworithßanhd offhiold There was no other way of reforming babes of a nation, pr oda :dee of so much training of boys and girls of a nation to the aide:rei drinking n tgh e d eneerscaud the truth. (Hear, hear.) Public men and bodies were slowly m eyed to reformation. some steps taken t prevent the sale hear.) Most of the bane- oAfirderaidnyktthoerhehwilderreen. (Hear. temperance Cause were to come the e and it was gratifying to know the- chinildtrheen, so many people weregh s titl eigianing to recognise that, and ntha through ur e were, for the sake of the children, doing much to forward the cause of the Band of Hope. Th ere were many difficulties and temptations to contend with. Large brewers comblned—eley had their associations and combined actions and th ay(tile temperance oarty)w ould also have to combine if thee' were to achieve any active good. Locally they would hive to excite the sympathies of the temperance workers 0f the district. To get people to take an interest in the distrioution of literature, the superintendency of the children, etc., which could al- be done if twe or three people could be induced to sacril fice a little time. They should avoid anything politica or denominational in their society. Steer clear of coo tarianism and party bias and in the Interest of the child ren they would be obliged to sacrifice for the time their denominational and political opinions It would be difficult for him to tell all that could be dose for a town like Widnes he the formation of a Band of Hope Union. But much could be Accom- plished by steady work. Widnes in the past ha I not been in the forefront with regard to temperance work, and there was much to be ac complish ed for the cause in Widnes, but he hoped that in the future, and as a result of that night's gathering that the Widnes friend; would make a geed effort for the cause. (Hear, hear, and applause). Then followed details of procedure wits re- gard to temperance reform. The Rev. H. W. Pates (Widnes), opened the dis cos- sion. He was pleased with the meeting, and looked upon it as a happy argury. Temperance work was two-40'd— legislatIve and instructive. In the temperance work of this country^ there was a danger that the legislative work would over-balance the instructive portion. Their ,lork was, clearly, then to put the instructive part of the work up on a better footing, wad lay in the training of boys, girls, young men, and women to become total I abstainers. (Applause). In Widnes, that work in the ' past had been allowed to become neglected, so that , something would have to be done to recaeet the lost ' ground. Be heped they would consider it we, desirable ' to form a Band of Hope Unfoe fle Widn-is. (Applause). , Mr. E. Lloyd Jones (Warringten), spoke of the benifi- cial results to be obtained by such an aiiocia,tion. utility was manifest. They bad an a-sociation in War- rington that was doing wonderful wevir. He, felt that if the work was carried out on practical lines and in accor- dance with the outlines detailed in Mr. Lee's paper they would have ice Widnes an association that would mak e , an impression throughout the town. (Applause.) Mr. R. ie. Potts (Newton Heath, Manchester), stated that when on the way to Widaes that afternoon, whilst , in the train, he read a description of Widnes by a clergy- ' man, and the description was not a favourable one. (Langhter.) Widnes was described as a " Hell upon I , earth.' It was clear that Widnes had a bad character ; I and they cOali not get a worse. Well all he would say was" form your 'Union ' and get to work" and they would soon begin to establish a better character. (a.p-</p>
        <p>planes.) Mr. T. S. Swale (Widnes) thought it was time to put in a word of explanation. There was not a single place of worship in Widoes that did not have its Band of Hopi. And, further, he did not think there was another town of the same calibre with respect to trade that had s many teetotalers. (Hear, bear.) They were not all wealtlings—indeed, some ef them were veterans—in the temperacce cause. (Applause.) He would admit, how- ear, r, the.; in the last 10 or 20 years Widnes had los: ground. He thought that it was due to too much en- tertainment, or tan much football—though people were apt to blame that for a great deal it had nothing to do with. Another reason for the falling off in Band of fore work was that the Bands of Hope had degenerated into" wishy washy affairs "—(laughter)—that were not worthy of the name. (Hear hear, and applause.) Of course, if the Band of Hope Union would be any use in joining the sticks togeth3r and making a good fagot i would be some good. But he thought it was like beginning to build a house at the chimney. (Laughter) The first step is his idea was to put the exist ing insti- tutions into order, for really they were at present enough to make one down right sick. (Laughter and hear, hear.) Another thing that accounted for the apathy in tha cause was the fact that certain people whilst professing to b 3 in sympathy with the move- ment always had beer harries calling at their front doors ; he considered that it those people who had stomachs like Timothy would do something to get thorn to call at the back door—(increased laughter)—that in- fluencl would be disposed of. Ho was in sympathy with the movement and wished it success. (Hear, bear, and applause.) Mr. J. Cassidy (Lsvenshulme), Mr, J. T. Harden (Failsworth), Mr. T.E. Haltsworth (bon, secretary of the</p>
        <p>Lancashire and Cheshire Union), Mr. Chandos Wilson (secretary of the counties union) also spoke. Mr. Eernshaw having to depart by an early train vacated tie chair,which was occupied for the remainder of the meeting by Councillor S. Owen (Widnes). On taking the chair, Mr. Owen said he did so with pleasure, as an avowal of the cause ref temperance. He questi med whether anyone could ever have doubted his attitude on the questioe. He would take that oppor- tunity and testily that his friend Councillor Davies and him-elf always drunk the health of the Mayor in water or soda-water and never departed from that principle. (Applause). Mr. D. Lewis (Widnes) said it was now time to come to the actuel business of the meeting. It would be a pity if that splendid gathering was to end in talk. With regard to the remarks about Widnes, he took that oppor- tunity of makieg a deliberate protest. He was in a position to know something about Widnes and had lived there almost all his life, with the result that be was knit towards it with greater affection than ever. (Applause). ' He rose to propose the following resolution : That for the better promotion of Band of Hope work in Widnes and district, we form a Widnes District Band of ipe Union, in connection with the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union." (Applause.) Thera could b, no possible objection to the resolution, which he hoped would be folio will up by vigorous and systema- tic work. (Applause). Councillor G. Davies said that he rose to second the resolution. They in Widnes were not half as black as painted. They were not going to be "black-balled" and called " Hell upon earth " for nothing. He resented it. (Hear, hear.) They had in Widnes as Rood institu- tions for the moral benefiting of the young as anywhere e'se. Their death rate was net as bad as that of some towns, anyhow it was as good as Manchester and better then Salford. (Hear. hear.) Yet, they were not to run away with the idea that they were perfect or even as they ought to be. Far from it, there was plenty of room for improvement. He seconded the resolution with pleasure. (Applause.) Several ladies and gentlemen, including Mrs. Jones, Mr. Swale, and Councillor Seddon, having further ex- pressed their approval of the proposition it was put an unanimously carried. Subsevently a provisional committee was appointed to give effect to the resolution in its details. Votes of thanks to the Chairman for presiding, and to the Lecturer (Mr. J. W. Lees) brought a most enthusi- astic meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON THE RUNCORN POLICE.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court,on Friday morning,before Messrs. F.J.Norman and Samuel Taylor,Tuomas Cannon, proprietor of the Ellesmere-street Laundry, was brought up in custody to answer to four charges, viz., being drunk on licensed premises, to wit, the Commercial Rotel, Ellesmere-streetwand refusing to quit; with unlaw- fully assaulting Edward Charles Hicks, police inspector; with unlawtully assaulting William Cooper, police sergPant ; and with assaulting John Upton, police con- sable, which offences were committed on the previous day whilst the officers were in the execution of their duty.—Priseeer pleaded not guilty.—lnspector Hicks said that about 2.30 in the afternoon, he received infer- matien from a gentleman that there was a tremendous row at the Commercial Hotel, better known as the " Glass darrel," Ellesmere-street, and accordingly went there in company with Sergeant Cooper. He found the prisoner being held down by Mr. Holmes (the land- lord),his barman, and another man; the three of them had prisoner on the floor. He asked what was the matter, and Mr. Holmes said "This man is mad." He sa'd "Come, COffls TOM" for the man was well known to him. "this sort of thing won't do; this is not the sort of work to carry on in a public house." He replied that it was "the other b— who were doing it." Mrelolmes explainei that the man had been having a row with his ' wife,and that they cull not get him to leave the house. Witness then said " now Tern veil know me pretty well, land you will get fair play from me, now come out of the house like a god fellow." The men who were hold- ing toe prisoner then let him get up. Cannon would not come oueof the house,so ar.t.ees bid him that if he would not ge they would be forced to take him out. Prisoner replied " Neither you nor the whole of the men in Run- corn can take me out." Re struck at them and became very 'violent. Witness eel the Sergeant thee closed vette him end had great diffieulty in getting him under coerce. Re (witness) was kicked very violently in the ribs and in other parts of the body, and his hand was so much hurt that he bad to get it bathed when he got hnir that nieht. He also kicked the Sergeant. Con- stable Upton had been the m ost hurt, however, as the prisoner struck him with the handcuffs whilst in the Police Station. When Upton arrived on the scene they were able to handcuff prisoner and conduct hi:n to the Poiice Stagers. It took three men to hold him, and the prisoner would not allow anyone to search him so that he had to be held b; three of the whilst P.S. Gill searched him. Ho was charged and after wards conveyed to the cells. Mr. William Holmes, the landlord of the Commercial Hatel, said that the prisoner was his next door neighbour. He had been in the house previously. About two o'clock witness was hiveog hi a dinner and heard a row. He ran do wnstairs and found the primer in the bar-parlour having a row with his wife. Prisoner had a stool uplifted and was going to strike his wife. Witness was in time to divert the blow, whereupon the prisoner turned upon witless. He managed to avoid the bow. Then assisted by the barman and another marl, they managed to hold the prisoner until the police came, when all as stated by affr. Hicks took place.—Cross-examined : Witness could not see anyone struck whilst in the house. On the last oecas'en prisoner entered the house he was not served with any drink.—Sergeant William Ceoper corroborated. Ile was kicked several thane and was sore about the bady.—P.C. Upton said that when he entered the house he found the Inspector mei Sergeant struggling with a inan. He had been severely injured by the prisoner who brought thu handcuffs down upon his wrist with great force whilst he was being searched. He had been at- tend 3,1 to by Dr. McDougall and the Dr. had told him to call again in the rnerding as at present he was unable to tell the real extent of the damage. He had suffered a great deal of pain during the night. He corroborated the statements of the previous witnesses as to what occurred altar be arrived on the scene.—Prisoner who i elected to give evidence stated all ha bad to say was n regard to the treatment he had received after being taken to the Police Station. When there Inspector Hicks said "Nw boys put your foot into him." And when his overcoat was sent in to hian,so.neone threw it to him saying; e"Here's your b—h— coat and if I hear any more of your b-- le-- noise from you to-night I will knock your b-- h-- head for you with this staff" and the officer who said it flourished his staff.— Cross-examined: He could net identify the officer as it was dark. The woman who lived with him was his wife and he had been married to her.—Police Sergeant Gill stated that he was on duty at the police station when the prisoner was brought in. He did not hear Ina specter Hicks or any of the officer 3 use any such expres- sions as allegel.—Superintendent Okell said that was the cage. He hoped the Bench would make an example of , him as the Police Coast able (Upton)had been so severely handled that he would net be able to resume duty for at I least another month, —After a retirement, the Chairman said that it was the old story of getting mad drunk and forgetting all ahreut it. As had been so often said by the Bench, be woul i now repeat, that they was determined to give their support to the police whilst in the execution of their duty. the prisoner would be fined 20s. and 103. ! costs for being drunk and refusing to quit ; for assault on Inspector Ef,cks, he would be sentenced to one month's imprisonment ; for the assault on Sergeant Cooper, 14 days, and for the assault upon Constable Upton, which they considered was very se- vere, he would have to go to prison for two months, the sentences to run consecutively.—After the decision of tee Bench, the wife ot the prisoner made an emotional appeal that her husband, who had never bees in gaol before should be let off with a heavy fine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WIDNES FARMER COMMITTED TO THE ASSIZES.</head>
        <p>A WIDNES FARMER COMMITTED TO THE ASSIZES.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED MENACES.</p>
        <p>At Widnes Petty Sessions on Thursday, before the Mayor and Mr. Ben Brows!, Peter Hodgkinson, a tom I firmer, was Summoned at the instance of Thomas Cook- son for demanding money from him by menaces on the 20% ult. Mr. Riley (St. Helens), appeared for the de- fendant, and Cookson was represented by Mr. J. W. Koowles.—Cornplainant, who is a toolmaker,residing at Upton, said that on the 20t1s nit, he went into Mr. Potter's field, which is directly opposite his (complain- ant's) house, to look after the sheep, taking his gun with him. He was E ccompanied by two men. named Robert Bill and George Case, and whilst in the field the latter drew his attention to a cock pheasant in the grass, and he shot and killed it. Defend- ant then drove up, and asked witness what he had shot. He also said that witness had no gun license, and threatened to report it. Defendant said he would say nething of the matter if witness would give him £3, but afterwards reduced it to and the gun, and complain- ant, who was afrai3 of exposure, agreed to pay the money and hand ever the gun. At the conclusion of complainant's evidence thecourt adjourned for luncheon. Prison-r was afterwards committed to the Liverpool Assizes.</p>
      </div>
