      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A_ Sr. HEIENS CANAL MY.STEI3.E.</p>
        <p>- The body of Thomas Gilligan, aged twenty-four, a labourer at Bibby's Copper VI orks, WAS recovered from the St. Helens Canal ea W,drie;.day morning under rernarkable circumstances. Gilligan lived at 52, Canal Bank West, at whicn place tie housei front to tbe canal. On Sunday he was spending the evening at 48, where, it is stated, his sweetheart livd. The police report states that at half-pa t two on Monday morning Gilligan left 48 to go to his lodgings neat door but one. Be was then u ider the infiaeacs it think. The young woman went aLer him to see tnat he got home safely, and she states that she saw him sitting on a chair in the house whera he lodged. A short time afterwards she heard someone shouting for help, and went to the door of the house, but could not see anything, and went to bed. Next morning it was found that Gilligan had dis- appeared. Tile police dragged the canal on Monday, but did not find any trace of Gilligan. As he still failed to put in an appearance, his friends and the police resumed the search early on Wednesday morning. In the presence of a crowd of onlookers, and at half-paSt too the body was recovered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>JUNIORS' ESSAY COMPE CITION.</p>
        <p>In furtherance of educational priaciples, and to stimu- late the young mind in th 3 cause of co-operation, the above Society a few weeks ago invited competition from children of members, in the writing of an essay on " What is the difference bctween a Co-operative Store and a private shop ?" There was a fair number of coat- petitcr3 and some of the essay were remarkable pro- ductions. The essays were adjudicated upon by the Committee of the Society, and the priz is were awarded las follows :-Ist, 7s. 6 1., Henry Judson, age 12 ; 2nd, 53., Louie Rawiings (13) ; 3rd, 3s. 63., George Fail (13) I 4th. 23. 61.. Thomas Morris (12) ; special prize is. Thomas,A. Clare (10) ; special prize is., /Nellie Garrett (14). MO following is the winning essay :—" A Co-oosra- tive Store belongs to the persons who subscribe the capital necessary to build or buy premises wherein the business is conducted, and to purchase the necessary goods to supply the members with. Therefore a Co-operative Store means the greatest good to the greatest number. The business of a private shop is owned by the person who carries on the business. There- fore it benefits one individual telly. The profits on geode sold in a private shep after paying wages, rent, rates, &amp;c., go into the pocket of the person owning the business. The profits mad 3 at our stores are used in a variety of ways such as the payment of a fair wage to all persons employed by tbe Society ' • interest on the capital subscribed by the members; depreciation of buildings and fixtures; a dividend of so much in the pound on all goods purchased by members at the stores. Each member's share of the profits is dwernaine 1 by the amount of checks presented at the quarters end. A reserve fund is also formed fr orn the profits of the society which is spent in many useful ways, giving money to help ties lock-d-out engineers ; colliers ; quarrymen ; for prizes at the Technical Institution Classes ; also for the writing of these essays, and the entertainment for members' children. Cottages have also been built out of the accumulated profits and deposits of the membera and sold to them. They le(c ene their own et the end of a few years on payment of a weekly sum, which is very little more Ulan an ordinary rent. It is to their benefit because a well-conducted Store either produces the article sold as much as peseible on their own premises, or purchases them from Co-operative Productive Societies, who employ their oem members and share their profits between producer and coosumer, and thus secure the middleman's profits which justly belong to them, and by so doing reduce their working expenses and increase their profits, therefore, it must be better than geing to a private shop." On Saturday night an eatertainmene, free to the children of members, was given in the Co-operative Flalewhich,as might be expected.was crowded. Mr. D. erre-ter, president of the society, was chairman, and addressing the children spoke chiefly on Co-operative matters. lie said that entertainment had been arranged for the amusement ani instructi me of the children and he asked them to remain quiet and learn something of an educational character whieh they did not learn at school. A musical programme was afterwards submitted the following contributed social items, Master Moran,Master Rigby, and Mr. J. Hu Idleeton. Miss Hughes gave a recitation. Mies Mee presidei at the piano. The chairman afterwards distributed the prizes to the winners in the essay competition. The entertainment cencluied with an illustrated lecture eetiVe "Co-operation and Jenny Jago's dream." Mr. W. Clare gave the lecture and the lant-ra was worked by Mr. Lawson, who afterwards amused the children with some humorous pictures. As each child left the hall, it was presented with a bag of sweets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>LATE REV. T. E. ROBERTS.</head>
        <p>THE LATE REV. T. E. ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>MEMORIAL SERVICE.</p>
        <p>An in memoriam service in connection with the death of the Rev. T. E. Roberts of Earlestown, particulars of which we gave last week, were held in the Wesleyan ' Chapel, E irle3town, on Suneay evening. There was a large congregation all the relatives of the deceased being present. Tee pulpit was draped in black. Mr. F. P. Clare sang " Then shall the righteous, " and the ,Ispecial hymn for th 3 occasion was " Peace perfect peace" At the close of the service Miss Greenall,organ- ist, played " The Dead Merch." Mr. W. Robinson, of Sutton, who occepied the pulpit,preached from the 23rd Proverbs, part of the 26th versa son give me thy heart." In the course of his sermon ho referred to the death of the Rev. T. E. Robvte, whose early and unex- pected death, he said, painfully reminded thorn that they also might meet an early end. Their deceased broth-r was born in Earlestewn on May 11th, 1869, and passed threueh their own Suerlay school frem toe lowest to the highest class. Weide there he was brought under the influence of Godly teachers, some of whom had welcomed him on the other side. Their efforts, together with his good home training led bins early in life to give his hoart to God. Thislwas not the result of any passing wave of religious ferveur. He had a strong conviction that all who owned Christ as Master and Lord ought publicly to declare the same, and in obedience to that conviction, ween the late Mr. Charlesworth conducted his mission hers he earns and knelt at the Communion rail, not t I seek the Lerd, but rather to declare with tha Akfulnegs that he had already found it to his soul's groat joy. At that time he felt an earnest desire for active work, and he commenced to labour in the Sunday school where so many had begun. This led him to take a deep interest in children which he manifested by obtaining subseriptions to enable him, with other helpers, to give the poor children of their district a free breakfast on the birth-day of the chil- dren's friend, and this he dii on two successive years. In the summer of each year he spent his holidays at Lytham, devoting most of his time in conduetang ser- vices for the children on the sands. He, the preacher, could bear personal testimony as to how earnest he was in these services, for in conversations he had with him after his return from Lrtham, his whole soul seemed to shine in his eyes as he described the rich enjoyment he bad experieaced in these seaside services for children. At the age of 18 ho became a local preacher on their own plan, and che love so grew upon him that he felt constrained to preach the glorious gospel of the blessed God. Six years ago he went to Newfoundland and took charge of the circuit at Clarkes Sewell. He had not been there very long bAore he discovemi teat a great evil had long been operating against the spiritual and temporal wel- fare of the people, namely, the practices of fishing on the Sunday. Fishing was the estaple industry of the piece and the fishermen who followed teeir occupation on the Lord's day could not attend divine Worship. There were many also who did attend the church who had a vested interest in the fishing beats, and to them Sunday cessation of their work would mean financial loss. Re- cognising all this, and probably resulting in the with- drawal of seine of his workers, he nevertheless COrn. menced a public crusade apainst Pe and by public preaching, and by all po3sible means, he endeavoured to step the practice. This, as he had foreseen, roused the the opposition of interested partiee,but in spite of all he persevered, and finally had the satisfaction of seeing the abolition of Sunday fishing in his district. He also set his face against Shebeening whieh was prevalent at that time, and by his eilorts he materi- ally reduced the evil. After three years probation, amid scenes and labours of this kind, he proceeded to the University of Montreal in Canada, where he studied hard for two years. During his time there he was invited to take charge of a sorvice in a small town in Kansas. He accepted the invitation, and to his great surprise at the end of the time he was unanimously invited to become its regular pastor, and though the change had every appearance of being desirable from a worldly standpoint, he refused the invitation, preferring to remain true to the church of his fathers. He returned to Montreal to complete his studies preparatory to ordination by the Newfoundland Conference. He was ordained by that Conference in the early part of 1897, and he came home to England in August of that year and married Miss S. J. Alderson, thus consummating the hopes of many years. After a brief stay in England Mr. and Mrs. Roberts with many affectionate wishes and earnest prayers following them, returned from the scenes of their childhood to nooke their new home in the far-off town of Bonne Bay on the shores of Newfound- land, there to bear each ether's burdens and be a mutual help in Christian work. Suddenly his family thfoer the young familyw were plunged in deepest sorrow when the newa web riot,r) for God had taken bin'. for y came last Sunday that their son W4B id co s feow in her ment and loneliness in th-lt far-off land. marriage,thaalwleifedirulbbebarrearttfelbvee t prayers went up that the God of all power might strengthen their hearts, and in the midst of their grief give them a peace that passeth under- standing,</p>
        <p>Ntoff RAPID CIIOLBS.—GARLAND, 51, Brlcl2e.-atrea. Warrington</p>
      </div>
