      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>THE BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>It is seldom that any discovery .Jf science is Sl.leil as will benefit all classes of people. Some of the greatest discoveries of the age, while they have proved directly beneficial te thousands, have also, at least for a time, injured other thousands. Eren some of the best medi- cinal preparations, however much they may do to re- move one class of disease, or suit some constitutions, at the came time either deepen the hold of another disease, or leave it ectirdv untouched, or if they benefit one class of patients, they may injure another. Now the great need of the age is some scientifically arranged preparation whzch will cope effectually with the preva- lent diseases of this country, which wilbe certain to d good when fairly tried,which will be equally adapted to the needs of the merchant and the workman, the pro- fessional man, and he who wins his bread by the sweat of his brow, the student, the clerk, the factory hand ,the , miner, and the roadside labourer.lt should be, too, such a preparation as contains no injurious ingredients, and which may be taken with impunity by the weakly child, or the delicate lady, as well as by the stronger constitu- ted man. Such a discovery would deserve to be called The Perfection of Medicinal i'reparations, and w mid indeed be an Invaluable Boon tc. Suffering Humanity. Now thi3 much needed 30011 has been found, has been tried, has been proved, and wherever proved,it has been found satisfactory. We refer t 3 Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic, which when once tried,has been always recommended, and has proved successful when all other medicine has failed to give relief, and we may say further, that it has proved permanently benefi cial, when other preparations at best only gave tempor- ary relief. It is strongly recommended as The Best Remedy of the Age for Indiges:ion in its different forms, such as Sick Headache,Pains in the Side, Giddiness,Loss of Appetite,also for Nervousness and Nervous Disorders, Sleeplessness, Neuralgia, Low Spirits, and all kinds of Weakness. It has often proved very beneficial to persons suffering from great Weakness, either after an Illness, long confinement to ill-ventilated rooms, or any other cause. It strikes at the source of the Disease, removing the c kuse of the illness, and strengthens those parts of the system which have been weakened by it, and therefore most liable to colds and other ailments. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters being purely vegetable, it is a Natural Tonic, and suited to all ages at all seasons of the year. It is everywhere acknowledged to be a powerful aid in restoring the body to a healthy state when deranged by disease.lf you s :sped that your health is beginnine to fail, brae* up your nerves, and fortify your constitution by taking now a course of this excel- lent Tonic, which is sold everywhere in bottles at 24.91 and 45.6 d. each,but should any difficulty be experienced in procuring it, the Proprietors will forward same for the above prices, carriage free. Avoid Imitations. When purchasing, see the name, " Gwilym Evans," on Label, Stamp, and Bottle. Sole proprietor; : Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>RETURNING FRODI THE RACES. At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday before the Mayor and ether magistrates, Robert Ander- son, described as a groom, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly at the Central Station, at half-past eleven on Saturday night, and further with travelling on the Cheshire Lines Railway between Liverpool and Warrington, without paying his fare, and with intent to avoid peyment. —Prisoner said he left his ticket in an overcoat in the railway carriage. —Detective Inspector Winters, who prosecuted on behalf of the company, said that on the arrival of the 10.45 train tram Liverpool to Warrington, a porter went to coll ect the tickets. On cpening an ap- parently empty compartment he saw defendant's legs underneath the seat and pulled the man out. He asked him for his ticket, and he said be had none. When the porter went to inform the foreman defendant got into another compartment. The fereman followed and asked , him for his ticket. He said he had none, and came , out with some fearful language. His language on the , platform was most disgueting, and witness had received , complaints from passengers. Ha hoped the magistrates would make an example of the man.—Evidence in sup- port of this statement was given be John Crosdale, parcel perter, and Benjamin Higeoa. They said defend- ant's language was most disgusting.—P.C. Dawson said he brought defendant to the Police Station, and on the way he used diseracef ul lenguage. The crowd who fol- lowed teld him to close his mouth.—Tha Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. G. F. F. White) said detendant had evidently come from lintree races.—The Mayor observed that the case was a bed one, and a fine of 403. and costs would be inflicted for each offence, or in default two months' Imprisonment. ALLEGED TRETY BY A LABOURER. At the Warrington Occasional Coast on Tuesday, before Mr. Claude Morris, William Wilcock, a labourer employed at the new asylum works, Winwick, was charged with stealing on the lgth November, a sovereign, a half-sovereign, two shillings in silver, and a purse, the property of Patrick Huban, and an over- coat, the property of William Johnson.—Patrick Huban stated that he lived in Winsvick-road, and prisoner lodged with him. On tho 12-11 inst he and his wife had been to Warringeon, and on their return found the prisoner in the house. Shortly after he kilaban) pue his purse in a certain place. Later on his wife asked him for the purse, and he found it was gone. Prisoner bad also disappeared, and it was found that an overc eat belonging to another lodger was gene. Prisoner did not return to the bowie, and information was given to the police.—P.C. Heath said he apprehended the prisoaer on Monday. He was wearing the overcoat which it was alleged had been stolen. Witness found upon him a purse containing 6d. The coin had a hole in it. and Mrs Ruben identified it as one she had kept in her purse ROMS thne.—Prieoner denied that he took the money, and sail the overcoat had been lent to him.— He was remanded for a wecic, in order that further inquiries might be made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. FRlDAT.—Before Mr. James Hepherd (in the chair), and Councillor Smethurst. QUARREL BETWEEN Woltz:v.—Men Mee was charged with assaulting Alice Wilkinson on the 6th Novembar.—Defend- ant pleaded not guilty.—Alice Wilkieson, 85, Church-at.. said on Sunday last Mrs. Mee put her baby on the 11 )or and she (complainant) picked it up and defendant asked her for it and tore her @tress. Witness gave her the baby and defendant then struck her on the bre .st and on the face.— Defendant's daughter gave evidence.—The Bench fined defendant in the costs. EFFLGY BURNING.—Sarah Ashley was charged with using threats to Martha Matfett on the 7th November —Defendant pleaded not guilty.—Martha Mallett, 18 Gaskell-strect, gave evidence of the threats used to her on the Monday and said that defendant and another woman burned her image in their xfard.—Defendant emphatically dented the threats. —A witness who gave evidence said the defendant did make a boo-fire and pn,", the image on It but the fire would not burn it.—The bench dismissed the case.</p>
        <p>SATE/DAL—Before Mr. Thoraxs Birchall (in the chair) and Messrs. R. T. Painlough and J. W. Hayward. BITE:Molt o TIME.—Mrs. Gilbert licensee of the Llon Hotel, applie t for extension of tin_e on November 17th and Dc;ember 2111, the occhdons of the Cyclists' ball and the H.S.P.C.C. ball.—Granted. A DESERTER UNDER THE TABLE.— Matthias Pye and Peter Dutton was charged with deserting from Her Majesty's 3rd North Lancashire Er :ment at tioncl at Preston.-- Prisoners pleaded guilty.—Superhatendent Monaghan said be went to the Whivricic-str et Dining Re nu* at about a quarter past seven on Fliday evening. Witness saw Pye and knew he had been a so' Her. He was taking Pye outside when he noticed sometting moving under the table. Witness on looking found it was the othet prisoner hiding.—The Chief Conskabie ask: i for the usual reward t, be paid to the Superintendent.—The Bench remanded the prisoners to await an evert and agreed to the Chief Coust .ble's applics- tion.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (Alderman Wright), Alder. men Blackhurst. Harr:son, Roberts and Sutton, Messrs. F, Monks, T. Birchen. J. W. Hayward, R. T. Fdiro!ough, Shaw Green and Smethurst. STEALING Tam CR".o's MomEr Box.—Sarah Alice Barnes, last from Bed'ord Leigh, was charged with stealing from the house of John Turner. 12, Market-street, on November 10th. two shawl., a plr of bc -its and a child's money box, valued at 255. She pleaded guilty.—Superintendent Monagh in said the prisoner was employed by Mr. Turner on the 4th November, and on the 10th she was miss I shortly after being called for her work. The articles mentioned were also Rolm Subsceiently it was found that she had pawned them far Bs. She was apprehended in a lod:ing-honse at Bedford Leigh.—Prisoner bad been previously convicted for theft, and was now sent to gaol for a mouth. " CLIMBING LP '.IIE GOLDEN STAl—."—Thomas LOGI&amp;X, labourer, 17, Crossley-street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly at Market-gate on Saturday. When asked by the constable to behave himself and go away, he said he would rather te climbing up the golden st.iirs.—He was fined ss. and costs.</p>
        <p>KICKING A WOMAN.—EXEMPLARY PUXISIDIENT.—James Thomasson, labourer, 5, Morris's-yard. was charged with having been drunk and dicorderly in Silver-square at a quarter to eleven ou Saturday night and further with assaulting Elizabeth Riley at the same time and place.—P.C. Cooper said he saw prisoner drunk in Bilver-square. He ran up to a young woman and kle_ ad hr three times. Witness told the woman to go to the Police Station and prefer a charge against prisoner. but she refused. He tcok prisoner to the Police station, and en the way he was very violent. P.C. Howard gave corroborative evidence.—Prisoner denied that he kicked the woman.—The Mayor said they were going to try to put kicking down. Prisoner would be sent to gaol for a month. EXPOSURE or • Citl.—Margaret Burke was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Dutton-street. at live minutes past twelve on Sunday morning, and also with</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>E p tiy 7,1 S</head>
        <p>E p tiy 7,1 S</p>
        <p>G:IATEFUL-COM FORTING.</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>BREAKFAST AND SUPPER.</p>
        <p>exposing her child, aged three years, in a manner likely to cause it unnecessary suffering and injury to health.—P.C. Taylor said the woman was drunk and made use of the most filthy language. She bat a little child with her, and they were both wet through. He took them to the Police Station, and had them attended to.—P.C. Metcalf corro or- ated.—lt was stated that this was the woman's 22nd appear- ance. She had two months in August for absconding from the Workhouse. She was now fined 10s. and boosts. ALL BECAUSE OF THE EIO6.—ThODULS Ashurst, 20, River- ros.d. was charged with having been drunk and disorderly on November 7th. He replied that it was loss of temper, and excitement.—P C. Kane stated that he was on duty in Diver-road, and heard a disturbance on the riverside. He went there and saw defendant, who was drunk and very disorderly. He was quarrelling with some women about keeping a dog that prevented him Loin sleeping.—Constables Dawson and Howard corroborated.—Mrs. Birdsall, a neighbour, and tbe defendant's sister gave evidence as to defendant's sooriety, and the magistrates, considering that defendant had probably been aggravated, dismissed the Case. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Peter Kelly, Acton Grange; William Atikland, Warrington.... .</p>
        <p>A CASE DISAUSSELL—Sarah King, an elderly woman was charged with being disorderly in Silver-square on the 7th November.—Detective Superintendent ifouaghaa gave evi- dence in support of the charge, but did not press the else.— The magistrates cautionet the defendant and warned her...</p>
        <p>DISORDERLY YouTas.—John Bradshaw, 52, Bautsford- road, William Charnock. jun., 49, Napier-street, and William Langley, 10. Parr-street. were summoned for com- mitting a breach of the peace by being disorderly in Napier- street on November Bth.—P.C. Turner said the lads stood in front of a shop and shouted and jostled one another about the footpath There had been several complaints from shop- keepers about that sort of coaduct.—Detenclants denied they had misconducted themselves in any way—Tamer's state- ment was corroborated by P.C. Edwards and P.C. Whittaker and defendants were bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs of the case. LISMIIZzLE.D Duos.—Frederick Gosicer, 60, Hoyle-street, and Harry Furness. 171, Orford-lane, were fined is. each for allowing tbe:r dogs to be at larze unrauzzled. TursDAT.—Before Mr, Robert Harnett (in the chair), and</p>
        <p>Mr: Thomas Birchall.</p>
        <p>AN AS9AULT OLSE. —Alice Owen of 3, Btkehouse-yard, was charged on a warrant with assaulting Catherine Meachim on the lit October.—Defendant pleaded not guilty.— Catherine Meechlad said on the day in question she went to the defendant's house and asked for sonde mantes which she had lent defendant. Defendant then struck tier in the face and putting out har foot tripped her actainst the wall. She Mod not been able to use her arm. Witness had seea a doc- tor and found stir would have to go into the Infirm ary.— Annie Lawton and Mr Meachim corroborated.—Margaret lareaves, a witness for the defendant said she saw Mrs. Owen strike the complainant.—Mr. Owen. husband of the defendant.said Mrs Meachim came into their house and called them names. Both Mrs. Meachin and his wife had had drink. He asked complainant to go out and she would not. His wife then struck her.—The bench bound defendant over to keep the peace for six months in one surety of £5 and pay 10s as part of toe costs which amounted to 2is or iu deEault 14 day.</p>
        <p>WIEDNMILLY.—Before 14r. Z. T. Fair°laugh (initho chair)</p>
        <p>and Alderman Roberts</p>
        <p>TEMPORARY AUTH )RY,T P. —David James Warden applies for temporary authority to sell at the Lees Arms.—Graratee. —James Standish, licensee of the Big Horse Sloe, applied for a license to sell at the Volunteers' Ball on November 21st at the Drill Hall from 8 p.m till 3 am.—The Magistrates Clerk (Mr. Cl. F. P. White) said last year it was on!y granted from 8 until 2 o'clock.— the applicant said the bell was on until 3 o'clock.—The bench observed they thought 3 o'cbck too late, and granted the license until 2 o'clock THEFT BY A Donnellan, a married woman, was charged on remand with stealing an overcoat, valued at 10s. from the dwelling-house of Thomas Leech, 12. lrwell-st., on Monday.—She pleaded not guilty.—Honor Leech, wife of the prosecutor said that at one o'clock on Monday afternoon prisoner came to her house and stayed until 4 p.m. Abou that time witness went into the back kitchen, and prisoner went out without saying anything. She then missed her husband's overcoat, which had been on the sofa. Prisoner was not quite sober, and witness tld her several times to go home. Information was given to the police.—Other evidence was given.—This was prisoner's eighth appearance, and she was flied £l. (which included coati), or iu default 14 days' Imprisonment. _</p>
        <p>15RI7EPIC AND DILI &gt;RDERLT. —Timothy Brennan wss 151.1M- monad for being Munk and disorderly in Bride-street at 5.15 on Diesday evening. He pleaded gully.—As this was defendant's first appearance the bench took a lenient view of the offence and fined him ss.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY pErrY SESSIONS. VIEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. 4, Comber (chairman), Mr. Claude Morris. and Mr. J. J. Blackly. CHARGH AGAIN3T a PENICETH FARMER DISMI3SED.—WaI- ter Ashcroft, of Penketh, was summoned for being drunk in charge of a hove and cart at Penketh, on the Ist inst.—Mr. Browne appeared for the defence, and pleaded not guilty.-- P.C. Coupe said he saw the defendant at three o'clock on. the let November. drunk in charge ol a horse and cart. Witness stopped him about 100 yards away from his home. He re- fused ta give his name and address, and he also refused to go home. Defendant went back with witness to Sankey Bridges Police Station, where his name was obtalned.—P.S. Devlin said defendant gave his name and address, and he let him go borne. He Wa3 drunk.—Mr. Browne said this was not an ordinary case of drank in charge of a horse and cart. Defendant had independ est witaesses who would say that he was not drunk and that he was capable of taking charge of a horse and cart He went to Warrington to do business on the day in question, and had two glasses of beer. On his way home he called at a bl teksmith's shop in Penketh and gave orders about a plough, He subsequently met Hr. Kelsall, a well-known farmer, and also a teetotaller. and they went into the Red Lion to tr msact their business. Whilst there defendant had two glasses of beer. He came out again and did business with Mr. Sampson. He then got into his cart toga to his father's house, which was about half a mile away. When nearing the house the police officer stopped him after walk ing at the side of the cart all the Way. Defendant said he would go back to the smithy and get witnesses to say that he was not drunk. The constable would not let him go, and be was beard to say lam going b have my cue." He (Hr. Browne) admitted that defendant had had drink, but he was perfectly capable of taking charge of the horse and cart.— Defendant denied being drunk, and said that his brother, in the officer's presence, wanted to take him to several magis- trates o see if he was drunk and incapable of taking charge of a horse.—Walter Ashcroft, Walter K dsall and Isaac Ash- croft, sen.. gave evidence in support of the defence. aad the Chairman said it was a very difficult question to decide. The officers were perfre.dy justified in bringing the cab forward, but they would dismiss it in the circumstan^es. UNMUZZLED Dons —The following were fined for allowing their dogs babe at htge unmuzzled Spalding. Burtonwood, Frederick Bate, Sankey, William Hatton, , Sankey. , CART WITHOUT NAME.—Tohn Sampson, Warrington, was tined 5s including costs, for using a cart without haying his name painted thereon. THP: LIGHTING Or VEHICLES.-70hIl Edward Johnson, of Wilderspool, for neglecting to have a lighted lamp on his vehicle at Smkey on the night of the Bth November, was lined 53 including Costs. A Sono° LBOY'S QUARREL.—A. youth named Harold Single- ton, of Great Sankey was summoned on a charge of assault- ing Harold Golding on November 7th. —The complainant. a boy 9 years of age appeared in Court with two black eyes. and stated that the defend int struck him In the face and that was the Cause of the discolouration.—The defendant said the complainant threw stones at him.—The magistrates ordered the defendant to pay the costs.</p>
        <p>, ..... . A CARELESS ENGINE DRIVF.R.—James Wray, dryer of a traction engine belonging to Mr. Carter, of Manchester was charged with acting in contravention of the byelaws by leaving his engine on the highway at Riaton without anyone In charge.—Th3 deieudant admitted the offence, and was fined 10s.</p>
        <p>SLEEPY DR/VERS.—John Roach. WoJiston. for being asleep whilst in charge of a horse and c4rt on the 4th Nov. was fided lOs including amts.—John Fairhurst was charged with a similar offence at Rlston en 4th November. and also With being without a Pghted lamp on the night of the date named.—Sergeant Brindle proved the case and said it was at a quarter to twelve when he met the defendant.—The mactie- traes ftled defendant 15s Including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.--Before Messrs. P. Spellman (in the chair) aad W. Timmins.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT FRIII A SHIP AT LATCHPAID. —Matthew Pattison, was brought up In coat° iy, charged with the larceny of a blue ser,re coat and towel, valued at 6s. the pro- rerti of &amp;tweed Keene. mate of a vessel lying near the Latchford Locks.—P.C. Nicholls gave evidence that Info:ma- tion was given him of the th.ft, and also a doscription of the defendant, whom he apprehended in High-street, on Satur- thy about one o'clock. Prisoner had the articles (produced) with him.—Prisoner at the request of the police, was re- manded for eight days. DRUNlC.—Hubert Glynn., labourer of Weston. pleaded guilty to being drunk in High-street, Runcorn. and was fined 55.. and costs.:-The-c Ise was proved by P. O. Smith.</p>
        <p>A NOISY "LADY SiMpson was brought up irk custody, charged with using obscene language in Highs street and Bridge-street on the 13th November.—Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—P.O. Bowyer stated that he first saw the prisoner in High-street and subsequently in Bridge-street she was using most foul exoressiona. He had to lock her up.—Prisoner said that she was certainly a bad person. but not so bad as the officer would have their Worships believe, and it was the policeman that used bad language.—The bench considered that they were bound to t Ike the officer's word. She would be fined ss. and costs, or seven days.— Prisoner then became very " noisy " and gave the Bench an exhibition of her" good, qualities."</p>
        <p>--Awil-Yacaxarrox.-:Johnidab -Bancroft, letter carrier. Runcorn, applied for a certificate of exemption of va Ination for his child. Edward Francis, born on the 17th of June. The applicant stated that he had a conscientious objection to vac- cination, and was of opinion, that it was not a deterrent to the disease smallpox. He considered that it caused more deaths than cures. He also instanced a case not far from his own residence la which the result of vaccination was detri• mental.—The application was granted on payment of costs. 2s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>CARD.</head>
        <p>A CARD.</p>
