      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>LA.NCA.SIIIRE SENIO-It COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. BROUGH TON RANGERS. A very herd struggle wee fought out between these clubs on Saturday, at Wilderspool, bafors abut 5,000 spectators. Warrington's improved form had encouraged their supporters to hope for better things, and there was a certain amount of confidence shown that Brough- ton would meet with their masters on this occasion. The home team were determined to win if poosible, and they made a big effort, but a combination of circum- stances caused them to kauckle under by eight points to three. The Rangers brought a strong team, Duck bain-r the only absentee. Lewis filling the vacancy on the right wing. Warrington turned out as selected. Mr. J. H. Smith, Widnes, was the referee, and the players in his charge were as follows : Warrington: Hallam, full back ; Fish, Duckworth, , Isherwood, and Holcroft, threequerters ; J. T. Bate,and lAshurst, half-backs; Taylor, Naylor, Deakin, Mason, Swift, Boardman, Fairhurst and Suilow, forwards. Broughton Rangers : J. Hacking, full back; Lewis, Igerratt, Chapman and Jackson, threequarters; Mes- senger and Nelson:half-backs ; Steel, Blacklock,Lazanby, Robinson, Whitehead, Deakin, Lamb, and Stickney forwards.</p>
        <p>Warrington won the toss and had the advantage of a , light breeze in the first half. Steel started, and Holeroft, I had his return charged down, the ball going in touch, so that Warrington had the advatatage. Holcroft put iii a fine punt ane play rested in mid-field. Ashurst, Bate. and He'croft took up the running from the first scrimmage, and c onsidereble headway was made before Holcroft fell a victim to Nelson. The Ringers next had a chance, but Nelson threw the ball wildly, and the hannsters rushed up to the 25 line. Bate got away and threw across to Isherwood,whe was only able to go a short distance before being upst. Then Holcr oft kicked to the line, Chapman making a fine mark in front of the posts. His kick out brought no relief how- ever, Warrington attacking hotly. Bete and Fish at- tempted an agressive movement on the left wing, but Lewis stopped the Warringtoe man before he hal got really dangerous. Chapman had to touch down to keep Warrington back, Messenger kicking past centre. leher- wood returned but Ja :kson respenried with a dashing run into the home belf where he was upended by Het- croft. A good move by Bate, whereby the Rangers were ordered back, gave the hemesters the upperoand again. end another spell of fierce attacking by Warrington fol- lowed. Chapman and Lewis both made mistakes in touch whieh caused Brought on to be still herd .r prease I. Barrett mulle I A kick fr ,m Bate on the has, and the vieiters were f etunats when the ball r dead. War- ringten strained every nerve and by speedy following up again reached the Rangers'line,where Taylor end Deekin seemed to miss a easy chance. Hellarn hod a truitlees drop at goal, Meeeenger,Nels en, and Barrett re•pendine with aepassing run which was nipped in the bud by Hof- croft. The latter a moment liter ran cleverly and stopp- ing in midfield had a shot at goal which failed by the merest trifle. Another movement by the Rang,rs' backs sent the ball to Lewis who was travelling wIl when Bate bowled him in touch, Play was more even until Holcroft intercepted a Lass and eluding Jackson ran strongly and well into the Rangere,half bef ere meeting an obstacle in H ackine. Then Fise smartly secured in the home half and made a grand sprint, half the length of the field,only being grassed a yard from the line. Be had hard lines in not scoring. At last the Rangers got down to the Lome end, and pleptrei the goal warmly. Isher- wood made a fine catch under the posts in front of the Rangers' forwards,whieh saved a certain try. Bate was instrumental in transferring operations to the Breugh- ton end, and a mistake by Jackson gave Warrineten a further advantage. I strong dash to the Rangers' line was nullified by Messenger who broke away, and aided by Lewis carried the game the length of the field in fine style. Just before the intery .1 Ceapmea made an in- effectual attempt to drop a goal. At half time tber was no score. Teeter re-eemme med hostilities, and Naylor kicking too hard forced a minor at once. A o ,natty kick to the Rangers caused the hornesters to fall back. Exciting play followed and the lengthy Jackson reeeiving the ball from Borne acramblin play rushed over at the corner. I The try was a doubtful one, and appeared to have been passed forward. There was no goal from Chaprnan's kick. Warrington were di6heavened for a time by this unlucky reverse, and the Rangers maintained the attack. Jecksce was again prominent with a clinking run, but found a match in Taylor. Play reachel tee home line, and after some very anxious moments Bate relieved with rare skill. Misfortune here dogged Warrineten's footsteps, arri Hallam mulling badly ' scramble I for the ball and was penalised for not playing it. The declaim was received very unfavourably, and hooting was loud when Nelson added a goal frem the easiest of positions. Hallam was at fault again s yin after, and appoarred for a time to have lost his head Warringteree defence was very good, and at last they gained a footing in the visitors' half. Aehuret made a , good ran and looked Irk scor;ng when hi was tackled vigorously by Steel. Once on the attack again and the borne stars werkel like hero's. Bate mini Ashurat got away and after quick passing sent to Holcroft who r Med over at the corner, Hacking making a us&amp;ess attempt to atop him, rho cheering was great, and the try deserved it. Misoe failed at goal from a difficult I position. Seeing a possible chance Warrington played for all they were worth and held teeir own. Fate was I against them however. Tee Rangers worked back to ' the hems half, and Fish making a nail-kick let Ceepinan in with a sift try. The goal kick failed, but it was all up with Warrington. and the game soon after ceded with tho score reading, Broughton Rangers, 1 goal 2 tries, Warrington one try.</p>
        <p>SALFORD v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The retUrn Match by these c:ubs was played at New Barns, Salford, in dull but fairly favourable weather, before upwards of 8,000 spectators. Salford were strengly represented, but Runcorn were weakened by the inability of Jolley and Robinson, their usual half backs, t) play; whilst Reece, one of their seleeted for- wards, waa rsplac:3 by Eingiies. Teams :—Silford : D Smith, bier; J Hoskins!, T Williams, WNee, an 1 A Pearson, three-quarter backs; I Grey and Griffiths, half backs; J Wihiarns, II Mes, M",--j Rhapp9, H Woodhead, G Heath, W Llewellyn, an P Tunny, forwards. Runcorn S Houghton. back C Butter. worth, TiWarder, J Bowan, and T V9'ilklnson, three- quarter backs ; J Nickson and Williams, half hacks ; S Walker, A Taylor, W Lightfoot, F Gavter, H Fume*, J Totnliuson, F Darlington, and 0 Hughes, forwards. Referee, tlr J Bruckshaw(SCerakport), The positions of the clubs in the Coinpetition table are ia etrong contrast, Salford being arnene the leaders, whilst Runcorn are low down in the list, and a some- what one-sided game was anticipated ; but the visitors, after being in a minority of eight points at half-tins' played up smartly on changing ends, :trod quickly brourrht the score level—eon points each. Tney then sustaiael a great less in the disablement of Heughtoi, their full back, who after a plucky attempt to continue ' playing wig carried :off the field. Thus handicapped Rune ora were afterwards entplayed, and Salford in the end secured the victory by thirteen points to ten. On the game being cinimencel the visitors forced the play and soon reached the Salford quarter, but a fine rush by the horn' forwards relieved tile pressure and a passing run by Phillips, T. Williams, and Hoskins took the ball to the Runcorn end, where it was pissed forward when a try appeared probable. Again the visitors attacked, Better worth and Warder being prominent on the right wing. Scrummaging followed in the Salford quarter and Nickson getting possession passed the ball to Wilkinsen, who deftly dropped it over the crossbar, Fairly even play followed the kiek-off from the centre until I'. Williams punted strongly to the Runcorn end. Houghton Warder, and Walker defended well, and for a Vine kept their lines intact, but at length Grey passed Out fr 3122 a scrummage to Griffiths, from whom the ball Wet to T. Williams, who eluded all the attempts made to tackle him and secured a try for Silford, which Phillips from a poor position placed inco a goal. Salford renewed the pressure on resuming, and a minor ' point fell to them ; whilst a little lat e• a flue issuing run by the home back division ended in Hoskins gaining a try, Pnillips placing *Rather goal. Runcorn then attacked, hut Smith nlieve I the pressure with a fine kick, and Houghton was tackled bet ;re he c'ould return the ball. From ties scrummage that followed the visitors were penalised in front of their goal posts, but Phillips failed to turn the opening to account. The visitors were at this point of the game playing five thres-quarter backs, but suffered by comparison with their rivals. Grey and Butterworth crossed the respective goal lines . before half-time' but tries were disallowed for breaches of rule, and the teams changed ends with the score : Salford, two goals and two tries ; Runcora, one goal. Rhapps resumed play after changing ends, and in a few minutes Salford were forced back to their quarter. Lively scrummaging took place, and Nickson getting possession 'passed to W lkinson, who quickly transferred the ball to Lightfootos bon the last-named player dashed over the goal-line and gained a try for Runcorn, which Farmer failed to improve,though he sent the bell against</p>
        <p>CADI3..JRY'S Coops. on the testimony of tile Lancet " represen. s the standard of highest purity." It is eatirely free from all foreign substances, such as Ito's, malt, hops. nor is alkali used to darken the colour (and so deceive the eye). Dr. Andrew Wilson. in a recent article in the 111ustratei Lonlon News writes : " Cocoa is in Itself a perfect food, and requires no addition oi drugs whatever." CADBURY's Cocoa is absolutely pure, and should be taken by old and young, at all times and in all seasons ; for Children it is an ideal beverage, promoting healthy growth and development in a remarkable degree. Insist on hav:ng CADBURY's. as other Coroae are often substituted for the sal-e of extra profit. Bold only in Packets and Tins.— Advt. (3)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>SWINTON v. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>SWINTON v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Played at Swinton ou S iterday. Widnes have for a time headed the championship table, but an opinion appeared to prevail that their position was a somewhat false one, , as they hid not previously met either of the other lead- ing clubs, and this match was therefore regarded as the first test of their being able Le maintain the lead they have gained. The result of the play on Saturday clearly stamps them as a team of uneoubted ateility, and shows that their position is by no means a false one. The conditions under which this ifs:tee was played were not very favourable owing to a dense mist welch hung over the ground, and at times it was only with difficulty that the play could be followed by the specteters, who were estimated to number fully 12,000. The game ended in a draw of three points each. The forwards on both sides played a hard and vigorous game, but the Widnes set showed greater cleverness in heeling the ball out and dabbling awl punting in the open field, while their halves appeared to be smarter than the Swinton pair. It was at three-quarter back where the Swintonians showed to better advantage, Valentine and Badger es- peeially being prominent in repelling the many attacks of the visitors, &lt;ollie Batey for Widnes prevented the home three-quarters fr .m crossing the line by sound teekling and stroag kicking. Swinton were fully re- presented, while substitutes had to be feund for Martin Brennan and Johnson on the Widnes side. Teams : Swinton :J. Goodneen,full back; R.J.Meser,O, Badger, J.Velentine (camtain),and J.T.Lewis three-quarter backs; W. Pearson and F. Morgan, half backs • J. Evans, G. Herris, J. Johneon' J. Reynolds, J. Shepherd, B. Tickle, E. Vie-rs, and J. Worthington, forwards. Widnes :R. J. Batey, full beck; T. Peacock, T. Rispan F. J. Plumpton, and T. Nevitt, thre-quarter backs ;E. Douglas and T. Murphy, half backs ;J. Hunt, F. Ramp- s-ea, 0. llesem (captain), J. Asper, W. Travis, T. Doervely, R Themes. and T. Handley, forwards, Referee, Mr. A. Brown (Brigheuse Rangers). Heslop kicked off for Widnes, and at zer the return kick the visitors rushed th bell close to the Swinton goal line. Paulsen relieved wiee a run and kick, but Batey picked up the ball end dropped at goal,a minor point resulting Upon ri leasing at th 3 quarter leg the Swinton backg- ran finetv, hut Lewis was unable to pass Batey. Gnod man twiee mulled the ball, and Widnes attacked strongly. After a scrimmage in front of goal Murphy gained nesseeeion and dropped, bet a minor point only resulted. The game was vigorously contested, and each end in turn wag quickly visited. Valentine and Badger in turn ran and punted, aud a strong rush by the S gluten forwards euded in Batey snatching the ball from as opponent over his goal line and sending it deed. Another minor fe'l ta Swinton by Pearson dropping at goal. Then Widnes had a turn, but only two dead balls were gained from kicks by Batey and Rispan respec- tivelY. The Swin'oe backs ran te midfield, where Lewis was splendidly tickled by Beier, the ball going into touch. The Widnes thre -quarters followed with sev- eral fine passing runs, but Valentine and Bader pre- vented any score. Thu home forwards screwed a pack, and Evens, Reynolds, Worthington and Johnson, carried the ball well into the visitors' quarter. At length, after Goodman had been collared with the ball, several scrim- mages foil-sired close to the Swiatoa line, and fine ;ma- itre between Douglas, Murphy, Peac, ck, and Nevitt ended in Plurnpton scoring a try for Widaes at the corner flag, but Batey failed at goal. Half-time score : Widnes, one try, (three points) ; Swinton nothing. Valentine r, started and the Swintoniane were quickly the agereesors ; a t suchdown however, by Batey was the Only result. The game, as in the first hell, was of a ding-dong charac,er, and bath sides in turn were com• pelted to defend their lines. At length Badger charged clown a kick, and Valentine picking up the hall, ran past several opponents, and scored a try for Swinton. Goodman foiled at th- place kick. For a time Widnes were severly pressed, and elorean. Valentine, and Lewis each nearly scored. Murphy, a Widnes half back was ordered off the field by the referee for tripping and roughness. Tae viaitors made strong efforts and rushed the ball to the home goal line. where Goodman cleverly collared Rispan and Peacock and returned the ball to midfield. A splendidly coot s:ed gains ended in a draw of one try (three points) leh. ST. HELENS v. WIGAN. Played at St. Holmes. St. Helens had their best team with the exception that" Tod Sloan " played at half back in place of Berle. Wigan were short of Hellewell, John Winstantley, full letee, played three-quarter. St. Helens wen the toss, and Brown kicked off for Wigan. John Wins: intley receiving, put the ball over the bar, drawing first blood for Wigan. From a iscrimnalge tbeb all was handed from Sloan to Briers, mei Trainer gained a good try, also kicking a goal. A smart bit of passing by Sloi.n, Barnes, and Deueleety resulted in the , latter voing over the line. 'The point was not allowed, howeves. From a scrimmage Sloan and Briers handed the bee on to Traylor, who bounded over the line and kicked another glal from the place. John Winetenley worked exceedingly hard for Wigan. Siddel was kicked OU the head, and lied to be carried off the field. Half- time : St. Elelems 2 goals 2 tries, Wigal 1 goal. Traynor restarted for St. Helens. Hard work by the visitors was at last reward, Blickburn dashing over near the goal, and Winstenley steered the leather over the bar. Fival result : St. Helens 10 points, Wigan 7 points.</p>
        <p>STOCKPORT v. LEIGII._</p>
        <p>A keen strug—gle—w—a7;i-tWes;ed—taTito—c-kport, and sin. gularly enough all the goals ac )red were dropped. Moly- rims obtained two for Leixh, and Saville one for Stock- port, who lost by two points t 4 four. ROCHDALE HORNEN v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>The Hornet. hd OWban a, their guesta --ti7e visitors winning by three points to nil. Williams (twice) and Martin crossed the Hornets' line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>LEEDS v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>1 Bradford suffered &amp;Teal at the hands of Leeds on the laner's grouni by two goals one try te one try. Lumley scored the first try for Leeds, which Pearson converted, and Walker dropped a goal for the rams side. LEEDS PARISEI CHURCFI v. FIECKNIONDWIKE• flecktnondwike were outplayed by their oppoientg at LAOCIS. Ch irch led by a try at half time, but after the iot3rvai Garsids, 'Moles? and Knight all go:., tries, the re- sult being :—L 'ads Parini Churc a, two gods four tries; Heckmondwike, nil.</p>
        <p>BRIGHOUSE BANGERi v. aumsLEr. Heavy scoring eharacterigel this match at Brighouse. Goldthrope dr wad two goa)s and plattei two penalties roe Hunslat, for wlrmn illannah also got a try. Pearson (two) tad Armitage obtair,ed points for Brighouse, who were defeated by four go lls two tries to one goal three tries.</p>
        <p>HULL v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY. Thre v 8.4, four tries to one dropped golf. was the verdict in f avow of Hull, on their own gravid. Lem- priere and Towne dil g led sorvice for the winnersois did Metcalf f-r the losers. LIVERSEDIE v. HUDDERSFIELD. Lirersede wern bettan at home by tvr) goals two tries tn nil. Wdliarni and Smith dropped goals, while the former and S itlliffe each get an unconverted try.</p>
        <p>ALINNINGHA.SI v. HOLBECE. Flockhart obtai led a Converted try, and Brown dripped a goal Cr the too tt club, atlhisnuinehena. whllet Elmstey and Leonard cross2d the line for Holbeck. Result : Minaingharn, two guts one try ; Holbeck, two tries.</p>
        <p>BATLEY. v HALIFAX. 131t1557 was the 17?It18 of this match. Glod PlaY wag wn by tie h)ma fifteei, for whom Davis, Oakland, Fozztrd, Fuzzerald, and Taylor were conspicuous, Bat- -le° won easily by two god; and bur tries' to nil.</p>
        <p>BRAAILEY v. CiSTLEFORD. These teatns plord at Broonlev. No scoring took scorsd a place in the initial period, bat Costteford afterwards try aid won by three points to uil.</p>
