      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>"A" TEAM CHAMPIONSHIP.</head>
        <p>"A" TEAM CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>- - WARRINGTON V. BROUGHTON RANGERS. The Warrington A. " team visited Broughton on Sa`urday and defeated the Rangers in decisive style, adding to their long list of successes. The home team kicked off. the fdrwardsi were very evenly matched and for a considerable time play was confined to mid- fled. Harris took the ball front a Broughton back and scored under the stunaps. No goal resulted, and 110 further score was made up to halt-time. Warrington had a decided vup:iriority in the full back line, and Eden getting poisAssion front a mull b 7 Broughton scored a fine try, which Heesora improved upon. Final score— Warrington, one goal two tries (eight points) ; Brough- ton Rangers. nil.</p>
        <p>RENCORN "A" V. SALFORD "A." These tam s net at Runeorn on Saturday afternoc..n. Runcorn kicked off with 14 men, and getting the advan- tage o: the opening exchanges they soon made tracks for the Salford line. A series of scrimmages ensued near</p>
        <p>RHOS, ANIMALS AND FISH STUFFED, AND ILI mounted in Glass Cases any design. HEADS and HORNS mounted, also for sale. Birds made into Hind Screens or Fire Screens, Deale in British and Foreign Birds and Animals' Skins. Butterflies, Birds' Eggs, Glass shades, Stags' Heads, Cabinets. Store Boxes, Nets and all Requisites for Butterfly and Egg Collectors,—B. COOKE, Naturalist and Furrier, 21, Renshaw - street, Liierpoo- Established 24 years.</p>
        <p>the visitors' goal, and at length Anderson got over. The kick at goal failed. Salford worked their way into the home quarters, and passing betwean the backs ended in a floe try being scored. SaHerd were once more becom- ing dangerous when half-time arrived without further score. On the change of ends Runcorn were soon on the aggressive, and scrummaging topic place near the visi- tors' goal. Runcorn continued to hold their opponents in close quartars, and one of the Salford men mulling the ball it rebounded into the hands of Rees, who scrambled over and scored a second try. No goal resulted. Play became fast and exciting. &amp;Herd carried operations lute the home 25, and after some per- sistent efforts managed to add another try, thus making matters equal. Runcorn, however, not to be denied, were soon in close proximity to the Salford hues, and they ultimately put a different complsxion on the game , by adding a third try. Some give-and-take play followed, and Ransom weie compelled to kick dead. The game eveetually ended in a win for Runcorn by three tries (nine points), t) two tries (six points). On the day's form the display of the Runcorn 15 was far superior to that of the visitors. WIDNES Av. SWINTON A.—Played at Widnes before about 2,000 speetators. The visitors were a man short throughout the game. Swinton started and Mines at once displayed their good combination. From a scrim- mage close to goal, the visitors were compelled to concede a minor. In exchange of free kicks then tank place. Play was now vory fast, Widnes being coetinually on the offensive but were unable to break through the stubborn resistance of Swinton. A passing movement carried the ball to the visitors' 25. From Wee scrimmage play was again forced back to midfield.A free kick by Grace at goal went wide, and a kick by Kinsella was charged down by the visitors, who rushed the ball into the home quarter. Hickey obtaining possession ran the ball down to the visitors' line. From the pack Hollan trickily eaosed his opponents and grounded the ball in a good position. Foster adding the major point. Half-time :—Widues, one goal one try ; Swinton, nil. In the opening period play was even, but Widnes getting away with a smart dribble, Murry snored. Foster being unfortunate enough to hit the goal post with a capital shot. Play continued fast. Valentine obtaining pos- session, with a splendid burst cleared the field from his own quarter flag, and touched down under the poste. Much disnatisfastion was expresso Iby the referee nulli- fying the kick at goal as it manifestly went over the bar. From a passing movement Taylor with a smart sprint scored. Soon after the line was again crossed by Hughes, Kir stilt improving. Final :—Widnes two goals foil. tries; Swinton one try. Throughout the game the combination of the Witless forwards excited the highest admiration.</p>
        <p>FLETCHER'S ENGINEER'S V. lIILAH AND CADISHEAD.— Played at Warrington on Saturday, the locals giving a rest to the majority of their league players. During the first half the locals showed superiority in passing move- ments. and the outcome of a really fine effort was a try to J. Evans, the point being improved upon by War- burton. Following this came a sharp raid by the visitors but they were eventually repulsed by Warburton, and even play was witnessed until the interval at which period the Engineers led by five points. The second half was even throughout, and no further points were notchel.</p>
        <p>BIRKENHEAD WANDERERS "A" v. LATCHFORD RANGERS.</p>
        <p>Played at Birkenhead. The opening stages were in favour of the WAnderers, and ultimately M'Keown secured, and by a splendid run scored a try. The Wan- derers continued to have the bestlof play to the interval. Half time—Wanderers, 1 try ; Albion ail. Duriag the sec mcl half the Wanderers again had a trifle the best of it, and in the end won by a goal and two trie3 (eight points) to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>MEETING OF TUE LANCASEIIRE SENIOR COMTE ri LION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF TUE LANCASEIIRE SENIOR COMTE ri LION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The committee ot the above tiTii-rnao-t7it the Spread Eagle Hotel, Manchester, on "fuesday evening, when the following business was transacted :—Oldham was fined 6s. for the late start of the A team at Swinton,and Roch- date hornets A team 153. ior the late start at Widnes. The following permits were granted : J Bumble (Black- wood Wanderers), Hulin3 (Stockport), and J H Dibbom (Abergavenny), to play with Stockport ; E Harris (Latchford Rangers) and W Townsend (Wakefield),with Warrington; and T Fletcher (Seaton). with Oldham. The matter of Swinton hiving played Owen Badger against Leigh when that player bad not been following his employment during the week was up for settlement. Badger had been on a visit to a sick child in Wales, and Swinton thought that un ler the circumstances they were justified in playing him. Tne decision come to was that Swinton have two points deducted from their score, in accordance with by-law 29, owing to the Ibreach of the prdfessional rules.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>CUMBERLAND v. CHESHIRE.</head>
        <p>CUMBERLAND v. CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>The Cumberlandrety has beerS notified of two changes in the team which will meet Cheshire this Satur- day at Runcorn. Tom Parkinson (Workington), lett wing threequarters, stands out, his father having died on Wednesday. W. H. Mandle (Maryprrt) will All thr position. , The veteran Buckett (Afillom) does not fed equal to the strain of this match,tiaving been indisposed recently, and his place will be filled by W. P. Robinson (Maryport), late with Brooghton Rangers. Tom Fletcher (Seaton) has assured the offis;ial3 that his connection with Oldham wilL not interlace with his services for Cumberland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON A.F.C. Y. BROORLANDS OLD BOYS.</p>
        <p>--- This match was played at Sale on Saturday. War- rington won the toss, and started with ten men. Hans having missed the train. Warrington were quickly on the attack, and Jewkes soon operAei the scoring with an oblique shot. From the kick off the OH Boys' forwards could snake no headway against the Warrington halves. A scrimmage in the Brooklande geal ended by one of their full backs!giving Warrington a goal. Warrington continued to press, and Silcock scored the third goal. Half-time i—Warriogton, three goals ; Brooklanda Old Boys, nil. On resuming Warrington continued to have all the game, and further goals were scored by R. Pier- point two, It. Silcock one, and W. Hind one. Final : Warrington, seven goals ; Bre Wands Old Boys, nil. PARR VICTORIA V. ST. ELPHINS. It was generally admitted that the Saints had a tough job on to beat the Vice. at Parr, especially after them beating the leaders, Peasley Cross.the previous Saturday and expectations for once were well founded. There were two alterations in St. Elphln's team, Howard and I Harrison replacing Hunter and Hickinbottom, both showing themselves worthy substitutes. Parr Vice. scored the only goal in the opening sages of the game, due in some measure to a poor clearance by the War- ' rington goalkeeper. The game was very stoutly cm- tested, the Parr men having a great tendency to rough- ness. A draw would haw bean a more fitting finish to , the actual play. Parr had an advantage at back, which will be stronger still when F. Singleton becomes used to his altered position. Howard made a very creditable first appearance at outside right..</p>
        <p>LOVELY-LANE /ATHLETIC (WARRINGTON) V. ASTON EINATH. Played at Warrington on Saturday. Evans scored for the home team soon after st srting. Matters were soon put straight S Johnson scoring for the visitors and P. Pennington adding another shortly after. Half•time : Lovely-lane one goal, Aston two goals. Na further score was made in the second half. Final—Aston Heath two gels, Lovely-lane Athletic one goal.</p>
        <p>EiRLICSTOWN V. ASIITON-IIV-MAKICRIPIELD CENTRA.L.</p>
        <p>These teams met on Saturday on the Earlestown ground, in the f ,uttn round of the qualifying stage of the Lancashire junior cup, before a fair gatheling of spectators. Earlestown won the toss and Ashton kicked off down hill and attacked. Limoad cleared and Earles- town at once rushed to the other cni and from a corner kick Leach headed through. Play was evenly con- testrd for some time, but gradually Aehton put in a spell of preosure and were enabled to test Chew on several occasions, but he succeeded in keeping his charge intact. Play became very even and both sides seemed in deadly earnest. Gradually Earlestown got the upper hand and by severe pressure were able to get up ta the Ashton goal where from some smart passing in front of the goal Leach scored with a very easy shot. Jenkinson bad to save on two occasions afterwards, and Ashton getting down were awarded a free kick right in the goal from a foul. This was manipulated into a goal. From the kick off, Earlestown raced away and Jenkinson had to throw away. A brief viiit to the other end by Ashton was repulsed and some very neat passes enabled Earleatown to again get up, and Leach scored with a daisy cutter. Frcm the kick off the Ash- ton goal was again in danger, but Jenkinson saved grandly,and Ashton worked down hill to the lower goal, and after a series of exchanges in front of the posts they Scored their second point. Earlestown worked up from the kick off and on two occasioas became dangercus, but pressure was relieved. Ashton afterwards secured a couple of corners in quick succession, but they were unable t 3 derive any advantage from them, and a brief visit to Jenkinson's charge was driven back, and at half 1 time the score stood Earlestown 3, Ashton 2. On turn- ing round Earlestown paid a brief visit to the lower ' goal, but they could not penetrate the Ashton defence. Some loose play gave the ashton right wing an opening, and racing away, Chew was beaten by a lightning shot which placed the teams on equal footing. Play after- wards ruled even and both sides strove hard to obtain the winning point. The defence at each end, however, was too good, and both goal-keepers were able to keep their charge intact. Earlestown presiesd and from a pass by Marsh, Settle had a good opening,but he missed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>x 2(. RARE PENCH ilr ENGLISH lirf',l,:,</head>
        <p>x 2(. RARE PENCH ilr ENGLISH lirf',l,:,</p>
        <p>Curios, Facetice, Illustrations, &amp;c. VERY VALUABLE PRIVATE coLLEcrzoN, CATALOGUE 11. POSTAGE 210,</p>
        <p>H. BOUVIER, 44, Rue St. George's, X .L PARIS. -</p>
        <p>in his final effort by inches. Ashton took up the pressure and only excellent play by Chew kept them from suiring. When the whistl, sounded neither side had secured the winning point. After eonsultation it was decided to play an extra half hour. Earlestown playing up hill pressed considerably and they hovered in a dangerous fashion round Jenkin- son's chsrge. Just upon half time short passing in front of goal gave Lea a splendid opportunity, and he man- , aged to place his side ahead. On changing ends Ashton I strove barn to equalise, but they could not penetrate the defence, and the match was finished in semi-dark- ness. Earlestown 4, Ashton 3.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN GTJILD V. RUNCORN ST.</p>
        <p>JOHN'S.</p>
        <p>This return match too-1c.13:-e-c-e on the ground of the latter last Saturday. The saints were strengthened by the inclusion of the brothers, Alf, Will, and Walter Hague, of Widnes. The Guild won the toss and played uphill. Weston receiving on the right made a neat run along touch and centreing well the Saints'goal was bom- barded for a little while. Jones with a ponderous kick cleared and put his forwards in possession. They at once • wane straight for goal, where after some neat exchanges, Walter Hague sent through at the corner, Wright making an ineffectual attempt to save. This seemed to stimulate the teams to greater things and both sides went at it " ding dong," but nothing further was scored up to half time though both custodians had plenty of work to do. ! At breathing time the score was :—St. John's, 1 goal ; Wesleyans nil. Cartwright .estarted. The Wesleyana were soon back and Weston, cleverly tricking Eaton, Jones and Rowlands, put in a beautiful centre which Cartwright turned toshaccount. Cartwright, Withington and Lambert mxt made tracks for goal and after trying to penetrate the Saintegoal Ackers kicked high over the bar. From the kick out the Saints were dangerous and pressedilague on the right wing put in a"teaser"which Wright kicked along the line for which the refern granted a corner. From the corner Baxter shot through and put the Saints ahead aeain. From the kick off the Wesleyans played like Trojans. Cart- wright obtained possession from a throw-in, and thread- ing his way very cleveily through the opposing backs, went right through the goal and equalised the scores. This was a marvellous piece of play. From the centre- kick the Saints threatened danger but aft-sr an exchange of kicks the visiting forvrards obtained pmaession. and combining well took play to the St. John's goal. Cart- wright securing banged the ball through, and thus Put the Gelid ahead. Right up tl the finish play was very interesting, but no alteration was made to the score, which when the final " solo " sounded, read Brunswick 3 goals ; St. John's, 2 goals. Next welk the Guild Play Runcorn Association on the latter ground Canal-street, kick off at 3 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>HOCKEY,</head>
        <p>HOCKEY,</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH"A" v.SOUTHMANCHESTER B.—Played on the ground of the former, South playing ten mon. Two 35's were arranged, and a very even and enjyable game ensued, marred, however, towards the close, by .) darkness making the game more or leis of a continuous hunt for the ball. South come out winners by two goals to one.</p>
        <p>BOWDON " A " v. STOCKTON IlEarn.—Played at Bow- don on Saturday, and after a fast game ended in a win for Bowdon by six goals to two. The home forwards played very well together, and Needham, on the left wing, played a paatioularly good game and shot Or( e goals. Moore was responsible for two of the other Teams :—Bowdon : N Mills, 9 Edmondson, G Sugden. Baum, R H Wolff, H K Waterhouse, J B Armitage, N O'Hanlon, H Moore, A G Whittall, E Needham. Stock- ton Heath : Wilkinson, Willett, Sutcliffe, Richirdson Podmore, Barlow, Lyon, Picton, Woods, Sandy, Bond. )1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>ALLEGED STARVATION OF A CHILD NEAR WARRINGTON. •</head>
        <p>ALLEGED STARVATION OF A CHILD NEAR WARRINGTON. •</p>
        <p>SHOCKING RhVELATION3.</p>
        <p>An isquest•was held by Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, at the Rope and Anchor Inn, Woolaton, on Monday, on the body of a child, Frank Stead, the son of John Albert Stead,ot Woalst an painter and sign writer. Mr. Thomas Houghton was ioreman of the jury. The National Society for the Prevention of Cruety to Child- ren,was represented by Inspector Mc ateer,of Warrington land Inspector Morgan attended on behalf of the police. Jane Gibbons said the child was born on the 2nd Oct. It was a healthy child.—P.C. Bellinger spoke to visiting Stead's house,situate in Long Barn-lane, on the sth Nov. with Inspector McAteer prior to the death of the child. There was no food in the hoes,. They saw the child, who was in a poor weakly condition. The lespector ad- vised the father to take the child to the doctor, but he said it was well and they had no money. McAteer sug- gested that he should see the relieving officer.— Inspector McAteer corroborated, and ad led that he intended visiting the house again on the following Satur- day but heard of the child's d.ath on the Friday. After its death he weighed the child and found its weight to be 411b.,the average weight for a child cf that age being 9ilb. Asked by the Foreman why he did riot aequaiut the relieving officer of the child's condition, the Inspec- tor replied that that was not within the scope of his duties.—P.S. Brindle said he visited the house with Bellinger at eleven a.m., on the 6th November. He saw the deceased being nursed by its mother. It wes undressed and unwashed. It looked very poor and ill. He had a conversation with the father advising him to take the child to the doctor, an I he (Stead) said that his wife must take it to a doctor on the (Viewing day.— Jane Stead,sister to the deceased, stated that her mother fed the child on milk and biscnita snake.' in sugar and water.—Dr. James Bennett, of Padgate,descrihed the re- ult of the pot mortem examination he had made cif the body. There was no fat on the bode, which was a sure sign of starvation, from which death was due.— Stead, the father, elected to give evidence, and in a lon4 statement denied that there had been any neg- lect on the part of him aid his wife. He alleged there woe a concp'racy against him.— The Coroner having summed up the evidence, the jury after due consideration, returned a verdict that death was due to starvation c Itl u.ii iejedicious feed- ing.—At the close of the ioqueht Stead was arrested by P.S. Brindle,and conveyed t) the Padgate Police Station, on the charge of persistently neglecting his other child- ren.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednes- day morning, before Mr. G. C3mber aid other , mdZis- trates, John Albert Stead and Charlotte Stead, Wools- atm], were summoned for wilfully neglecting and ill- treating their six children, Mary Ellen, Alice, Charlotte, Eakin, Milly and Baby, on the Sth November and other dates during th 3 last six months, in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health. Mr. Br-. wne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne, in opening the case, said he was instruc- ted to prosecute the prisoners for one ef the worst cares which had ever come before the Ceurt. At an inquest held on Monday night on a baby five weeks old, of which the prisoners were parents, the verdict of the jury was that the child " died from starvation," The male prisoner was severely censured, but the woman was free from blame. The male prisoner was a foreman painter and got 6s. a day. He had had constant work but it was simply the same old tale—drink. In most of the cases brought be the N.S.P.C.C. Society drink was the cause of the cruelty to the poor children. The prisoners lived at Woolston and they had nine children. six of whom were under 16 yeers of age. Oa the sth of November, Inspector McAteer visited the house and found the children in a neglected condition. Mr. Browne proceeded to speak of the scanty clothing worn by the children who were in a dirty condition. The baby was filthy and smelled very bad. The female prisoner told the inspector that she could not get fooi because her husband had not given her any money for weeks, and Ishe had been obliged to keep the house on the wages of the boy and girl. The children had lived on raw turnips which had been given them by the farmers, and also dry potatoes. Neighbours had been extremely kind to them and given them food. Thorn was no doubt that the child died from shortness of food. They did not blame the wife as much as the hueband. P.C. Billinger corroborate - . Sergeant Brindle stated that he recived the male prisover into custody. On examining him he found a letter in his possession from one •i the children, dated July 26th, which at sted that thee were ill through eat- ing so mane. petatoes. They had been starved that week and did not want to 'starve the following week. Continuing the eeresant said that when he v.sited the house on the 6th inst about eleven o'clock in the morn- ing the defendant was at that time having 'his break- fast. One of the children cried for a bit of bread and during that time the male defendant was enjsying mutton chop. The children had been eating green potatoes which farmers had discarded and woull not pick up. Jane Stead, 18 years of age, daughter of the prisoners, and Inspector el'Ateer also gave evidence. John Albert Stead, the male prisoner,7then addressed the Bench. He denied that he was a foreman painter, but admitted that his wages were 8d per hour. The peintenz trade was a very irregular one. Ile teed lived In Warrington and district 22 years and had never been brought before the magistrates before. He contended that the case had bean palatal blacker than it really was and that he had done hie best for his family. He had a situation waiting for him at Leigh and one that would last him through the winter. He threw himself on the mercy of the Court, and if they would give him another chance he would do his utmost for his children and never come into court again. The magistrates considered that he bad neglected his children and been idle,but as he promieed to turn over a new leaf they deferred sentence for a month to see if he effected an improvement. The wife was discharged.</p>
        <p>To MOTHICII3.—Mrs. Wilmslow's Soothing Syrup hasbeen for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their chit drat: while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. ft is pleasant to taste : it pro- duces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child fro npain and the little cherub awakes" as bright as a button." Of al chemists 16.10. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>41 THE MAYOR AND '; ,„ , CORPORATION AT CHURC</head>
        <p>41 THE MAYOR AND '; ,„ , CORPORATION AT CHURC</p>
        <p>- -- The Mayor and Corporation of Warrington at- tended Divine service at the Parish Church on Sunday morning, and favoured by fine weather, there was a large attendance. Shortly after ten o'clock a start was made from the Town Hall, ' order of the procession being as follows Detachment of Volunteers and band, detachment police, ambulance corps. fire brigade, his Worsh._ the Mayor and his officers, members of the To Council, borough magistrates, Poor Law Guardian/ townsmen. A large number of people watched th procession which presented an improved appearan The Volunteers, who numbered about 50,were und the command of Major Buckton, who was &amp;core panied by Captain B Fairclough. The Voluntee, ei brass band under the conductorship of Mr. Arm strong played a suitable selection of music both o going to and returning from the Parish Ch The Volunteer bugle band was also in atter:tr. in charge of Mr. Plinston, and the police-24 c. - stables, four sergeants, one chief inspector and two inspectors—were under the direction of the Chief Constable, Mr. Talbot. This is the first time the ambulance corps have figured in the procession. It consisted of 15 privates, one supernumerary, the Chief Superintendent, Captain Waring; Superinten- dent Secretary, Mr. G. Crosfield ; Superinten- dent of Stores, Mr. Hardy ; and Second Officer Mansseur. Superintendent Turner was in charge of the 16 firemen who attended.. Immediately preceding the Mayor were the Mayofte Sergeant and mace bearer (Sergeant Changer), and the sword bearer (Sergeant Dean). The Mayor Wag accompanied by the Deputy-Mayor on one side and Alderman Sutton on the other, Ile was followed by Aldermen Hutchinson and Roberts ; Councillors Heaton, Shaw Green, Dr Cannel!, Monks, Smethurst, Bennett, John Evans, Pierpoint, Lee, Jolley, Grace, Peacock, F R. Roberts, John Roberts and Baird, with Messrs Rogers and Malkin, who tied in ihe recent election in the Bewsey Ward. The Tore Clerk was unable to be present owing to indisposi- tion. The officials of the Corporation present were Mr John Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr Stanley Wilson (assistant to the Town Clerk), Mr Thomas; Longdin (borough sueveyor), Mr John Sutcliffe (assieunt borough surveyor), Mr G F F White (deputy clerk to the mawistrates), Mr Luke Talbot (chief constable), Mr Elijah Place (marke's inspec- tor), Mr Frank Tamar (superintendent of the Fire Brigade), Mr Robert Young (cemetery registrar), Mr W S Haddock (gas engineer), Mr Fred Taylor (secretary). Mr W S Howard (cashier), and Mr Charles Madeley (secretary, librarian, curator, &amp;C)se Amongst the townsmen present were —Messrs 'K Birchall, J.P.; J W Hayward. J.P., R T Fairclough, J.P , Messrs George Pilling (chairman of the Board of Guardian'), T II A-hcroft, Peter Cunliffee S Davies, It Forshaw, W Hickman, William Houghton (members of the Board), Mr Ward (master of the Workhouse), Mr A Wright (superintendent of the Padgate Schools), Mr T J Garnett (relieving officer No. 1 district), Mr F H Aukland (relieving officer No. 3 district),Mr Basil bhearer (assistant relieving officer and inquiry officer), Mr George Holford (vac- cination officer), and ',lessrs James Bolton, Thome! Birtlee, John Birtles,John Walton, William Dooley, R II Pearson, W Lawless, s N Green, James E Earle, Burton, Reid, Clare, Arthur Hatton,Thomas Pilkinge ton, H Isherwoed,William Gill, EJ Mitchell (mana- ger of the Liberal Club),G Watkins (manager of the Conservative Club), T Amos, Bickford, Greatrex (local inspector of the Soch ty for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals), and others. The church was reached at half-past ten, seat! being reserved on each side of the cenire aisle for those in the procession. The clergy who took part in it were the ft iTS. Canon Willis (Rector), A. A. Markham, Arthur F. White, and Gerald W. Dickin- son. Mr. W. S. Woods presided at the organ with his usual ability. 'THE RECTOR AND LAWLESSNESS IN THE CHURCH. The sermon was preached by the Rector from the words, " Render unto Cfeser the things which are Cmsar'e, and unto God, the things that are God's." St. Matthew xxii. SI. Cinon Willis sail be might be allowed on such at occasion as that to preface his address with a few words of welcome, of cons gratulation, and of condelence. Of welcome be- cause it was a metter of the sincerest satisfaction to all who were connected with that church to see present on that occasion the authorities of the town and the representatives of the State. Of cone gratulation because the Mayor was present on the second occasion for the second year of his office in, they thanked God, restored health. Of condolence because they followed to the grave the day befor yesterday, one who for nearly half a century le held office in that town, and was highly spoke of by all as a man of courage and as &amp; man whose one desire was to do his duty. Al. lusion was subsequently made by the Rector to the duty of the clergy to the State and to the Church. All people now-a-days, be said, read the new! papers. They all knew what interest had been shown with regard to many matters ecclesiastical of late. They knew what charges had been brought against many of them the cleegy of lawlessness. With all the force of soberest language he could command,he would rebut those charges of lawlessness, and he was sure that many who had studied the subject would be most careful how they made them. He asked them to consider that four judgments had been given in the civil courts, of which two had been decided one way, and two the other way, and when they found that the rubric way open in tbe opinion of the highest authorities and the greatest experts to various interpretations, when they re- membered that the late Archbishop of Canterbur9 had decided in favour of what they believed to boo right, how could men lightly accuse them of leer less.ness ? It might be a question of wisdom, it migbt be a question of reason, it might be a qu.ese tien, as it was, of charity—whether it; was 17_101 right, and kind to force a thing upon a people who were unaccustomed to it without the gravest consideration. He said again that it was a question whether it vras right, wise, and kind, and he would not hesitate to answer "No ;" but that was veil different indeed from the question of lawlessness. At the conclusion of the service the procession was re-formed, the churchwardens, sidesmen, and others now joining it. On arrival at the TOW° Hall the Mayor thanked the gentlemen present for accompanying him to church, particular praise being due to the Volunteers and to the Ambulan.ce Cerps. The appearance of the Ambulance Corp! was exceedingly praiseworthy, and reflected great credit upon Captain Waring, who he was also pleased to see present.</p>
        <p>THELWALL PA.RLSH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>An adjourned meeting of the Thelwall Parish COUSICII was held in the schaolroarn on Monday evening. At the previous meeting it was resolved that the village stream be piped up, levelled and put in a thoreughli sanitary condition,bat before taking any definita action the Council aereei that the matter should be brought to the notice o the District Council, and if thought dee sirable be dealt with by them. The meeting was there fore adjourned to Monday, when there were pre:ent the Rev. T. Pyre Williamson (chairman), Admiral J011,5' Perry, and Messrs. W. H. Mee.;ly, Norman, Tayltc Peter Warburton, G. Lembert, William Houghton, fl• Roebuck, J. Kite, and William Ford. with Mr. W. He Cooke (clerk).—Mr. Bleckly understood that tbe fiat reiolution rotating to the piping of the stream was negatived by tne resolution tete.. ring the matter to the District Council.-- Mr. Taylor observed that it was thoroughly ualerstood, that the resolution was to stand pending information re. the District Council from Admiral Parry.—Admiral Parry handed the following letter from Mr. J. Swintoes surveyor to the District Council, to the chairman r—'"' Replying to yours of the 18th inst., te pipe the straaq or pond with 18-inch pipes would cost about £l4, an to fill un the ground to suitable level with the road an° the cinder !metal?, about £8 more. I don't think re deicing the stream to 18 or 20 inches would be yea sati4actere. as there would still be a nuisance in thO summer months. I think the best and cheapest school°. would be to lay a six-inch drain, say abaut 150 'inset yards and pick up the chief drains whieh pollute the stream, and the outlet of thi; to be into the ;street* again below the road platt or bridge. This could be done for Le or about is. 21. per yard and leave the village pond practically clear. You will no doubt remember that* commitiee from the Council met in June,lB9s,reapectini this pond, aid decline I to do anything to it, and agree that it was more a matter for the Parish Council or the , adjoining owner to deal with, and you promised to deo - the needful.—Admiral Parry observed that he and t agent would join them in narrowing the stream. last paragraph in the letter meant that the v't trict Council would not take over the matte!. After some diseusiion Mr. Tayler moved that his origin resolution stand.—Mr. Roebuck seconded Mr. Taylo motion.—Mr. Blrekly observed that it would be ince° injudicious on the part of the Council to rush into expense. Tney ought to accept the offer made ~I); Admiral Parry. He should oppose the motion er..°4l fore the meeting because he Was not willing te landing the Council into a large expenditure by Ince'L. ring a law suit.—Mr. Taylor's motion was then the mooting and °ivied by eye votes t three, Clerk was instructed to obtain tenders for .the pip and levelling of the gw re with two•foot pipe. o :</p>
        <p>the t rehip lurch. dance ' Coe' 11 :) t sd zeig •</p>
