      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>latFrs to tqc OZittor.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PHILHARMONIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am desired by the Committee of the above Society to exams] through yeur paper their very teit thanks to the lady members of the Society, their lady friends, ard to all who so kmdly assisted in making the bazaar such a success. It is mainly through the hearty way in which the ladies co-operated that the Society has been freed from debt and put on a firm footing once more. The Committee also wish to thank the public for their kind patronage and support. I am, yours truly, J. BOOTH, Hon. Secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>RUNCORN scaooL BOARD ELECTION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN scaooL BOARD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Allow me to trespass upon your valuable space to answer "Curiosity " a correspondent of a contempor- ary of yours. He states: " I should be very glad if someone would explain why this contest is to be forced upon the town. For several years the two great parties have been content to be equally represented on the Board leaving the odd seat to the Roman Catholic, and the Board has done its work well. The supporters of Volun- tary schools are quite content that this arrangement should continue, but the so-called undeno-ninational candidates evidently are not. Why is this ? Why is the town to be put to all this expense ? Is it because the Board has not properly done its work, or is it to gratify the wishes of one or two ambitious people ?" In explanation I wish to state that personally it is my opinion that such an arrangement, which clearly gives the "S.ctarians" the upper hand, is no satisfactory. And if it can be brought to an end much will result from it of benefit to the Runcorn ratepayer. The chief gain will be economically,f er it is very evident that the supporters of Voluntary &amp;be .I's are guilty of many enormities one of which will suffice for illustration. They love to compare the cheapness of the Voluntary System with the costliness of the School Board System. Therefore they are, as administrators of the School Board System, excessively extravagant. And further, it is high-time that the management of the School Board should be taken out of the hands of the opposition. It would never do to let your rival run your "store" for you.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>"TIME."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>WARRINGTON,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MARKETS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday new wheat (white) tetched 44 01 to 43 31 (701 b to the bushel) ; red wheat, 33 lid to 43 24 ; new oats, 2s to 23 41. Flour, best seconds, per 2401b5, 21s to 22s Od. Bran, lid to Is per 201 b. In the Warring- ton Provision Market, prices were as follows ! Fresh butter. 19 to 19 21 per lb; fresh eggs, 7 for I+3; fowls 4s 61 to 54 per couple ; ducks, 51 to 53 61; English beef, 51 to 104; mutton, 61 to 0.1 ; veal 8d to 101 ; pork, 61 to 81. FOR EVENING WEA.R. Late,t Novelties in Silks, Alpacas. Nun'a Veilings, Fans, Gleves,and Feather Boas. W. T. WILLILMS, Sankey-atreet,Lezh-street,Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>, WYCLIP'FE P.S.A. SOCIETY. – • •</head>
        <p>, WYCLIP'FE P.S.A. SOCIETY. - • •</p>
        <p>-- There was the usual meeting of this society on Sunday aftern4on. Mr. Wood. of Warrington, de- livered a brief address in which he touched upon the evils of intemperance. The speaker for the afternoon was Mr. King, of Cheetham, Manchester, and he spoke upon lessons to be leant from failures. Mr. F. Fieldhouse gave an eff-ctive rendein ef the solo "The Singers." Mr. B. Farrar, assistant secre- tary announced that the officers for the P.S.A. sick club had been appointed and that it had been de- cided to commence on December 4th. Tie service was also taken part in by Mr. Wallington and Mr. B. Atkinson. ANft. or CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINHEIAL</p>
        <p>WATERS.—Advt. BRICK-STREET MISSION BAND OF HOPE Focurtv. The second monthly entertainment in connection with the above was given in the schoolroom on Tuesday evening. There was a large attendance,and the entertainment was much appreciated. The presi- dency of the meeting was in the able hands of Mr. S. B. Stringer, and in the course of a short address, which was full of exhortation and ad vice,he sought to invert into the hearts of the young Band of Hope members, the important and beneficial result which they would enjoy by their strict adherence to the total abstinence pledge. The following recited : Lizzie Gallop. Willie Hill, Albert Mavh all, Cissy Gallop, Lily Maybe.ll, Alice Davies, and Lily Lewis. The juvenile choir rendered very creditably choruses entitled " Sign To-Night," " Stand like men," and " Merry Laughing Water" Cissv Holt sing sweetly the solo " Come ye Wanderers." Three amusing and instructive dialogues were also given, viz., " Whatever we may be," "Only the figure one," and "That mean old uncle" A hearty vote of tianks was accorded to Mr. Stringer.oroPosed by Mr. Wm. Wright, seconded by Mr. henry Jolley, and was suitably replied to. A pleasant evening's entertain- ment was brought to a conclusion by singing the Doxology. The Band of Hope Society is in a very flourishing condition and has a large membership, STRANGE DEATH OF AN OLD WORKHOUSE INMATE. The death occurred at the hospital on Saturday morning of Henry Platt, 72 years of age, who has been an inmate of the Warrington Workhouse for 18 years. About a month ago Platt was lifting a heavy form, when it slipped and fell on his toe. He took no notice of it at the time,but a fortnight later he presented himself to the doctor at the In- firmary, and it was then found that his foot was in an advanced state of gangrene.— The Coroner (Mr. F. A. Jones) held an inquest eela tive to the death at the Poliee Parade BOOM on Monday evening.—Eleanor Walsh, daughter of the deceased, said her father was a butcher by trade. She saw him on the Thursday before he died. He did not complain. She heard him say "Oh ! my toe."—Nurse Gertrude Blease said the deceased died at the Infirmary on Saturday at 12.45 a.m.—The Coroner said the deceased seemed to have made no Complaint to anyone. He (the Coroner) had received a letter from Dr. Braide reporting the death, and saying the cause of death was senile gangrene. The deceased had applied to him for treatment and later had gone to the Infirmary, and under the circumstances he thought he ought to report the case to him.—The jury brought in a ver- dict of " Accidental death." LO.G.T., No. 1 CONVENTION, SW. LANCASHIRE On Saturday the monthly session of the above convention was held in the Central Hall,Wigan,and there were representatives present from all parts of the Convention area. Bro. Taylor, C.C.T. (Earles- town), opened the proceedings at 3.45. After the appointment of the Press and Credential Commit- tees, Bro. Bowler (Ashton) read the Secretary's re- port,which showed a slight increase. Sister Wools, C.S.J.T. (Earlestown), reported an increase of 63 juveniles, and urged the members to render more assistance to the S J.T.'e. The C.E S., Bro. Smith (Ashton), reported that the lodges had been very active at the brewster sessions and at the muni- cipal elections. The report of the Executive Com- mittee showed that a new lodge is about to be opened at Golborne, and that weak lodges bad been visited. Bro. Tomlin,C. Treasurer (Warrington). re- ported a balance in hand of .£2 93. 7d. The Con. vention decided to hold its next meeting at Leigh, under the auspices of the" Alpha" Lodge. Bro. Rev. J.A. Lee, D.C.T., P.G.Chaplain (Liverpool).gave an address on the work of the order in general. After a vote of thanks to the Wigan members for the arrangements, the meeting adjourned for tea. After tea a public meeting .was held, when there was a large attendance. Rey. Mr. Hodgson presided, and songs,Stc., were given by various members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>_ ELLESJITSUE -ST EIRET MISSION.</head>
        <p>_ ELLESJITSUE -ST EIRET MISSION.</p>
        <p>A concert was given in aid of RP .e9niere-streat Mission on Monday evening in the Bold-street Wes- leyan Sunday School. Unfortunately, the weather was unfavourable, and the audience was not a large one. Mr. John Taylor presided, and in a brief address, spoke on the needs of the mission. The programme was opened with a song by Miss Lily Jowett, entitled " River Dreams." and she also con- tributed "The Vesper Hymn," both being very tastefully rendered. Mr. Davies gave two oboe solos, "The Lost Chord" and "Minuet." and a xylophone solo was contributed by dr. Fred Ran- dall—" The Washington Post" who received a very hearty encore. He also played a violin solo entitled "The broken raelody and a merry game." Mrs. D. S. Stone's song," Pearls," ,nreativ pleased the audi- ence, and she responded with "Daddy." "The dear Homeland " was her other contribution. Mr. Arthur Griffiths received the approbation of the audience for his rendering of "The Island of Dream.," and" Our Jack," and in. response to an encore for the former song, he replied with " Johnny Sauna." Mr. F. Ford executed two cornet solos, " Queen of the Barth" and " Fantasia," very pleasingly, and Mr. Llewellyn Pearce was respon- sible for a couple of songs entitled " B andolero " and "Jack's the boy for me." He also took i . part n a duet with Mr. Griffiths, entitled " Excelsior." A musical trio; " True Blue," was given in good style by Miss M. Randall (mandolin). Mr. Percy Randall (mandolin), and Master Bertie Randall (pianoforte), and they were very heartily encored. Mr. Percy Randall contributed a humorous song in excellent style, and be was, of course, encored. The concert concluded with the singing of the National Anthem, Mr. J. W. Jowett presided at the piano.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <p>141.,:1P., , - i ,* tl. 1 , , ill, t' i? It, ( 1 ; 1 1 4 ~ , t 1 , I i 4 Ss i _ ^v</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>At the usual weekly meeting of this Society on Sunday there was a very fair attendance. Mr. Markland presided, and an interesting address was given by Mr. T. K. Thompson of Wigan. Vocal selections were given by Miss Stronach, and recita- tions by Mr. C. Drew. Mr. S. Dring presided at the piano. INJURED WHILST PLAYING FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon a boy named William Linaker, about 17 years of age and living at Shef- field Row, Vulcan, was playing football with some more boys when he slipped and fell, breaking lois thigh. Some of the boys he was playing with, at once conveyed him on the ambulance t 3 the War- rington Infirmary, and he is now progressing favourably. VULCAN WIISLRYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>There was a very successful gathering on Satur- day last in the above school room when Ur. Jelin Hartley of Manchester paid another visit to give a lime light entertainment in aid of the school funds. The stories of " Bob the Shoeblack " and "True as Steel," were highly appreciated by a full school On the following Sunday Mr. Hartley conducted a gospel lantern service which drew together a very large congregation in the evening. A collection was taken at the close in aid of the schools. EIRLESTOWN WESLEYAN JUVENILE MISSIONARY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>COLLECTORS.</head>
        <p>COLLECTORS.</p>
        <p>The annual gathering of the Earlestown Wes- leyan Juvenile Missionary collectors was held in the schoolroom on Saturday afternoon last. Tea was provided at which about 40 of the collectors were present. Games, songs, recitations, &amp;c. were entered into both before and after tea, and a very enjoyable afternoon terminated by presenting each with a bag of nuts and sweets, kindly given by two gentlemen friends of the mission and to whom the children recorded their very best thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>POLICE CASES.</head>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestowa Police Station on Monday morning before Mr. C. B. F. B3rron, Joseph Tapley was charged with being drunk at Newton on Satur- day night and was fined 21. 6d. and costs. James Hyland and Michael McHugh for being drank on Sunday were each fined 2s. 61. and costs. Michael Howley for being drunk at Winwick on Saturday was also fined 23. 6J. and costs. On Tuesday morning before Mr. Borron at New- ton, Henry Greenwood of Bolton was committed for soven days for lo?ggin;.; in Bridge-street. &amp;tries- town. P.C. Gathercombe proved the case,and Henry Murray, of 41, New Top-road, Walkden, was com- mitted for .seven days for being drunk and disor- derly in Bridge-street, Earlestown, on Monday.— P.C. Gathercsmbe proved the case. 1.0.G.T. HOPE OF EARLESTOWN LODGE.</p>
        <p>On Monday last the above lodge held its weekly meeting, presided over by Bro. T. Cutter. Five candidates were admitted and several proposed. Bra. J. W. Robert., D. Councillor, of Warrington gave a very interesting address on the work of the Order. Digew; of District Lolge to be held at St. Helens on Saturday, December 20th, was read and the representatives instructed thereon. Pro- gramme for the evening 'Topical Temperance talks," "The 1.0.G.T., What it is, and what it does." Cards were handed round to all the members with questions and answers, Bro. J. Taylor was chairman Thanks were tendered to the chairman tor the arrangements made for the meeting. P.S.A. Cnorn.</p>
        <p>In aid of the funds of the above choir a concert and illuminated lecture was given in the Primitive Aethadist schoolroom, Earlestown, on Saturday evening. There was a very fair attendance of the public. The concert programme was as follows "Friend of the, brave," Mr.S.Dring ;recitation, "The Fireman's Wedding," Mr. Robinson ; song, " Should he upbraid," Miss Marion Halt ; recita- tion, " Ambrose Jul," Mr. Robins ,n ; song, "Genevieve," Miss Marion Holt • song, " Queen of the E trth," Mr. S. Dring. Miss 'Williamson accom- panied. The lecture was "A. tour to Grindelwald," by Dr. Valentine, being really the recounting of a visit paid by Dr. Valentine some short time ago, to Switzerland. The tour was illustrated by 50 beauti- ful lime-light pictures, which lent additional in- terest to the description given by the lecturer. EARLEST3WN BAPTIST</p>
        <p>Special services in connection with the anniver- sary of the above place of warship were held in the church on Sunday. The Rev. EL V. Williams Occu- pied the pulpit morning and evening and preached appropriate sermons. In the afternoon a P.S A. was held when selections of vocal and instrumen- tal music were given by the choir. An address was also given by the Rev. H. V. Williams. On Monday evening the annual tea was held in the schoolroom, but there was only a poor atten lance. After tea an adjournment was made to the chapel, lwhere the usual annual meeting was held. Mr. Griffiths, of Liverpool, presided, and he was sup- ported by the Revs E Bocock (Primitive), J F Matthews (Latchford, Baptist), and E F Miller. The subject of the various addresses given was " Eng- land's debt to Nonconformity." The Rev E Bocock took the first part of the subject "Truths that have been maintained." The Rev J F Matthews spoke on " The civil and religious liberties which have been secured," and the R3O F E Miller on "The men wha have been produced." To Nonconformists the snbject was most interesting, and made doubly se by the clever manner in which it was handled by the various speakers. During the evening members of the choir gave several selections. FATAL RESULT Or PLAYING WIT/I MATCHES. At the Railway Hotel Earlestown, on Monday, Mr. Jones, deputy coroner, held an enquiry into the , circumstances attending the death of Gertrude McDermott, of 36 Derby-st., dauguter of Mr. and Mrs McDermott, which took place on Friday morn- ing, the result of injuries received from being set on fire through playing with matches. It appeared from the evidence that about 10 minutes to three on the afternoon of Thursday the 10th inst , the deceased child was in the back kitchen with the eldest sistr, Mary Ann, and her mother, playing. The child went upstairs, leaving her sister and mother in the kitchen, and going into the middle bedroom occupied by the boys, gat hold of a box of 'matches. Whilst playing with these they seems.' to have ignited and set fire to the child's cotton pina- fore. The sister Mary Ann, hearing screams ran upstairs and found deceased in flames. She celled her mother, who also ran quickly upstairs, and wrapping a quilt round the unfortunate child carried her downstairs. Mrs. Perkin, a neighbour came running in ant applied linseed oil and lime water. Drs. Valentine and Mouncey were sent for and arrived in 15 minutes. The latter attended the child up to its death which took place at 4-45 on Friday morning. Deceased was shockingly burnt on the face, neck, chest, arms and legs.—A verdict of accidental death was returned. MASONIC INSTALLATION AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>An installation ceremony in connection with the Makerfield Lodge No. 2,154, of Freemasons was held in the Town Hall, Newton, on Tuesday after- noon. Thera was a large attendance of brethren including Brothers C Burchall, P.M PG. S., 1,035, T Yarwood, P.M P.P.A.G.D.C, 1,354, W Harrison, P.M.P.P.G.S,267—Sutton P.M Warrington,Whitten- bury-Kaye, S.W. 484, T S nith, Chap, 2,404, J Banks J.D, 484, J Fairclough, S. 484, F J Dell, W Walker, W H Arnott P.M. P.P.J.G. W. H Stones.P.M.P.P.S.G.D R Brierley, P M.P.P.G.S. of W P Garton, T Ormand, P.M. The installing master was Brother E Fairclough P.M, and with the usual ceremony he invested C S Bennie, W.M. The Brother Bodmer was presented with a handsome P.M. jewel. The W. M. afterwards invested his officers as follows, C H Bodmer, I.P.M ; .1 Sankey, S. W ; J Gooding, J. W; J Singleton, treasurer; A W Ander- ton, secretary; J U C Starkie, S.D ; J Dodd, J.D ; W H Arnett, ; J Prescott, organist; B Corlett I.G ; T Graham, J Williamson, J H Lane and T Barnes (stewards) R Cummings (Tyler). Tbe brethren at the conclusion of the ceremony pro• needed to the Lion Hotel, Warrington, where the festival of St John was celebrated by a sumptuous banquet prepared in the Hostess's bast style and to the entire satisfactron of the brethren. Between 50 and 60 assembled round the festive board. After the meal tne W.M. gave the following toasts which I were duly honoured:—"H.R.H. the Prince of! Wales," "The Right Hon the Earl of Lathom," and "V.W.D. Prov. G.M. Brother R Wylie and Prov Officers," the latter responded to by Bro. Burchall. The other toasts were "The W.M," "The 1.P.M," "The installing Master," "The visiting brethren " responded to by P.I. Sutton and Whittenbary Kaye. "Masonic charities," "The P.M's of the lodge," "The officers of the lodge. 2155," and "The Tyler's toast."At intervals the following programme was submitted "Life is a river" Brother W W Shirley; "Four jolly Smiths," Brother F J Dell; recitation "The Scotch boy'a lament o'er his lost whittle," Brother J II Lane; song "Our Jack's come home "Brother B Corlett ; "The Powder Monkey," Brether W Walker; "Monks of Brother A ICaunce; "Maid of Athens," Brother F J Dell; "Friar of orders grey," Brother W W Shirley; "Admiral Tom" Brother B Corlett; "The sea is England's glory." Brother A Caunce. Theproceedings through._ 013 wore of a moat enjoyable character,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 18 1898. NOTES ON GAIVIES AND I WARRINGTON PARISH ATHLETICS. CHURCH BAZAAR. OPENING BY THE DUKE OF WESTMINSTER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 18 1898. NOTES ON GAIVIES AND I WARRINGTON PARISH ATHLETICS. CHURCH BAZAAR. OPENING BY THE DUKE OF WESTMINSTER.</p>
        <p>Since Wednesday afternoon a bazaar has been conducted in the Parr Hall, Warrington, in connec- tion with the Parish Church. She proceedings have commenced each day at 230, and they have been generously supported by all classes of the com- munity. The object in view was to raise £l,OOO for the church, missions, and schools. In outlast issue we spoke of the several institutions which are to benefit by the bazaar, and the reasons which had caused the deficiencies in the accounts. Briefly stated, £3OO is needed to wipe off the debt on the offertory account; £230 to pay off the loan on St, George's Mission ; £ll5 to make S. Clement's Mis- sion tree of debt; and £l5O for voluntary schools. The promoters of the bazaar were fortunate in securing the services of the Duke and Duchess of Westminster to perform the opening ceremony. Considerable interest was manifested in their Graces visit to Warrington, and it did much to ensure a large and distinguished gathering on the opening day. Tile Duke and Duchess arrived at Bank Quay Station by the 2.18 train, and were met on the platform by the Rev. Canon and Mrs. Willis, Cap- tain H. It. d' A. Willis, and the Re r.A. A. Markham. They proceeded to the station entrance, where there was a police guard of honour, under the direction of Chief Inspector Phillips. Their Graces, accom- panied by Canon and Mrs. Willis, proceeded by car- riage along Parker-street, Sankey-street, Bridge- street, and Rylands-street to the Parr Hall. Large crowds of people awaited the arrival at the station and at Parr Hall, and the route was in places thickly lined. A number of tradesmen and residents along the line of route displaye a large number of flags from their windows, and there was also an effsctive display in Rylanis-street and in front of Parr Hall. Flags were hoisted at the Town Hall and at the Conservative Club. The arrival at Parr Hall attracted a large concourse of spectators. The Mayor (who wore his robe and chain of office),stood in the door- way, arc )mpanied by the Mayoress, Mrs Lyon, Dr White, and other well-known ladies and gentlemen, who were introduced by the Rector to their Graces. The Mayor's sergeant and mace bearer (Sergeant Chan ler), and the sword bearer (Sergeant Dean) were also in attendance, and lent additional attractiveness to an already brilliant gathering. On the platform, in addition to the Duke and Duchess, there were the Mayor and Mayoress, Cation and Mrs. Willis, Captain 11. R. d' A. Willis and Mrs. Willis (Present), Mrs. Lyon, Mrs. Baamont, Mrs. Pickmere, Mrs. Jahn White, Miss Pierpoint (St. Austin's), the ladies engaged at the stalls, the Revs. A. A. Markham, A. F. White, and G. W. Dickinson, Mr. James G. Craik (rector's warden), Mr. John Dewhurst (people's warden), Messrs. S. Davies, W. Price, F. Taylor, S. Taylor, Thomas Mason and Thomas May. Mr. W. S. Woods played several appropriate volun- taries on the organ. Pilot to the commencement of the proceedings Mr. 1. Births photographed the company. Miss Dorothy Browne (niece of the Mayoress, and daughter of Mr. Arthur Browne), then presented a very hqn Isom," saquet (supplied by Miss Rigby, florist of Bridge-street) to the Duchess, which her Grace accepted with thanks. Master Tom Craik (son of Mr. Craik, Rector's warden) presented the Duke with the official hand- book of the bazaar, and in doing so he said : May it please your Grace to accept this book as a sou- venir of our bazaar. ____</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>OPENING CERMIONY.</head>
        <p>THE OPENING CERMIONY.</p>
        <p>The RECTOR said before calling upon his Grace the Duke of Westminster to declare the bazaar open he wend as briefly as possible state the present position of affairs; of what had been done and what remained to be done. During the past year £14,000 had been raised, Partly by do iations of money and land and partly by the sale of school heildings to the Training College. That sum of £ll,OOO had been expended in the following way : £7,000 upon Fairfield schools ; £3,000 upon Ellesmere-street schools • £2,000 upon St. George's Missio and £2,000 upon St.diernenc's Mission. They now needed for present necessities £l,OOO more; £3OO for the church, £2OO for St. George's, £lOO for St. George's school, £lOO for St. Clement's Mission, and £3OO f street making at the Fairfield schools. He should not be doing right if he did not tell them that they really re- quired more than £l,OOO. They had been told by the Government to enlarge the Ellesmere-street sohool, and he knew that before long they would have to extend the Fairfield scheols. The parish was growing rapidly and he felt that they would have to build another mission, and throngh the kindness of Mr. Ireland Blackburn° they had obtained a site. (Hear, hear.) Referring to his Grace the Duke of Westminster, tee R'ctor said he was known to them as a very distinguished noble, as a true English gentleman, as a most shrewd and popular landlord, and as a great and good man. (Applause). His beautiful hall and grounds had been seen at various times by nearly all of them ; a delight +itch they who lived in a town greatly appreciated. By his Grace's kindness and liberality he thought Eaton Hall was al- most as well-known to the public as to the owner.(Hear, hear). They did not forget that the Borough of War- rington was partly in the County of Chester, nor did they forget that Manchester and Liverpeol were the two well-grown daughters of a venerable mother to whom they all looked up with just pride and she was Chester. (Applause.) He would now call upon the Duk of West- minster to declare the bazaar open. . . -</p>
        <p>The DCHCE-of WESTMINISTER, who had a most cordial reception said he had to thank them for giving himself and his wife an opportunity of coining there that day. He taiga begged to thank Canon Willis for the very kind words he had used with regard to him. He had always had some difficulty in getting rid of the idea that War- ington was in the county of Cheshire, but he was not aware that a little bit of it was ache illy in the county of Cheshire. Therefore he oweejl them a leas apology for being there that day After all the main part of the borough was only divided by a limpid etre trn—(laught3r) —and a still more limpid stream that had been cut through the district during recent years. (Renewed laughter.) However, they had no insuperable difficulty in getting there that day, reach- ing it in about 20 minutes from s Cheater. For these reasons h 3 could not (pal get rid of the idea that Warrington was a part of the county of Cheshire, and that he was not altogether in the wrong place in being present on that auspicious occasion. (Applause.) The town of Warrington was so happily sithated that he re- nv ntbered in the old days—the days of the cotton famine when such dire distress swept over the kingdom—that Warrington was the more favourably situated in that it d'pendel not upm cotton alone, but upon its various Industries, which it maintained up to the present lay. And th 3 Mayor told him that the population, which had for a long time been a large one, was increasing rapidly still. That showed, of course, that the wants of the Church, whether with regard to the fabric or to the spiritual ministrations, were inereas- km, and that they must keep pace with the times. He imagined that during the next few days the amount required would ba quite within the compass of the monetary powers of the people of Warrington, not for- getting the kind neighbours who lived over the limpid stream to which he had already alluded, because Cheshire took a great part in whatever interested War- rington don amore ' • and that bazaar was for the benefit of the old town of Warrington. (Applause). A great deal might be said about bazaars. In some quarters they were not quite so highly approved of although they were a legitimate way of getting money for a legitimate object. His own experience tended to the conclusion that whatever money they wanted to raise by these bazaars was always forthcominss ; and with regard to the articles displayed—not here, 6but in ether cases—if people were willing to give a larger stun than the original cost of an article, it was their look out. But these bazaars meant a great deal of hard work, and it was, therefore, quite right that they should reap success. In order to benefit such institutions as the Parish Church, missions and schools, it was their duty to do all they could, and his present duty, in obedience to the summons of the Rector, was to declare that beau- tiful bazaar now open and to request them—it was hardly necessary to ask them to do so—to spend all their money, and a little bit more than they had available in purchasing all the articles in the room. (Laughter and applause.) The MAYOR said he had been requested to propose a vote of thanks to their Graces the Duke and. Duchess of Westminster for so kindly coming there that day. He was sure they all owed them a deep debt of gratitude, seeing that their Graces had so many calls upon them. They were net only indebted to them on the present oc- casion but for many kindnesses in the past. (Hear, hear.) He hoped that during the next four days the debt which hung over their Parish Church might be cleared ; and that theladies might have a full and satin- factory roward for the labours they had put forth. (Ap- plause). Mr. JOHN DEWITT:MST seconied ties vote of thanks. He said he had been closely cennected with the Parish Church and its work all his life, and as people's war- den he could sneak feelingly of the debt which hung over the church, missions and schools. After referring to their indebtedness to the Duke and Duchess of West- minster he proceeded to point out that they were engaged in a great work ; that of religious education. He hoped that they would not only raise £l,OOO, but also be able to have a "little nest egg " in addition.</p>
        <p>The vote was carried with acclamation. The DIME of WESTMINSTER in responding said he re. ' membered spending a delightful afternoon the last time he visited Warrington. It was in connection with the Training College where the future mistresses of ele- mentary education were so admirably trained. It waS an afternoon he should never forget. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>LOCAL BREACH OF PROMISE CASE. --- AN EARLESTOWN DEFENDANT. ALLEGED HEARTLESS CONDUCT. THE JUDGE AND SOLICITORS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL BREACH OF PROMISE CASE. --- AN EARLESTOWN DEFENDANT. ALLEGED HEARTLESS CONDUCT. THE JUDGE AND SOLICITORS.</p>
        <p>A young lady of the age of 21, dressed entirely in lelactr, appeared in the role of plaintiff in the Nisi Prius Court at the Manchester Assizes, before Mr. Justice Bigharn on Wedaesday in an action for breach of promise. Her name was Mary Jane Weston; the , defendant was Richard Ball,a joiner. She lived with leer , father at Leigh, and he at 14, Earl-street, Earlestowe. 1 Mr. M'Keever and Mr. De Soberon represented the plaintlf, and Mr. Roe Ryecroft the defendant. The plaintiff's story as related by Mr. M'Keever was pathetic enough, for she was a mother, and the defen- dant was alleged to be the father of the child. In res- pect of this circumstance the father of the plaintiff, James Weston. claimed damages from the defendant for tne seduction of his daughter. It appears according to Mr. M'Keever's statement that in August, 1895, the defendant, whilst at Belfast, wrote a letter to the plaintiff, and on his return in Sep- tember they began to keep c Imp any toeeteer. lathe course of events he asked the plaintiff to become his wife and on September 18th, 1895, they became en- gaged, and they kept company afterwards. Sometime in the summer of 1896 the defendaat went to lode at the plaintiff's father's house, and on October, 1896, the parties being still engaged, he seduced the plaintiff under promise of marriage. He continned tg live at Mr. Weston's house until Christmas. When the girl dis- covered her condition she spoke to the defendant about it, and he premised to marry her straight off. Instead of doing so he kept putting it off,and in March the girl's mother became aware of the facts, and spoke to the de- fendant about it. He, however, still made , excuse, bet continued to live at the house ' until about the end of April, 1897, when, being in the Army Reserves, he went Southport. Mrs. Weston was greatly distressed at her daeghter's disgrace ; her health gradually declined, and at last she lay on her death bed. Even than the theueh's of her daughter were with her, and it was her dying request to the defendant chat he should marry her. This,in the pessmce of a Mrs. Jeckson, he promised to do. It sub- sequently transpired that on one occasion the girl told the defendant that there was only one thing for her— the workhouse—bit the defendant assured her that he would marry her at once. Immediately the funeral was over the defendant behaved in a most scandalous man- ner. He borrowed the money from the girl's father with which to buy the wedding ring, which was left in the custody of the girl herself. But he continued to make excuses for not carrying out the coetract,altheuele last Christmas he visited the girl at heir father's house at Leigh in the capacity obviously other future husband. He asked her for the ring, wrote a letter to the plaintiff, and on his return in September they began to keep company together. It subsequently happened that the defendant asked plaintiff to become hie wife. They become engaged on September 18, 1895, and kept company together afterwards. Some time in the summer of 1896 the deco(' int went to lodge at the plaintiff's father's house, and in October he seduced the girl under promise of marriage. He con- tinued to lodge at the house. At this time, however, the defendant was engaged to another girl, whom he subsequently married at Earlestown on the 10th of January. He paid his attentions to the plaintiff even after his marriage, and the girl was oblivious of the fact. Counsel concluded by stating that the girl had been treated in a most shameful manner, and aethoegh the parties were bete in a humble positien in life lee appealed to the jury to give the plaintiff as substantial damages as they thought she could xecever from him considering his circumstances. With regard to the actee for damages for seluctioe brought by the father of the girl he submitted that he was entitled tO them because it happened in her father's house and, teeeni- celly speaking, whilst in his service. The plaintiff in her evidence corroborated these fees. She acknowledged having received a letter from the defendant, who, writinv from Warrington,where he was working at the Royal Court Theatre, said that a friend, whom be referred toss Ernie, intended to get married at the same time. It was galling him to death, he said, for him to be at Warrington. H 3 concluded very affec- tioaally. "Good night, darling girl," he wrote, " your leveed and loving Dick. She ;died that she visited the defendant at E %Hest° wn, an in the course of a stroll in the perk she put the question to him as te when he was going to marry her. He asked her why she kept nothering him, and sail," You knew very well I will merry you." Later on, however, he told her that his mother wanted him to marry another woman. With regard to the welding ring incident, the plaintiff made a rather amusing cenfession. The defendant, it seems, gave her a set of spoons and asked her far the ring, as he said "it is the place of the bridegroom to keep the ring till the wadding day ; end the bride's place te keep the spoons." Tney walked out continually to- gether, she said, after her chill had been born. Mr. Ryeeroft did not call any evidence. The Judge, in summing up, ell I tooth these actions were brought against an ordinary working man of small and uncertain wages, and whatever the verdict he could not suppose that it would result in any benefit to the woman, who hid but one action, or to her father who had brought the ether. In cases of this kind the only persona who could gain anything were the solicitor I. Their might fellow this defendant and get out of him some little costs, but to suppose that the girl or her father would ever get anything was to suppose what ex3erinace told them was not at all likely. For hi 3 part be regretted that such actions should be brought. They were net bro.' sht in the real interests of the plaintiffs. He did net think it would mike the least difference whether the jury gave the girl a million pounds damages or ten pounds. The jury degber ate a few minutes arid then returned a verdict for the plaintiffs—damages in the girl's MO of £75, and in the case of her fa'her £l5. THE LATE REV. H. MONK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PULPIT REFERENCES.</head>
        <p>PULPIT REFERENCES.</p>
        <p>At St. Peter's a.a-e-r-cii, 11-7wcon, -cra-S-Unday, the Rev. Canon Penrhvn, at the morning service referred in sym- pathetic terme to the late vicar. At Wargrave Church, the Rev. J. Carson, rector, preaching from the 16th chapter of St. [Ace, 2ad verse, " Give an account of thy stewardship," asked were not all mea stewards ? WAS it not certain that everyone of them might be called to give an account of their stew- ardship ; dil they not day by day lee one and another from amcngst them called. They were specially re- minded of this by the cell given to one well known in that neighbourhood, one who was not only a steward, and he referred to their neighbow, the late vicar in that capacity, but be was not only a steward as a Christian man, but a stwarl of stewards as a Ciristian pastor, he was a steward of the mysteries of God and God' work. We thin had before us a steward of stewards called to his dread acceunt, he had gone within the grave. But it afforded the great's': solace not only to his bereaved perish but to all their fellow Christians to know that in the 36 years of his ministry. God enabled him to be more or less faith- ful, and to make use of the ministry which he had received by the grace of God. In all his public dutps in God's sanctuary, he was retiring, eicaet and particular. As to his private capacity there was an aneieet charity belringine, to the Parish of Newton-ia- Makerfield of which the vicar of St. Pater's was a trustee and chairman, and they always met about St Thomas's day to make out the list of needy persons who had a claim on the charity, and he (the speaker) had been greatly struck year after year by the minute and sym- pathetic kers Wedge and intimate acquaintance which the vicar of St. Peter's had with every needy and skit Person in his parish. Hs seemed to know them well,and their particular neede, and i's a sick case he was a most welcome and sympathetic visitor. They were bound to offer their sympathy and to offer their prayers to Almighty God that he had put into the heart of the patron of the pariah to apunint a faithful successor.</p>
        <p>THE QUEEN AT-WARRING-TON. On Saturday Her Majesty the Queen passed through Bank Quay Station at 3.22 a.m. on her return journey from Balmoral to Windsor. There were three or four railway officials and a couple of policemen only on the platform while the royal train passed through. DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MISS PRRRIN. On Saturday afternoon the funeral took place at Bowdon Parish Church, of Miss Mary Perrin, formerly of Ashfield-road, Altrincham, whole death took place on Thursday at Southport after a com- paratively short illness. The deceased lady, who was 71 years of age, was the younger daughtsr of the late Mr. Josiah Perrin' of Warrington. She had resided for many years inAltrincham, and until her fatal illness manifested a strong intertPt in a variety of local movements, and a large circle of friends admired her for many excellent qualities. SERIOUS ACCIDENT TO A WARRINGTON FOOTBALLER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday in the course of a league football match at Newton, between Warring:on St. Mary's A.F.C. and Newton Athletic, a serious accident hap. pened to George Aspinall (21), son of Mr. W. Aspinall, landlord of the Railway Tavern, Bank Quay. Aspinall was keeping goal for the Warrington team and about five minutes from call of time the home- titers were pressing. It seems Broster one of the Athletic forwards sent a shot in, which brought Aspinall tohis knees. He was immediately pounced upon by the Newton men and knocked down with five of the forwards on top of him, and received serious internal injuries in consequence. He was removed to the Millstone Inn, and attended by Dr. Watkins. Subsequently he was conveyed home. His condition was regarded as very serious, and on Monday evening he was removed to the Infirmary, where his depositions were taken. He passed a fairly good night and is reported to be slightly bettor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>RUGBY FOO rsALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOO rsALL.</p>
        <p>I am beginning to think it is time I gave the Warring- ton Committee another prod with my pin. Tney profess to be on the 104 k-our for new men, but they are not very energetic in their endeavours to strengthen the team. Whenever a cornmittee-man is tackled on the subject he says that good men are difficult to find, and there is a prejudice in Warrington against Welshmen. But against these excuses we have the plain fact that otner clubs are making important captures. Oldham have added Tom Matcher, the Seatoa international, to their ranks quite recently. Thera are good men to be had if a genuine effort is made to secure them and a club possessed of money is not afraid of spending it. Kat this is the rub, and it is in this par- ticular that I am inclined to blame the Warrington Coin' mittee. They have money at their command and could have had a really capable team. by non if it had been expended wisely, and moreover tisere would have been a good return. There it no glossing over the fact that the gates this season are not se large as formAdy, and unless the team can strike a vein of success they will I continue to diminish. That vein will not he found by the present team. The threeqearter line is too weak altogether. None of the men are really fit substitutes 1 for Barber, Burton, and Broady. At the present moment I a couple of clever centres, who will play a centre game, are badly needed. There seem to be one or two prom- ising wings in the neighbourhood, but no centre-three- quarters fit for the p3ition. A little more weight f ere ward would be of great service too. Now that I have once more had my say I trust the Committee will rouse uo and ensure the complet 3 silencing of that murmur of discontent which is again being heard among the 1 football section of the community.</p>
        <p>The struggle at Warrington on Saturday between Warrington and Bmightla Ringers was Titanic, but though the hemesters did their very best they had to succumb to a superior team and a Jo:. of hard luck. It was an open secret that Wirrington were bent on doing what Salford did a fortnight before, when the Rangers lost their Arse two points this season. And as the match was on the Warrington grouod there was some chance of the horn esters succeeding in their endeivour. The impreved f )rm of the team in recent matches encour- aged the crowd, and there was a meet satisfactory " gate " ire cmsequence. Both teams were strmg,,ly re- presented.</p>
        <p>Warrineton made the pace a ' cracker " right from the commencem snt. fhey knew their game and they were determined to play it. By bustling forward tae- tics they hoped to make up for any lack of weight, and keep the burly Rangers from eacerisieg theirs too freely. Broaghton were soon on the d2f sasive, and Warrington were at it hamincr and tongs trying to break through. Bate ,„rie I all tee tricks ef the trade Wth considerable success, but did not receive adequate support from his three-quarters. The home forwards however were in rare fettle. The way they followed up every punt and downed the expe3tant Ranger was of immense sorvice to their side. Broughton appeared un- able to cope with the determined rushes .of their fors, and it seemed only a matter of moments ere a score was regi,terel. First Elolcroft, awl then Fish had a try but were kept out. Next minute the forwards were over the line with a rush, but unaccountably failed to notch a threepointer. If there were four Warrington men on the line together, and only one Ranger, the latter was sure to obtain pas3assion of the ball before it could be touched d uwn. rise Fates were against Warrington,and no matter how they strueelsd for supremacy they were unluckily robbed of the fruits of their labours at the critical moment. The score sheet was blank at half-time, when the effects of the struggle was visible on boll sides.</p>
        <p>For a few minutes Warrington looked like taking the upper hand again. once the ball W 4,3 rolling, but after a brie!' visit to the Broughton end they were driven back helter-skelter. A wild struggle ensued on the home line. Bate cleared the Ringers out, only to find them hick the next minute. Toe ball bobbed abut excitingly until " the lengthy Jackson" clasped it to his bofom and wathi throw:4h the home ranks over the line. It was a try, but what sort of a try ? Some say th3re was obstruction, and others talk of a pass forward, and I myself tbought there was something fishy abaut it, but toe try counted.</p>
        <p>Warrington's hard luck was now taking a definite form. Soon after this score Hallam's over-eagErne 8 causA him to miss a fair catch and have a scramble on the floor. He hal just secured the oval when the whistle sounded, Reforee Smith waved his ar n snaje3- tically, and the borne custodian was penalised for not playing the ball. Well ! to say folks were astonished does not describe matters at all. They were sitrinlV fiabblrga3ted, and when Nelsen kicked a goal he could hardly have missed with eyes shut, the comments about Mr. Smith were not of the drawing. room order. According to strict rule it may be correct to penalise a full-back for not playing the ball if he is plainly transgressing this rule, but it is a thing which seldom happens. I climate th 3 fairness or accuracy of Mr. Smith's flec,sion. Hallam was playing the ball to the best of his ability. It took him all his time to secure it and save from the Broughton forwards. And then for what was a plucky effort to stem disaster he was penalised under the posts. Es-en for any sort of in- fringement a referee is usually chary about giving a free-kick in front of goal, but Mr. Smith apparently ' thinks nothing of presenting a team with two points on the smallest pretest and that a doubtful one. I hope, for his own sake, he herd some of the candid comments of the crowd as he left the field.</p>
        <p>Though plainly disheartened, the homesters rouse up in fine style and paved the way for Holeraft ti score a brilliant try. Hopes of success had hardly been indulged in before Fate stepped in and administered another blow. Fish roads his only mistake of the afternoon,and Chapman profited by it, another try being registered against Warrington. All was over now, and tho close earns with the Rangers triumphant by 8 points to 3.</p>
        <p>lam ready to admit that the best team won, but without Mr. J. Smili and a lot of luck, I think the &amp;Lagers would hsve gone home latnenting. Warring- ton's determinaticn should have won them the game, and in the first bale they were most unfortunate in not scoring a goal and a try at least. They were superior at every point up to the interval. Afterwards their exertions told on them, and the Rangers by sheer strength and punting power assumed the aggressive with what result I have shown.</p>
        <p>Ho!croft's try was the bast of the match, and of the hems three-quarters he was the only one to acquit him- self with credit. Fists had little to do, Isherwood was selfish and played a very poor centre game, while Duck- worth showed unmistabeable weakness. Bate and Ashurst worked heroically behind the pack and were superior in tactics to their opponents. The forwards deserve nearly all th 3 praise however. for their play all along was m ignificsnt. They were a bit overweighted in scrimmags but shone brilliantly in the open. Hallam spoiled his p'av by over•eagerness and useless following up. He ran himself off his legs at times, and would do well to follow up less frequently, or only when the ball has gone high. He has no chance when the punt is a long one.</p>
        <p>Messenger and Nelson got through an immense am-mot of work, their defensive play being excellent. They were no so slow as at Salford the week before,and of course had a winning set of forwards before them. Jackson, the giant, was the best of the threequarters. Be is speedy, and resourceful, and Ho!croft had all his work cut out to watch him. Hacking was in his best form at full back, and punted with rare judgment and power. In fact at kicking the Rangers knocked War- ington into the proreflial cockscl hat. Arncng the for- wards Steel and Whitehead were the best.</p>
        <p>The po‘itilns of the dotal, and the result; of matches in the Lancashire League up to date are as follow : . .</p>
        <p>- . or. Against. P. W. L. IL G. T. P. GI. T. P. P. Widnes 9... 8... 0... 1 ..11 16— 73... 7 2 23...17 Broughton Rang 9... 8.. . 1.. . 0.. 16 28-116... 3 4 18...16 Salford Swinton Oldham Leigh 11.. 6... 5... 0...18 18— 90... 7 18— 68...12 St. Helene 10... 5... 5... 0...16 16— 80... 6 16— 60-10 Warrington Runcorn ....... Tydleeley 10i.. 2... 7._ 1_ 6 7 33...15 23 99... 5 Wigan Rochdale Hornets 8... 2... 6... 0... 9 3 27...17 21— 97... 4 Stockport 8... 1... 7... 0... 5 6-- 25...20 21-103 2 Morecambe 10... 0...10... 0... 2 2 10...13 27-107... 0</p>
        <p>Warrington have had to give way to Leigh and St. Helens, who have both played more matches and may yet be pissed. Still, to-day's fixture with Oldham 15 n?t likely to give Warrington a lift, though the charn- mons will find themselves possessed of no easy task. There is always hope, and shmld Dime Fortune look in Warrington's direction on this especial occasion—just to make up for last week—we might possibly get a point or twa. It is not very likely, but a game is never lost till it's w.m. and Warrington must remember this, and play like they did last Saturday. The threequarters will hays to smarten un however.</p>
        <p>SLEEP FOR SKIN-TORTURED FILBIES and rest for Tired Mothers in a warm bath with CUTICURA SOAP, And a single anointinz with CUTICURA, purest of emolli- ent skin cures. This treatment will afford instant relief permit rest and sleep to bath pa,ect and child, and points to a speedy, permanent, and economical cure in the most distreming forms of itching, burning, scaly, and crusted skin and scalp humours, with loss of hair, when all else fails.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>TRH STALLS AND STALLHOLDDRS.</head>
        <p>TRH STALLS AND STALLHOLDDRS.</p>
        <p>The sale of goods commenced immediately after Vie ceremony. No special design had bean followed in the arrangement of the stalls, but so far as tasteful decora- tion could lend effect to the scene it had boen very suc- cessfully utilized. The stalls contained a choice selection of saleable wares. Tha sans and shallholders were as follows :—(1) Mrs Willis and friends :—Mrs Lyon, Hon. Mary Cross' Miss Feilden, Miss Rosalind Brooke, Miss MacDougall of Lunge' Miss Lyon, Miss Brown, Miss Florence Brown, Mrs Coos, Miss Cash, Mrs Darwall, Mrs R Faireloulh, Misses Fairclough, Miss Massey, Mrs. Pickmere, Miss Pierpoint, Mrs. Morley Stevenson, Mrs Walton, Miss Boyle, Miss Francomb, Miss Cecilia Va Willis, Mrs F W Willis. (2.) The Misses White and friends :—Miss Bessie Davies, Misses Woods (Walton), Miss Kirkcnnnall, Miss Milling, Misses Margaret, Ethel, and Susan White. (3) Church Officers' Stall :—The Mayoress, Mrs. Craik, (Greenbank), Mrs Dewhurst, Mrs E Atkin, Mrs J D Buckton' Mrs Cooper, the Misses Craik, Mrs Davies, Mrs W Fairclough, Mrs May, Mrs W Price, Mrs F Taylor. Mrs S Taylor, Mrs James Wood. (4) The Teachers' Stall:— Miss Shaw, Mrs Stanfield, Miss Swards, Miss Bibby, Miss Wallbank, Miss Hether- ington, Mrs Barrett, Miss Boardman, miss Brundrett, Mss Locker, Miss MOSS, Miss Woods. (5) Flower St ill :—Mrs John Crosfield, the Misses Crosfield, Mrs Vero Davies. (6) Refreshment Stall :—Mrs John White, Mrs flair], Mrs Herbert Woods,the Misses Litton Miss Ridgway, the Misses Holford, Mrs Greener, Mrs Reginald Smith, Miss Gardner, Miss Mortimer, Miss Seeker. (7) Fish Pond :—The Miens Wright. (3) Fruit and Vegetables :—Mrs Persia, Mrs Hughes, Mies Constanc 3 Ward. The stalls hscl been kindly erected be Mr. R. A. Naylor, free of charge. The arrangements for the bazaar were entirely satisfactory, arid a meed of praise is due to Mr. John Dewhurst and Mr. W. Ernest Atkin (hon.secretaries to the bazaar), Mr.R.T.Fairclough (hon. treasurer), the Rector, his clergy and the com- mittee. _ _</p>
        <p>.—lifejion should be made of an autograph quill, the work of Mrs. J. G. Craik, wife of the war len. It con- tained the autographs of 983 persons, chiefly in War- rington, who we under nand paid the modest fee of one shilling to inscribe their names on the quilt. The oaMe3 had been w4rked in blue, rei and gold silk on a cell" ground and r,flTted great credit upon t le worker. We understand that the work was COMMInCOI in May !alt. and that the shilling subscriptions have ralisel Z5O, was exhibited at the bazaar at a charge of twopence, and was subs.quently raffled for. The centre pieoe Goa tained the names of the R ctor and his cler2y.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>TEA MEETING AND CONCERT AT TRE WESLEYAN CHAPEL —On Wednesday there was a tea meeting and coneert givers at the Flalton Wesleyan Chapel in aid of tho Flalton Bays' Brigade. The proceedings throughout, were eminently successful,and were very largely at- tended (the tea alone requiring two sittings). The pro- ceedings were promoted by Mr. Clark' s So :lolly Class for young women, and Mr. J. B. Houghton presided at the evening concert. During the interval the Chairman diAributel membership cards and non-commissioned officers' certificates, with two prizes (books) given by Captain Smith for full attendance at drills,which ha,l been won by Sergeant John Hankey and Private Ernest Tom- linson. A capital programme of songs, dial-)gues,reeita- tions and ins:rumental solos was submitted by the fol- lowing Tomlinson, Miss Higginson, Miss M A Hughes, Miss Ternperlay, Miss Ryalls, Mice A Looker, AtS3 M Newp,rr, Miss Asbury, Miss Goulden, MISS Barrow,Miss N Temperley, Miss Chadwick, and Messrs J 6 Clarke, P Rowlinion, F Hanky, A Saunders, J Barker, T Barrow, J Norburv, T Houlden, C P Bradbury, and T Johnson. Mr 11 Sutton was the able accom- panist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>SIR JOFIN BRUNNER, BA.RT., 3.ND TECHNICAL EDUCA.PION.</head>
        <p>SIR JOFIN BRUNNER, BA.RT., 3.ND TECHNICAL EDUCA.PION.</p>
        <p>A FURTHER GIFT TO RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Once again has the Runcorn Technical Instruc- tion Committee been laid under a d3lot of gratitude to Sir John Brunner, for getting them out of a dilemma. It appears that the Committee found themselves ia a diffiau lty with regard to the Art Schools established at the beginning of the session, inasmuch,as the Science and Art Department (South Kensington) objected to the rooms which are at pre- sent devoted to the art class, being used as part of the day school. Teis meant either the abandon- ment of the day schools, at a stage when it is be- ginning to assert its utility, or the return to the Science and Art Department of the building grant of £5OO. Thanks to Sir John Brunner, the difficulty will be surmounted, for he has generonsly offered to build a new wing to the existing building, which will provide the necessary acconamodaqoa for day school purposes, and thus the art rooms will be left free for their original purpwe. 'Sir John's generosity is the subject of general congratulation, and is an- other link in the affectionate connection between himself and the people of Runcorn. Already Sir John Brunner has contributed something like .C 1,700 towards Technical Instruction Baildings in Runcorn and district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD GOOD TEMPLARS.</p>
        <p>The Bonehill Branch Lodge held their weekly meeting on Monday night in the Temperance Hall, Knutsford-roid, Latchford. There was a large attendance of members, presided over by Brother Ditchfield, chief tenaplar. The programme was en- titled " A night of poetry." After the usual busi- ness several members gave recitations and songs,and the evening was enjoyably spent.</p>
        <p>THE LIVERPOOL WEEKLY Pour gives on Saturday. November 26. a Shee* Ai -,anac. with picture of St. 11,or2e's Hall and British ironcieds, end the first chap- ters of two new stories of very great interest, one of them called " The Fate of ztlary Paterson." a story founded on fact.— Advt.</p>
        <p>Sr. ALBAN'S ROMAN CATHOLIC Camtur, BEWSHY-</p>
        <p>STREET. We would remind our readers that the annual sermons will be preached in this church to-morrow (Sunday). The excellent choir of St. Alban's will render Ilaydn's famous 16th Mass in the morning at eleven. There will also be special service in the evening. The morning preacher will be Father Toohey, of Liverpool, and in the evening Father Bernard Vaughan will preach. No doubt the latter gentleman will have romething to say in reply to the attack made on him and his Brother the Cardinal, by the Vicar of Holy Trinity Church, in a local paper on November sth.</p>
        <p>SP/RITUALIST SOCIETY. At the Academy-street Temperance Hall on Satur- day evening a "social " was held promoted by one of the private " circles " of members connected with the society. About seventy people sat down to an excellent tea, after which Mr. R. Dolphin presided over the proceedings. Songs and Spiritualist hymns were sung by the members of the circle, and during the evening fruit and other refreshments were handed round to the guests. The whole of the provisions had been given, and the proceeds were to be devoted to the Lyceum funds. On Sunday afternoon Mrs. Porter. of Patricroft, delivered an address on " Spiritual gifts." In the evening the speaker took for her subject" The soul's dissatisfac- tion." After each address good clairvoyance was given. On Monday evening, after a short address, Mrs. Porter devoted the time to clairvoyance and psychometry.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL TOAST.—" Here's to John Bull, Pat, an Sandy in Grant's Morella Cherry Brandy !' Acknowledged by all to be delicious and comforting, and well calculated by its soothing properties to make all parties work together. for the benefit of the nation. Sold everywhere.— THOS. GRANT AND SONS Maidstone. Makers of Cherry Whisky, Sloe Gin, /cc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>BARLESTOWN PRIZE BAND,</head>
        <p>BARLESTOWN PRIZE BAND,</p>
        <p>In connection with the above and in aid of the funds, a social evening was held in the Mission Hall, Earlestown, on Saturday. Th.ra was a very good attendance, and dancing was indulged in until a late hour. During intervals, Mr. J. Iluddleston gave a couple of humorous songs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-11-18_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>COLLISION AT SEA.</head>
        <p>COLLISION AT SEA.</p>
        <p>THE FAUOU3 L. AND Y.</p>
        <p>TAWNY FLAKE TOBACCO.</p>
        <p>THE SWZETEST AND COOLEST SMOKE</p>
        <p>ON BARTEL</p>
        <p>IN loz. AND 20z. FOILS,</p>
        <p>4cl PER OZ.</p>
