        <p>WILSON LINER SUNK. An Exchange Hull telegram reports that news was received there pester Jay morning that the Wilson liner Corso has sunk five miles off Cuxhaven in collision with a supposed German Atlantic liner. The crew were saved. The Corso left Hamburg for Hull on Tuesday at midnight.</p>
        <p>- BIG FIRE IN LONDON. A big fire broke out early yesterday morning at the premises of Messrs. 3/latthew arid Webb, at BermondseY. London. The premises consist of two large buildings, each of six storeys, c.sviring an area of over 1,700 feet. The two upper storeys were burnt out, and the rest of the buildings were much damaged. One hundred and sixty firemen were engaged under Commander Wells. AMERICA MOBILISING. WASIIINGTON, THURSDAY. Orders for tho mobilising of tbirteon thousand regu- lar troops at eastern po;ts' in readiness to invade Cuba, in anticipation of the withdrawal of the Spanish Com- missioners on Saturday, -csre issued to-day. The fleet is ready to sail to Spain.</p>
        <p>FOUR AT A BIRTH. The wife of a warier in Woolwich Arsenal, who lives at nunapstead, has just gi17?11 birth to four children. One died on Wednesday morning, bat the others, with the manor, are making favourable progress. A WIFE SOLD FOR Z. 250. In the Chancery Court yesterday, during the hearing before Mr. Ju3tiee Kekewich of an adjourned summons which!possessed no features of public interest, counsel stated that one of the parties in the rase, prior to leav- ing this country, sold his wife f 250. THE ALLEGED DISCONTENT ON THE „ .</p>
